const CVP_PULMONARY_TRANSCRIPTS={"sources":[{"id":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","displayName":"A14. Aortic Arch Transcription by Wasan Binsadiq.pdf","name":"A14. Aortic Arch Transcription by Wasan Binsadiq.pdf","kind":"Supplied lecture transcript","unit":"page","lectureIds":["CVP-A14"],"pages":[{"page":1,"text":"Aortic ArchesPaul GangulyProfessor of AnatomyAlfaisal UniversityTranscribed by Wasan BinsadiqPlease refer to original ppt as I changed the order of the slides based on how the doctor explained them.“How the blood vessels are formed, the arterial system, mostly the arterial system, they all come from the aortic arch, now you may think about the aortic arch but I’m thinking there are difficulties that the students experience, it’s international problem. When we say aortic arch its not arch of aorta”1"},{"page":2,"text":"Please note that “aortic arches” are different from “arch of the aorta”“So, I put it, I make sure people should understand that what I’m going to explain to you is not arch of aorta, arch of aorta is a part of aortic arch. So let's do it in time.”Doctor reads the slide and moves on to the next slide.\n2"},{"page":3,"text":"“So, I have a nice photograph for you, I’ll keep it over here for some time and try to understand what I'm drawing over here. Now, all of you have understood this part, this is the heart (doctor is pointing at the little U-shaped thing under the circle thing labeled “aortic sac”) this is the truncus arteriosus, you remember that which is forming the aorta and the pulmonary trunk, right over here (right side of U-shaped structure, the heart) so this is the atrial part going posteriorly then you have the ventricle over here, then the truncus arteriosus (doctor is pointing at right side of the heart, going down to the middle of the U, then going back up to the left side of the heart). You remember the last part of the endocardial tube. Then, the blood goes to a place called aortic sac and from there aortic arch start. Not arch of the aorta. We know the arch of aorta is only one, this side that side, we’ll talk about that a little later, but arch of the aorta is coming from aortic arch. There are 6 of them. You count it (doctor is pointing at the arrows at the top). There are 6 aortic arches on either side. This is lateral view so I can only show you one side of it but trust me on the other side there are 6 aortic arches also there. Now, my diagram is such that it's showing you some kind of arch and pouch over there, (doctor is pointing at the black things around the arrows at the top) these are called pharyngeal arches, which is responsible for the formation of the head and neck. This area is formed by 3"},{"page":4,"text":"pharyngeal arch (doctor is pointing at the same area around the black things) and they are depicted like this way. When its elevated we call arch, when it’s going down it’s a pouch. Arch and pouch. There are 6 of them. So, 6 of them are structures, they need blood supply. That’s why each one is fed by a blood supply/arterial supply. You can see. Arch number 1 is supplied by this one, arch number 2, this one (doctor is pointing at same area, follow where the arrows are pointing at the aortic arch in the image). Aortic arch is coming from where? Aortic sac. Aortic sac is the end portion of the truncus arteriosus.”\n3"},{"page":5,"text":"Now get a concept of aortic arch that forms through the migration of neural crest. The arches join dorsal aorta\n“If you like the book, there's this one. Basically, the same thing. And again, they are all functional because of the migration of the neural crest cells, they become functional. Let's keep it in mind that all these aortic arch is forming the arch of the aorta and other arteries because of the migration of the neural crest cells. This is very important.”\n4"},{"page":6,"text":"Pharyngeal arches (just get the concept)-six of them (four visible)-responsible for the formation of head and neck of the embryo-each supplied by a separate nerve and artery\n“Now let's go back to the beginning, what I'm trying to say, pharyngeal arches, just get the concept, 6 of them, 4 visible and the 2 disappear very quickly (doctor reads the sentence at the top of the slide). We are interested only at the artery today, not the nerve. Nerve will be done when you are in the year 2. but for the time being, please understand the arterial supply of these 6 arches, they are showing 4, 5 and 6 will disappear but think about 6 arches. In that sense, I think I like my diagram.” \n5"},{"page":7,"text":"“Because that exactly shows you how many arches, we have to start with. Remember the arch number 1, pharyngeal arch number 1 is this one (doctor points at the firstarrow + black thing on the left side). Remember how much force the heart has to apply in order for the blood to come to this place, it's almost impossible. For example, somebody gave me a chocolate over here, if I have to give this chocolate to the last bench (to the back of the class) it will be difficult for me. So, there are 2 ways to do that, one, I throw that, it may not work, or I give it to her, and she will just give it to her and give it to her, and it reaches at the end. Same scenario over here. By the time the 6 arches are formed, I'm talking about the aortic arches are formed, 1 or 2 disappear . So how are they supplied? They're supplied by the number 3, number 3 is the carotid artery which supplied number 1 and 2. Number 4 becomes arch of the aorta, from the aortic arch, arch of aorta is number 4. number 5 disappears. Remember the fact only that this area (doctor is pointing at his neck) is formed because of the arch and the pouch we call the pharyngeal arch and the pouch. Each arch needs a nerve supply, muscle, artery those kinds of things are there. But we are interested only in the artery today. Which artery is going to the number 1 and 2, which artery is going to the number 3, which artery is going to the number 4, and so 6"},{"page":8,"text":"on. What I'm trying to say over here, the little heart cannot pump the blood very properly to the last part, by the time number 3, number 4 is formed, it starts from forming over here (doctor points at first pharyngeal arch, leftmost arrow), 1, then it's forming these two, number 3, number 4, etc. chronological order . But by the time the 6 is formed, number 1 or 2 disappear, because you know, heart cannot keep it up. So,number 3, which is the carotid artery, supplies number 2 and number 1. Number 4 is arch of the aorta, number 5 disappears. Pulmonary trunk divides into left and right pulmonary artery which is coming from aortic arch.”\n6"},{"page":9,"text":"“Here is the sac (doctor is pointing at the bottom most part of the image, at the part beside the letter A), from the aortic sac, this is the left side, you have 6 of them and on the right side you have 6. These are aortic arches. Number 4 which is arch of aorta on the right it will disappear. I’m coming to that later but for the time being, appreciate the fact that there are 6 aortic arches. Why 6? Because they are supplying to the pharyngeal arches, they are 6 in number, that’s why. And many of them disappear and many of them survive. The one which is disappearing is number 1 and 2, number 3 survives, and number 4 survives, number 5 disappears, number 6 survives to form pulmonary artery. Let me do a little bit more on this.”7"},{"page":10,"text":"“Now look at this carefully, this is an important slide, and I request all of you to spend at least 15 minutes on this slide. (Doctor repeats each number again), number 4 is your arch of aorta on the left side. Right side also the arch of the aorta started but the distal part disappeared so the subclavian artery is forming. Subclavian artery on the right side is forming from the aortic arch number 4. Number 6, if you see the left side is forming two things. One is pulmonary artery on the left side, and ductus arteriosus. So, if I ask in the exam, ductus arteriosus coming from where? Ductus arteriosus is coming from the left 6thaortic arch, the distal portion. Proximal portion forms the pulmonary artery.So, this particular photograph you should practice a little. So, if I ask you right subclavian artery is coming from where? Right subclavian artery is coming from right aortic arch number 4. Left I have not talked about yet just hang on. Sometimes distal portion of the 4thright aortic arch doesn’t go away, we can ask in the exam, so what will happen? There will be double arch of aorta. (Doctor is pointing at ductus arteriosus in the image), so if there is a structure over here, can you tell me what it would be? Recurrent laryngeal nerve. The left recurrent laryngeal nerve is hooking around the ductus arteriosus;it will be ligamentum arteriosum later but anyway I'm talking about embryology, look at on the right side what is happening? Right side we also have recurrent laryngeal nerve, but it cannot be hooked because there is no ductus arteriosus. On the right side it is below the 8"},{"page":11,"text":"subclavian artery.”Doctor comes back to this slide later on to review.“Proximal part of the number 6 aortic arch on the left and right will form the left and right pulmonary artery. This picture tells you 1000 words, but you have to really work it by yourself, concentrate on 2 parts, number 4 and number 6. Number 6 comes out from the aortic sac; this proximal part it forms the left pulmonary artery and the rest of that is forming the ductus arteriosus. On the right side, it forms the right pulmonary artery but there is no ductus arteriosus. Number 4, very important, is what we call as arch of the aorta, and on the right side we call subclavian artery because the distal part is absent. If it is present, then we have the double arch of the aorta. \n8"},{"page":12,"text":"Observe the position of the right and left recurrent laryngeal nerves. The left is below the ductus arteriosus. The right goes up and stays below right subclavian artery because of the disappearance of right 6tharch.\n“Look at this carefully, this is the recurrent laryngeal nerve on the left side, it cannot get out, if there is no ductus arteriosus, it will be over here, arch of aorta. On the right side, this is arch of the aorta almost, but this is disappearing (distal part) we call subclavian artery on the right side. But look at this recurrent laryngeal nerve, there is no obstruction over here. It slowly goes up and goes just below the subclavian artery on the right side. Not on the left side. We ask in the exam, if you are looking for the recurrent laryngeal nerve on the right side, which structure should you look for? Subclavian artery on the right. It is below the subclavian artery. And on the left side for recurrent laryngeal nerve, you will be looking at ductus arteriosus or ligamentum arteriosum over here.”9"},{"page":13,"text":"•Although six pairs of pharyngeal arches usually develop, they are not present at the same time•By the time the sixth pair of pharyngeal artery is developed, the first two pairs disappeared•The fifth arch either never forms or forms incompletely and then regress•The arrangement of the arteries will explain why is the left recurrent laryngeal nerve lower in the left side•Also look at carefully for the 7thintersegmental artery for the formation of left subclavian arteryDoctor reads this slide.\n10"},{"page":14,"text":"“So, your question is finally, where is left subclavian artery? This is the left subclavian artery, and this is the intersegmental artery (intersegmental artery is labeled as ‘vertebral artery’ in the image) I have another picture to show you that later . This goes up little bit (the intersegmental artery) to form, you know when the baby grows, then it goes over there (doctor points at aortic arch), to form the left subclavian artery. Right subclavian artery you already understood. Intersegmental artery on the left side forming the left subclavian artery which ill describe to you more a little later also.”\n11"},{"page":15,"text":"“(Doctor reads first point) you will remember carotid artery, that’s all, 3rdone, carotid artery that’s it. (Doctor reads 2nd point) forget it.”Doctor reads the rest.\n12"},{"page":16,"text":"13"},{"page":17,"text":"Adult life-Organization of arteries from aortic arch\n14"},{"page":18,"text":"“(Doctor reads 1 and 2) maxillary artery, hyoid, this is derivative of the aortic arch, not important for you, just disappearing that’s all. (Doctor reads 3) Carotid artery, good enough.”Doctor reads the rest.\n15"},{"page":19,"text":"•The left subclavian artery is derived entirely from the left seventh intersegmental artery, whereas the portions of the right are derived from the right fourth arch and the right dorsal aorta.“(Doctor reads first part of the sentence, until ‘intersegmental artery’) just memorize it.”Doctor reads the rest.\n16"},{"page":20,"text":"Abnormal subclavian artery\n“Now here is the subclavian artery on the left side, so this goes up, this is the intersegmental artery (the thing pointing out on the left side, under ductus arteriosus in the image), 7thintersegmental artery is forming the subclavian artery on the left side. Forget about abnormal subclavian.”\n17"},{"page":21,"text":"Double aorta\n“What is happening here? When I have to draw arch of aorta, you have to draw it in 2D. It is basically like this way (doctor folds a piece of paper in a tube and bends it like the image), posterior to anterior, it's not like this way (lateral view). You have done the mediastinum, if I ask you that the descending thoracic aorta is present where? Posterior mediastinum. So, arch of the aorta is anterior to posterior. So, if there are 2, that will constrict esophagus and trachea, you have to understand why esophagus and trachea will be constricted, and you need immediate surgery when you find there is a double arch of the aorta. So, what about the structure at the back will be constricted, esophagus is a posterior mediastinal structure, trachea is anteriorly placed but that is also obstructed if there is double arch of the aorta. This is important because it's more common. Double aorta means double arch of the aorta.”18"},{"page":22,"text":"“Double aortic arch is possible because of the persistence of the distal part of the 4thaortic arch. Normally it disappears.”\n19"},{"page":23,"text":"Coarctation of aorta\n“Coarctation of aorta, you have done it already? Yeah, so I'm not talking about that.”\n20"},{"page":24,"text":"“Doctor Olena or somebody has taught you already about patent ductus arteriosus. So I'm not going into that.”\n21"},{"page":25,"text":"Cardiac parametersWeek Heart Volume Stroke Volume(ml) (ml) LV10 0.6 0.0218 4.3 0.3230 26.6 2.07(cardiac output at 30 weeks-284.71 ml/min left ventricle) (Cardiac output-right ventricle?)“You are quite familiar with stroke volume and etc. you know the stroke volume is approximately 70mL, but you look at it in embryonic stage, how tiny we are thinking about, how little we are thinking about the blood. Only 2mL, and that’s also in the 30 weeks, in the 10 weeks only 0.02. So, we are dealing with the tiny blood, tiny heart. So, this is just to show you, I'm not going to ask you any questions on that, because I'm not embarking on the physiological part. But try to understand how little thing we are talking about at the outset of the embryonic period. And then in the adult, obviously this cardiac output or the stroke volume they all change. Stroke volume is around 70cc, and the cardiac output is almost 5L per minute. So that’s in the adult but in the embryonic period, very small. So, this information is important for you.”22"},{"page":26,"text":"Development of aortic arch and large vessels•https://youtu.be/-fsSufrjTBU•This is an excellent video and you can understand the development of vessels in detail step-by-step.\n23"},{"page":27,"text":"24\nSolve the Puzzle!!!Which of the following congenital cardiovascular malformations is most commonly associated with maternal rubella infection?A. DextrocardiaB. Patent ductus arteriosusC. Persistent truncus arteriosusD. Coarctaion of aortaE. Double aortic arch"}],"url":"/cvp-pulmonary-reader.html?source=cvp-pulm-t-a14"},{"id":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","displayName":"A15. Intercostal Space, Diaphragm - Dr. Olena - by Merehan Soliman.pdf","name":"A15. Intercostal Space, Diaphragm - Dr. Olena - by Merehan Soliman.pdf","kind":"Supplied lecture transcript","unit":"page","lectureIds":["CVP-A15"],"pages":[{"page":1,"text":"Transcribed by: Merehan Soliman\nReviewed by: Adam ElMorsey\nQuestions & answers are at the end.\n1\nCVP 112\nCVP 112\nIntercostal space, \nDiaphragm\nOlena Bolgova, MD, PhD\nProfessor of Gross Anatomy\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":2,"text":"Intercostal space — why do we call the space between two ribs intercostal? Because the Latin name for the ribs is \"costa\".\nIntercostal: between the two ribs.\nDiaphragm — the main muscle of respiration, and the phrenic nerve. Why do we call it the phrenic nerve? Because the Latin \nname for the diaphragm is \"phrenic\" muscle.\nSo, in medicine, everything comes from the Latin language.\n2\nOBJECTIVES:\nOBJECTIVES:\nBy the end of this lecture students should be able to describe:\n◼ Intercostal space, intercostal muscles\n◼ Diaphragm, Phrenic nerve\n◼ Clinical cases:\n◼ Intercostal nerve block\n◼ Flail chest  “stove-in chest”\n◼ Paralysis of half of the diaphragm\n◼ Diaphragmatic rupture\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":3,"text":"This is a section of the thoracic wall.\nMost external layer: skin → subcutaneous tissue, where we have the fat cells and adipose tissue → superficial fascia (under \nthe subcutaneous tissue) → deep fascia (which covers the muscles) → intercostal muscles; three layers of intercostal muscles:\n1. External intercostal muscle\n2. Internal intercostal muscle\n3. Innermost intercostal muscle\nWhat do we have in between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles? See this (referring to vein, artery, and nerve \nunderneath groove of upper rib)? The blood vessels and nerves are not traveling separately in our body; they always travel \ntogether. They are neurovascular bundles or fascicles.\nThis is the intercostal neurovascular bundle. We have:\n1. Intercostal vein sitting on the top\n2. Intercostal artery in the middle\n3. Intercostal nerve is the lowest one \n3\nIntercostal spaces\nIntercostal spaces\nIntercostal blood vessels and \nnerves:\n◼ run between the internal \nintercostal and \ninnermost intercostal \nmuscles in the costal \ngroove\n◼ arranged from superior to \ninferior as vein, artery, \nnerve\n◼ Most vulnerable \nstructures – intercostal \nnerve and posterior \nintercostal artery \nbecause ribs do not cover \nthem.\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":4,"text":"Do they really have these colors in our body? Definitely not, you'll see in the dissections that nerves are white, veins are a little bit blue-\nish, and arteries are a little bit reddish. In preserved specimen, everything does look alike. But location is still the same: on the top = vein, \nmiddle = artery, lowest = nerve. Can you see the compression of what they have done on the lower border of the rib? How do we call \nthis impression? In the inferior border of every rib, we have a costal groove. This groove develops there because of the impression of \nthe neurovascular bundle. This groove is supposed to protect it, but it does not completely protect it. The most vulnerable structure is \nthe nerve because it's exposed and not protected by the bone.\nUnderneath the neurovascular bundle → pleura; the blue layer covering the inner surface of the thoracic wall = parietal (parietal means \nwall); blue layer covering the surface of the lungs = visceral (viscera means organ, so visceral is covering the organ itself); in between the \nparietal and visceral pleura is the pleural space, filled up with pleural fluid.\nIf anesthesia is needed, what will the anesthesia do? In the dentistry office, they don't pull your teeth out right away or drill a hole \nimmediately – they inject drugs called local anesthetics. The difference in generations is how long it lasts: first generation is Novocain –\nlasts up to 10 minutes, the second generation is Lidocaine – lasts up to 20-30 minutes, and the third generation is Bupivacaine – lasts up \nto 40 minutes, usually the one most used. Anesthesia is needed before every surgery to stop pain, it means we must stop the pain.\nWe have different ways of anesthesia. The local anesthesia, we do the injection of those local anesthetic drugs. They basically stop the \ntransmission through the axons. If you cut off this nerve (referring to intercostal nerve) or any nerve, histologically, you will see axons. \nSo, we are (targeting) doing the injection close to the nerve in order to stop the transmission, specifically pain transmission. This injection \nis to be done in the lower border of the rib because the nerve is sitting there.\nCollateral branches are small branches from the main structures over here (neurovascular bundle) running on the top of the rib. Since \nthey are small, we can neglect them; but in reality, we are doing two injections.\n3"},{"page":5,"text":"The intercostal nerve block:\nWe are doing two injections, but in theory, one is enough (lower border of the rib targeting the main neurovascular bundle). \nOverlapping means the dermatomes being on top of each other.\nDermatome: strip of the skin that is innervated from the one dorsal root ganglion, and that dorsal root ganglion is a location \nof the sensory neurons in the spinal nerves. We have 31 pairs of spinal nerves – 31 left and 31 right -- and we are supposed to \nhave 31 dermatomes, but it is the reality is different, we have 29 dermatomes. This is because the first and last spinal nerves \ndon't have the dorsal root ganglion, or a sensory component – they are purely motor nerves. So, total of 29 dermatomes in our \nbody. This is being mentioned because of the overlapping.\nIn a dermatomal map, you can see lines over there (referring to intercostal spaces) through the body, but we do not really have \nthese lines in reality; we have overlapping. We have one dermatome covering half of another dermatome, and another \ndermatome, and another. So, technically speaking, if you will do those two injections over here (referring to collateral branch \non image), it does not mean that we will get anesthesia in this intercostal space. This is because we have one dermatome \nsitting on the top, then another dermatome under it, and then below we have another dermatome; they overlap in this one. \nThe patient will still feel pain. So in order to make the anesthesia in this dermatome, we have to anesthetize the one that is \n4\nIntercostal nerve block\nIntercostal nerve block\n◼ Local anesthesia of an \nintercostal space is produced \nby injecting a local anesthetic \nagent around the intercostal \nnerves between the \nparavertebral line and the \narea of required anesthesia.\n◼ Because any particular area \nof skin usually receives \ninnervation from two \nadjacent nerves, \nconsiderable overlapping of \ncontiguous dermatomes \noccurs.\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":6,"text":"above and the one that is below. So, three; the middle one will get complete anesthesia.\n4"},{"page":7,"text":"Intercostal muscles:\n• Three of them (external, internal, and innermost)\n• Neurovascular bundle sits between internal and innermost intercostal muscles\n(Upcoming for GIT block)\nThree layers of the muscles on the lateral abdominal wall – they look like those intercostal muscles, except with no ribs. The \nhuman body loses the ribs in the abdomen. Unlike snakes that have ribs across the whole body, the human body don't have \nribs across, but the three layers of the muscles are alike. What is the difference?:\n• External intercostal muscle: direction of muscle fibers are oblique [going to midsagittal line] (hands-in-pockets direction). \nBecause of this position, when they contract, they will elevate the ribs and help inhalation or inspiration of the air\n• Internal intercostal muscle: direction of muscle fibers are oblique [going away from midsagittal line]; opposite direction of \nexternal. Once they contract, they will help in exhalation.\n• Innermost intercostal muscle: direction of muscle fibers run the same as internal (oblique). These are tricky because we \ndon't have them everywhere. They also participate in expiration.\n5\nIntercostal muscles\nIntercostal muscles\n◼ External intercostal muscle\n◼ extends from the rib tubercle \nposteriorly to the costochondral \njunction anteriorly where the \nmuscle fibers are replaced by the \nexternal intercostal membrane\n◼ Internal intercostal muscle\n◼ extends from the sternum \nanteriorly to the rib angle \nposteriorly where the internal \nintercostal membrane replaces the \nmuscle fibers\n◼ Innermost intercostal muscle\n◼ similar to the internal intercostal \nmuscle (deep portion) - separated \nby intercostal VAN\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":8,"text":"Fractures of the ribs are typically common; one or two ribs get fractured. In these patients, they have pain during breathing \nand respiration, especially when they inspire. Normally, we put the bandage over here (referring to right lateral side of thorax\nas example) and they breathe very quietly and rest a lot. In approximately 2-3 weeks' time, if there is no displacement, those \nfractured ribs get better and heal up very well.\nThis is not the case with flail chest.\nFlail chest:\nVery rare situation that always happens because of automobile collision, or because of a really huge, round-shaped force which \nhits in the lateral thorax. This will fracture multiple ribs, so this fragment in the middle (referring to upper image pointing \nat \"flail area\") gets disconnected from the rest of the thoracic cage. The skin, both fascia, etc will be there, but the ribs will be \nbroken. T echnically speaking, this means it can move, and it will move with the breathing.\n• Once we inhale, in order for air to get into the thoracic cavity, the pressure inside the thoracic cavity decreases. The \ndiaphragm (main muscle of respiration) gets flatter. If we increase the volume of the thoracic cavity, the pressure will drop \nand the air will get in. This fragment (flail area) will also get inward because the pressure will drop when the patient \ninhales.\n6\nFlail chest (“stove-in chest”)\nFlail chest (“stove-in chest”)\n◼ It occurs when a large \nanterior or lateral chest wall \nsegment moves freely due to \nmultiple rib fractures \n(paradoxical respiration).\n◼ Loose segment of the wall \nmoves inward on inspiration \nand outward on expiration.\n◼ Flail chest is an extremely \npainful injury that impairs \nventilation, affecting blood \noxygenation and causing \nrespiratory failure.\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":9,"text":"• Once we exhale, in order for air to leave the thoracic cavity, the pressure inside the thoracic cavity increases. This fragment will go \noutward because the pressure will rise when the patient exhales. \n6"},{"page":10,"text":"Diaphragm:\n• Once we have breathed in (inhalation) and are sitting quietly, the diaphragm is being used for respiration. It is the main and \nonly muscle on why we are still alive, and also called phrenic muscle. It is dome shaped; once it contracts → becomes \nflatter.This flattening of the diaphragm decreases the pressure in the thoracic cavity and increases the pressure in the \nabdominal cavity – these two cavities are separated by the diaphragm.\n• Once we have breathed out (exhalation) and are sitting quietly, the diaphragm is being relaxed; because of the elastic \nproperties of the lungs, the air will go out.\nParts of the diaphragm:\n• Sternal part – starts from the sternum\n• Costal part – the biggest part which comes from the ribs\n• Vertebral part – from the vertebral bodies\n• Central tendon – in the center of the diaphragm\n• On top – the pericardium, which is a fibrous 'bag' and inside it we have the heart\n7\nDIAPHRAGM\nDIAPHRAGM\n◼ Parts:\n◼\n◼\n◼ sternal part – from xiphoid process \ncostal part – from lower 6 ribs and \ntheir costal cartilages\nvertebral part – from the bodies \nand IV discs of lumbar vertebrae by \nmeans of vertical columns or crura\n◼\n◼\nand from the arcuate ligaments \nCentral Tendon – where diaphragm \ninserts\n◼ clover-leaf shaped to which the \nmuscle fibers of the diaphragm insert\nsuperior surface partially fused to \ninferior surface of the fibrous \npericardium\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":11,"text":"Video:\nWhen the diaphragm contracts, the whole sheet of muscle together, with the central tendon, moves downward, expanding the \nlungs and causing us to breathe in.\n8\nMovements of the diaphragm\nMovements of the diaphragm\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":12,"text":"If we cut the human body vertically in the middle, this is a sagittal section, specifically a midsagittal section; we'll have two \nhalves, left and right. The cavities that can be seen in the midline are two in the body:\n• The ventral cavity – every other cavity; the thorax, abdomen, and pelvis are collectively under the ventral cavity of the body.\n• The dorsal cavity – intracranial cavity (where we have the brain) & vertebral canal (where we have the spinal cord)\nNow, we have two subsections because of the diaphragm:\n• Thoracic cavity – upper one\n• Abdominopelvic cavity – lower one (abdominal cavity & pelvic cavity), because there is no septum in between the \nabdominal and pelvic cavity\nSince the diaphragm is making partitions, we need to communicate those two cavities; that's why we have openings in the \ndiaphragm, for the major blood vessels.\nWe have three of these openings:\n11. Caval, foramen for the inferior vena cava(IVC) and right phrenic nerve.\nLevel of the eighth thoracic vertebra.\nThis foramen comes through the central tendon of the diaphragm (important); this is because veins have very delicate walls \n9\nOpenings of the diaphragm\nOpenings of the diaphragm\n◼\n◼\n◼ Caval (T8): transmits \nthe IVC and the \nterminal branches of \nthe right phrenic \nnerve\nEsophageal (T10): \ntransmits the \nesophagus, right and \nleft vagus nerves, \nesophageal branches of \nthe left gastric \nvessels\nAortic (T12) transmits \nthe descending aorta, \nthoracic duct, \nazygos vein\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":13,"text":"and can easily collapse. In order to prevent this collapse, it goes through the diaphragm.We do have the superior vena cava here (the \nblood vessel, not the foramen). We have two vena cava:\n• Superior vena cava\n• Inferior vena cava (the one that goes through the diaphragm).\nThe right phrenic nerve also runs through this foramen; it will supply the diaphragm with two components:\n• Motor component – exclusively supplies the diaphragm\n• Sensory component – supplies the left and right thoracic viscera; the right one will go through the foramen and end up at the liver\n(which will get sensory innervations from the right phrenic nerve, & the other is in the GIT block). We have the right phrenic nerve \nbecause of the sensory innervation of the liver.\n12. Esophageal, foramen for the esophagus, right and left vagus nerves, and left gastric vessels.\nSits two vertebrae below the caval, at the level of the tenth thoracic vertebra.\n• The esophagus travels with the vagus nerves (plural, two of them).\n• Left & right vagus nerves – cranial nerves number 10 (C10), which are mostly parasympathetic at this level – they do the esophageal \nplexus (GIT).\n• The left gastric vessels are also passing through (also GIT).\n13. Aortic, foramen for the descending aorta, thoracic duct, and azygos vein.\nSits two vertebrae below the esophageal, at the level of the twelfth thoracic vertebra.\n• So, we have ascending aorta, aortic arch, and descending aorta. When the descending aorta goes through this foramen, the upper \nportion = thoracic aorta and the lower portion = abdominal aorta.\n• Along with the aorta, we have the azygos vein\n• We have the thoracic duct – major lymphatic vessel in the body.\n9"},{"page":14,"text":"Innervation of the diaphragm; Phrenic nerve– C3-C5.\n\"C\" does not mean the cervical vertebra; the nerves are not traveling from the vertebra. Those T (thoracic), C (cervical), L \n(lumbar) could be related to two structures: the vertebral level, like in the diaphragm, or the segments of the spinal cord.\nIn this case, we are talking about spinal nerves, so we are talking about the spinal cord levels. The third, fourth, and fifth \ncervical segments of the spinal cord. It's not the brain, but it's the spinal cord. So, phrenic nerve starts from the three levels of \nthe spinal cord, these spinal nerves (C3-C5).\nWhat happens if the spinal cord gets dissected, or damaged, or broken, or injured above this C3 level? This happens in the \nhangman fracture (MSK block); when somebody is hanging, they die because the spinal cord will be broken at the level of C2, \nthe fracture of the second cervical vertebra. The spinal cord broken at this level will disconnect the motor nucleus of the \nphrenic nerve from the brain, so there will be no activation of the diaphragm, complete paralysis, and no rhythm, and the \npatient dies because of the asphyxia (related to the lack of oxygen in the brain; without oxygen, we will not survive).\nDiaphragm– the major muscle of inspiration.\nOnce the diaphragm has contracted, the shape becomes flat. The volume of the thoracic cavity will increase, and the pressure \ninside the thoracic cavity will decrease. The abdominal cavity is the opposite: the volume of the abdominal cavity will \n10\nNerve supply  function of the diaphragm\nNerve supply & function of the \ndiaphragm\n◼ Motor: phrenic nerve \n(C3-C5) ONLY\n◼ Sensory:\n◼ phrenic nerve - central \nportion\n◼ lower 6 intercostal nerves - \nperipheral portion\n◼ Diaphragm is the muscle \nof inspiration: contraction \npulls down the central \ntendon → increased vertical \ndiameter of the thorax → \ndecreased intrathoracic \npressure → expansion of \nthe lungs\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":15,"text":"decrease, and the intra-abdominal pressure will increase. Simple physiology brings us to the model.\n10"},{"page":16,"text":"This is a lateral view; you can see that (referring to bronchus) is the root of the lung. The root of the lung is something that \nholds the lungs during the dissection; once we dissect, we expose the lungs, and the lungs don't fall because of the root of the \nlung. Three structures will be there, and there are two of these roots on each side:\n• Main bronchus\n• Pulmonary artery\n• Pulmonary veins\nWe have two nerves running side by side:\n• The phrenic nerve – sits in front of the root of the lung (anteriorly). Motor nerve for the diaphragm and sensory nerve \n(pain senses) for the pericardium, pleura, and all structures sitting nearby. The right phrenic nerve will go to the \nabdominal cavity; also helps innervate the liver.\n• The vagus nerve – cranial nerve 10 (C10), sits behind the root of the lung (posteriorly)\nT echnically speaking, once you have the cadaveric dissection, the nerves look alike; zero difference except for the location.\nThe one sitting in front is the phrenic nerve. The one sitting behind is the vagus nerve.\n11\nPhrenic nerve\nPhrenic nerve\n◼ It arises from the anterior \nbranches of C3-C5 nerves \nand lies in front of the \nanterior scalene muscle.\n◼ Phrenic nerve runs anterior \nto the root of the lung, \nwhereas the vagus nerve runs \nposterior to the root of the \nlung.\n◼ It innervates the fibrous \npericardium, the \nmediastinal and \ndiaphragmatic pleurae \n(sensory innervation), and \nthe diaphragm for motor \nand its central tendon for \nsensory.\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":17,"text":"Paralysis: the muscle, any muscle, can get paralyzed; it means the muscle will lose innervation. Without nerves, the muscle \ncan't contract.\nIf the diaphragm will lose innervation, it will be paralyzed. If both phrenic nerves are damaged, then the person will die \n(hangman fracture); what if only one will be damaged?:\nE.g., if the right phrenic nerve was cut off, the right side of the diaphragm will get paralyzed, and the left one will remain \nokay.\nOnce this person will inspire (the air will go in and the diaphragm contracts), the left side will contract and becomes flatter; the \nright side, which is paralyzed, is supposed to stay the same, but the pressure does not allow it – once the left side is \ncontracted, the intra-abdominal pressure will rise (thoracic pressure falls), so the abdominal viscera will push the right \nparalyzed side up.\nThat's why it is called paradoxical movement of the diaphragm. The normal side will move the way it is supposed to, but the \nparalyzed side will move in the opposite way.\nIt is visible on the X-ray – we have the fluoroscopy, the machine where we can see under the X-ray in real time.\n12\nParalysis of half of the diaphragm\nParalysis of half of the \ndiaphragm\n◼ It may result from \ninjury or operative \ndivision of the \nphrenic nerve of \nsame side\n◼ It can be detected \nradiologically.\n◼ Paradoxical \nmovement: dome of \ndiaphragm of injured \nside pushed superiorly \nby abdominal viscera \nduring inspiration \ninstead of descending\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":18,"text":"The traumatic rupture of the diaphragm (rare).\nCould happen because of blunt trauma, typically as a result of automobile collision. 80-90% of the time it is on the left side\nbecause of the liver. On the right side, because the liver is there, the liver's pressure is probably equal to the thoracic \npressure, so it is protecting the right side.\nIn the case of the rupture of the diaphragm, we have a hole and the abdominal viscera. Once the patient will expire, the \npressure will increase in the abdominal cavity, and the left side of the abdominal viscera (ruptured side of diaphragm) will go \nup, and we can see this on the X-ray.\nNowadays, we have good machines where we can see the contours, not the details, of internal organs on an X-ray.\nThis rupture cannot heal itself because rupture of the muscles never heal themselves. Any herniation will not heal itself, so \nwe have to do surgery and reinforce this broken part. Then we have the special inner material which we can embed in the \nfibrous tissue that will travel through and cover it up by making a really strong connection over the ruptured area.\n13\nDiaphragmatic ruptures\nDiaphragmatic ruptures\n◼ Diaphragmatic injuries are relatively rare \nand result from either blunt trauma or \npenetrating trauma.\n◼ Presently, 80-90% of blunt \ndiaphragmatic ruptures result from \nmotor vehicle crashes.\n◼ The majority (80-90%) of blunt \ndiaphragmatic ruptures have occurred on \nthe left side.\n◼ Blunt trauma typically produces large \nradial tears measuring 5-15 cm, most \noften at the posterolateral aspect of \nthe diaphragm.\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":19,"text":"Muscles of respiration.\nMuscles of inspiration:\n1. Most of it will be done by the diaphragm\n2. External intercostal muscles also help\nMuscles of expiration:\n1. Internal intercostal and innermost intercostal muscles.\n2. Serratus posterior and inferior muscles, which are muscles of the back\nKeep in mind the quiet breathing, like how you are sitting now, the elastic property of the lungs will do the job once the \ndiaphragm is relaxed (passive).\n14\nMain muscles of respiration\nMain muscles of respiration\n◼ Muscles of inspiration:\n◼ Diaphragm\n◼ External intercostal\n◼ Serratus posterior superior \nmuscle\n◼ Muscles of expiration:\n◼ Internal & innermost \nintercostal\n◼ Serratus posterior inferior \nmuscle\n◼ In quiet breathing, expiration \nresults from passive elastic \nrecoil of the lungs and rib cage\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":20,"text":"Now about the movements of the thoracic wall.\nDiaphragm is a diaphragm – it moves in between the thoracic and abdominal cavities.\nIntercostal muscles are sitting between the ribs; once they are contracted, the shape of the thoracic cavity gets changed.\nInhale and you can see what happens to your chest:\n• The pump-handle movement – the sternum will go up. Like in ancient cities, in streets when you need water, you have to \ndo those movements (pumping up and down).\n• The bucket-handle movement – lateral aspect (ribs) also go up once the intercostal muscles will be contracted.\n15\nMovements of the thoracic wall\nMovements of \nthe thoracic wall\n◼ The vertical dimension (height) of the \ncentral part of the thoracic cavity increases \nduring inspiration as a contraction of the \ndiaphragm causes it to descend, \ncompressing the abdominal viscera.\n◼ The AP dimension of the thorax \nincreases considerably when the \nintercostal muscles contract: movement \nof the ribs at the costovertebral joints \naround an axis passing through the necks \nof the ribs causes the anterior ends of the \nribs to rise - the pump-handle \nmovement\n◼ The transverse dimension of the thorax \nalso increases slightly when the \nintercostal muscles contract, raising the \nmiddle of the ribs - the bucket-handle \nmovement\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":21,"text":"Accessory muscles of respiration are not used on a regular basis – only in extreme situations (i.e., patient has extreme \ndifficulty breathing), those extra muscles can be utilized in order to get some air. Those extra muscles being:\n• Sternocleidomastoid– the muscle of the neck. They are not normally used for breathing, but if someone is compressing \nthe chest, then there is no other option; the body will use whatever it could, including the muscles of the neck.\n• Scalenus– also muscles of the neck.\n• Pectoralis major– right on the anterior chest.\n• Serratus anterior– on the lateral wall of the thorax. They could be utilized in order to get some air.\nMuscles for forceful expiration also could be utilized. In some pathologies (emphysema; POD), in order to exhale in this \nextreme situation, you will use the muscles of the forceful expiration. Under normal circumstances, we don't use them for you. \nThose muscles being (the Dr. did not go over them, only putting for sake of completion):\n• Rectus abdominis\n• External abdominal oblique\n• Internal abdominal oblique\n• Transversus abdominis\n16\nAccessory muscles of respiration\nAccessory muscles of \nrespiration\n◼\n◼\n◼\n◼\n◼\n◼\n◼\n◼\n◼\n◼ Muscles of forced inspiration\n- elevate sternum and/or fix \nupper ribs\nSrternocleidomastoid \nScalenes (anterior, middle, \nand posterior)\nPectoralis major \nSerratus anterior\nMuscles of forced expiration\n- depress the lower ribs\nRectus abdominis\nExternal abdominal oblique \nInternal abdominal oblique \nTransversus abdominis\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":22,"text":"All questions & answers in Dr.'s slides:\n➢ The intercostal neurovascular bundle is particularly vulnerable to injury from fractured ribs because it is found in which of\nthe following locations? \nAnswer: B. Beneath the inferior border of the ribs.\nSo, it's a lower border – they are talking about the costal groove. Why not the upper one, why is it not A? Because it says \nneurovascular bundle. We do have the collateral branches, but they are not considered as a bundle, they are just tiny \nbranches.\n➢ Following a car accident, a patient developed  \"Flail Chest\". The condition allows the loose segment of the chest wall to \nmove:\nAnswer: A. Inward on inspiration and outward on expiration.\n➢ A 43-year-old female patient has been lying down on the hospital bed for more than 4 months. Her normal, quiet expiration \nis achieved by contraction of which of the following structures?\nAnswer: A. Elastic tissue in the lungs and thoracic wall.\n17\nPlease take the practice quiz:\nPlease take the practice quiz:\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":23,"text":"This is expiration, which means exhale. It's breathing; in a quiet situation,in order to inhale, you use the diaphragm; in order to exhale, \nyou just relax the diaphragm and elastic tissue.\nNowadays, we don't commonly put the patient in the hospital bed for four months – it's rare, but sometimes it depends on the situation; \nit may be a severe fracture, it could be some stroke, it could be MI (myocardial infarction) which is a very difficult condition. If the patient \nwill stay in the bed for 4 months (especially elderly people), they could die easily. Not because of the major problem (situation that led \nto them being in the hospital in the first place), but because of pneumonia. Pneumonia happens because in the supine position, once \nthey sleep, it is most likely that in this long period of time, the fluid will go in, and the patient can get this pneumonia.So, nowadays we \nhave functional beds in the hospital; they have specialled devices that we use to change the shape of the bed. Two points are important:\n1. We always elevate it to 45°, even when the patient is sleeping. Once we elevate the patient's head, there are less chances of \npneumonia.\n2. We make the knee flex. If the knee will stay in a straight position for a long time, arthrosis could happen to the cartilages, and the knee \nwill never move again. We have tomove the joints in order for them to survive.\n17"},{"page":24,"text":"18\nReferences\nReferences\n◼ Gray's anatomy for students by Richard L. Drake, Wayne Vogl, \nAdam W. M. Mitchell; Churchill Livingstone; 2 edition; ISBN-13: \n978-0443069529, ISBN-10: 0443069522\n◼ Clinically Oriented Anatomy by Keith L. Moore, Arthur F. Dalley,\nA. M. R. Agur; Lippincott Williams & Wilkins; 7th edition; ISBN-\n13: 978-1451119459; ISBN-10: 1451119453\n◼ Langman's Medical Embryology by Thomas W Sadler; Lippincott \nWilliams & Wilkins; 12th edition; ISBN-13: 978-1451113426; \nISBN-10: 1451113420\n◼ Netter's Clinical Anatomy by John T. Hansen, David R. \nLambert, Frank H. Netter; ICON Learning Systems; \nISBN13: 9781929007714\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"}],"url":"/cvp-pulmonary-reader.html?source=cvp-pulm-t-a15"},{"id":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","displayName":"A16 Lungs Anatomy Transcription - Haadiya Zeeshan.pdf","name":"A16 Lungs Anatomy Transcription - Haadiya Zeeshan.pdf","kind":"Supplied lecture transcript","unit":"page","lectureIds":["CVP-A16"],"pages":[{"page":1,"text":"Transcribed by Haadiya Zeeshan\nReviewed by Hibbah Elahi\n1\nCVP 112\nCVP 112\nLungs Anatomy\nOlena Bolgova, MD, PhD\nProfessor of Gross Anatomy\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":2,"text":"2\nOBJECTIVES:\nOBJECTIVES:\nBy the end of this lecture students should be able to describe:\n◼ Anatomy of right and left bronchi\n◼ Anatomy of lungs\n◼ Clinical cases:\n◼ Aspiration of foreign bodies\n◼ Bronchopulmonary segments\n◼ Bronchogenic carcinoma (Pancoast tumor)\n◼ Pneumonia\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":3,"text":"Anatomically, we can subdivide the respiratory system by respiratory tract and \norgans. Respiratory tracts, which provide airflow, can be subdivided into upper and \nlower respiratory tracts.\nUpper respiratory tract:\n1. Nasal Cavity\n2. Pharynx\n3. Larynx\nLower respiratory tract:\n1. Trachea (after larynx)\n2. Bronchi\n3\nRESPIRATORY SYSTEM\nRESPIRATORY SYSTEM\n◼ The respiratory system \nincludes the lungs and \nrespiratory ways that link \nthe sites of gas exchange with \nthe external environment.\n◼ There is also a ventilation \nmechanism, consisting of the \nthoracic cage, intercostal \nmuscles, diaphragm, and \nelastic and collagen \ncomponents of the lungs, that \nis important in the movement \nof air through the conducting \nand respiratory parts of the \nlungs.\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":4,"text":"The trachea sits under the cricoid cartilage of the larynx. The thyroid cartilage is \nmore prominent in males, but you can palpate it. If you move your finger down and \nfeel some thickness, that’s the cricoid cartilage. Once you move your finger even \nlower, right above jugular notch in the sternum, you can feel your trachea. This \nphoto is an endoscope (using bronchoscope) of a trachea from inside. The \nbronchoscope has a little camera, light, and some tiny tools we can use to extract \nforeign bodies. Those bumps are C-shaped cartilage. In the trachea, the posterior \nsurface has soft tissue for the esophagus, which is needed to swallow food. Once \nthe ball of food will swallow through, we need expansion which is done because of \nthis soft spot on the back. The trachea is 10 to 13 cm long in adults. It is shorter and \n4\nTRACHEA\nTRACHEA\n◼ It is a thin-walled tube about \n10-13 cm long and 2.5 cm in \ndiameter\n◼ It consists of 16-20 C-shaped \nrings of hyaline cartilage \nembedded in its fibroelastic wall \nand which serve to keep the \ntracheal lumen patent\n◼ It extends from the lower border \nof the cricoid cartilage (C6) in \nthe neck to the level of the \nsternal angle (IV disc T4/T5) in \nthe superior mediastinum, where \nit divides into the right and left \nprimary (main) bronchi\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":5,"text":"narrower in newborns but we still have the same number of cartilages eventually. Once the \ntrachea reaches the T4 to T5 intervertebral disc level at the sternal angle (angle of Louis), it \nbifurcates and splits/divides into two main/primary bronchi (bronchi of the first \ngeneration). At T4, right above of the bifurcation of the trachea, we have the aortic arch. \nThe trachea has respiratory epithelium with cilia, which are tiny projections at the top of \neach cell that sweep/propel the mucus upwards. These movements clean the airway \nchannels because dust which enters air will settle down in the mucus and needs to be \nconstantly removed.\nTracheostomy:\nIf a patient has blockage of the upper respiratory tracts and cannot breathe, the only \nemergency way we can save their life is by cutting off at the trachea and putting a hook \nshaped tube to provide air to the lungs.\nTypes of endoscopes:\n1. Gastroduodenoscope: stomach\n2. Colonoscope: rectum and the large intestine\n3. Laparoscope: abdominal cavity\n4. Bronchoscope: respiratory ways\n4"},{"page":6,"text":"You cannot see carina in this picture because it's the separation on the inside (needs \nbronchoscope). It is an elevation/ridge (projection) where bifurcation is happening. \nNormally, carina is supposed to stay strictly in the midline, like here.The deviation of \nthe carina from the midline is a bad clinical sign indicating that something is pulling \nthe carina, most likely a tumour of the lungs (bronchogenic carcinoma). In the \ncarina, we have a location with receptors which will initiate a protective cough \nreflex. It is protective because when we cough, we exhale forcefully. With the \nairflow, we eliminate dust and small foreign bodies from our respiratory way. We \nhave seven generations of bronchi, after which we have bronchioles split by the \nrespiratory duct. C6 means the sixth cervical vertebrae (we have 7).\n5\nCarina\nCarina\n◼ It is a ridge separating the \nopenings of the main bronchi \nat their junction with the \ntrachea\n◼ The carina: may be \nexamined with a \nbronchoscope and may be \ndistorted, widened \nposteriorly, and immobile \nin the presence of \nbronchogenic carcinoma.\n◼ The mucous membrane over \nthe carina is one of the most \nsensitive areas of the \ntracheobronchial tree and is \nassociated with the cough \nreflex\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":7,"text":"Primary/main bronchi (bronchi of first generation):\n- The two bronchi after bifurcation \n- They also have the soft spot on the back like the trachea. \n- They are called main/primary because they supply lungs. \n- Once they enter the lungs, they divide into secondary\nSecondary/lobar bronchi (bronchi of the second generation):\n- 3 on the right and 2 on the left because of the number of lobes in the lungs.\nTertiary/segmental bronchi (bronchi of the third generation):\n- Give air to bronchopulmonary segments\n5"},{"page":8,"text":"The right primary bronchus has the arch of the azygous vein running above the root \nof the right lung to join the superior vena cava.\nStructures running in the hilum (door) of the lung are collectively called the root to \nsupply the lungs:\n1. Primary bronchus on the top to bring air.\n2. Right pulmonary artery escapes from the pulmonary trunk, which splits by the \ntwo pulmonary arteries exits from the right ventricle. \n3. Two right pulmonary veins going to the left atrium.\n6\nRight primary bronchus\nRight primary bronchus\n◼ It is shorter (1 cm), wider, \nand more vertically \ndisposed than the left \nprimary bronchus\n◼ Upper lobar (secondary) \nbronchus (Eparterial \nbronchus) – given off \nbefore entering the hilum \nof the right lung, all other \nbronchi are hyparterial \nbronchi.\n◼ Middle and inferior lobar \nbronchi – given off after \nentering hilum\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":9,"text":"Deoxygenated blood in lung is carried by arteries, while oxygenated is carried by veins \n(reversed). This is because we need lungs for gas exchange. CO2 from blood will go into the \nair and O2 will go from air to the blood (deoxygenated blood becomes oxygenated). The \nonly reason we are alive is because this happens constantly. The right primary bronchus \nsplits by the two bronchi of second generation (lobar bronchi). \nEparterial bronchi: lobar bronchi sitting above the pulmonary artery (right superior \nbronchus)\n6"},{"page":10,"text":"ANSWER: C\nSmokers have 10 times more chance to get tumours in the lungs compared to non-\nsmokers, but it doesn't mean they cannot get this tumour. The bronchogram is a \ntype of plain x-ray, but bronchi cannot be seen in it, so we need a stain/contrast dye\ninhaled by the patient using special inhalators. It will cover the entire bronchial tree \ninside, making it visible.\n7\n"},{"page":11,"text":"At the left primary bronchus we have:\n1. T4: arch of aorta \n2. T4 to T5: bifurcation\n3. T5: pulmonary trunk\nLeft vagus: yellow\nRecurrent branch: runs underneath and behind aortic arch, then comes up\nRight recurrent branch: loops around right subclavian artery\nRima glottidis: between the two vocal folds \n8\nLeft primary bronchus\nLeft primary bronchus\n◼ It is longer (2 cm), \nnarrower, and more \nhorizontal than the right \nprimary bronchus\n◼ It passes to the left inferior \nto the arch of the aorta and \nanterior to the esophagus\n◼ Upper and inferior lobar \nbronchi – given off after \nentering the hilum of the left \nlung\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":12,"text":"Lesion of recurrent laryngeal nerve:\nHoarseness (problem with voice production), because this nerve supplies muscles of the \nlarynx, so they get paralysed. If the left recurrent laryngeal nerve is injured, muscles on one \nside get paralysed. The vocal folds deviate towards the midline and half of the rima glottidis \nwill be permanently closed (one half is working). The right recurrent laryngeal could also be \ninjured during the surgery of patent ductus arteriosis (PDA), but PDA is a pretty rare \ncondition, so most of the cases when the nerve is damaged is during neck surgery of the \nthyroid gland. The left main bronchus is longer, narrower, and more horizontal than the \nright because the heart sits mostly on the left side, so eventually it compresses the left \nlung. It is located a little bit further from the midline. A bronchoscope is a device with a \nlong tube that can go through the oral cavity, larynx, trachea, and bronchi. \n8"},{"page":13,"text":"Toys for specific ages should be big enough to prevent them from swallowing or \ngoing inside of the nose (foreign bodies). Most foreign bodies are coughed out and \ndo not pass through the larynx. If the foreign body goes through the rima glottidis \n(which is very narrow) into the trachea, there are more chances that it will lodge in \nthe right primary bronchus because it is wider and more vertical. If this foreign body \nis small enough to get through the right primary bronchus and reaches the bronchi \nof the third generation, it will most likely lodge into the inferior one. Sometimes \nmiddle, but NEVER superior. If the foreign body is even smaller, it will lodge into the \nposterior basal segmental bronchus.\n9\nAspiration of foreign bodies\nAspiration of foreign bodies\n◼ Inhalation of FBs (pins, parts \nof teeth, screws, nuts, bolts, \ntoys) into the lower \nrespiratory tract is common, \nespecially in children\n◼ More likely to enter the \nright primary bronchus \nand pass into the middle or \nlower lobe bronchi\n◼ If the vertical position of the \nbody, the foreign body \nusually falls into the \nposterior basal segment \nof the right inferior lobe.\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":14,"text":"Ribs are not called “T” because they are not thoracic.\nRight lung: 3 lobes + extra horizontal fissure\nLeft lung: 2 lobes \nBoth: Oblique fissure between lobes. \nAuscultation of left/right lung using a stethoscope:\n1. Superior lobe: listen from front\n2. Middle lobe: listen from front between ribs 4 and 6 (horizontal fissure ends at \nrib 4)\n3. Inferior lobe: listen from the back of the body (sits on posterior surface)\n10\nLUNGS (Pulmones)\nLUNGS (Pulmones)\n◼ The right lung is divided into 3 \nlobes (superior, middle, inferior) \nseparated by two fissures, the \noblique and horizontal fissures. \nThe horizontal fissure separates the \nsuperior from the middle lobe and \nthe oblique fissure separates the \nmiddle from the inferior lobe.\n◼ The left lung is divided into two \nlobes (superior, and inferior) \nseparated by an oblique fissure. \nThe lingula of the upper lobe of \nthe left lung corresponds to the \nmiddle lobe of the right lung.\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":15,"text":"Surfaces:\n1. Costal: lateral (biggest) and related to ribs\n2. Mediastinal: medially located and has the hilum of the lungs with some \nimpressions from related organs\n3. Diaphragmatic: inferior and related to diaphragm. \n11\nParts of the lungs\nParts of the lungs\n◼ Apex\n◼ Base - sits on the \ndiaphragm\n◼ Surfaces:\n◼ Costal - corresponds \nto the thoracic wall\n◼ Mediastinal - faced \nthe mediastinum\n◼ Diaphragmatic – \nrelated to diaphragm\n◼ Anterior border: cardiac \nnotch – left lung only\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":16,"text":"Left lung impressions:\n1. Aortic arch\n2. Left subclavian artery\n3. Descending aorta \n4. Esophagus\nLingula (small tongue): part of the left lung related to the heart\n12\nLeft lung\nLeft lung\n◼ It is divided by the oblique \nfissure into 2 lobes: upper \nand lower\n◼ Hilum – depression located \nat about the center of the \nmediastinal surface -\n◼ Root of the Left lung ABV:\n◼ 1 pulmonary Artery,\n◼ 1 main Bronchus,\n◼ 2 pulmonary Veins\n◼ Mediastinal surface:\n◼ Aortic arch\n◼ Left subclavian \nartery\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":17,"text":"Right lung impressions:\n1. Superior vena cava \n2. Subclavian veins\n3. Inferior vena cava \n4. Esophagous \n5. Azygous vein\n13\nRight lung\nRight lung\n◼ slightly larger than the left\n◼ divided by the oblique and \nhorizontal fissures into 3 \nlobes: upper, middle, and \nlower\n◼ Right lung: BAV\n◼ 1 main Bronchus ,\n◼ 1 pulmonary Artery,\n◼ 2 pulmonary Veins\n◼ Mediastinal surfice:\n◼ Azygos vein\n◼ SVC\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":18,"text":"ANSWER: C\nPeumonia is inflammation of the lungs and can happen due to infection, irritation of \nforeign bodies, or tumours. The horizontal fissure is only on the right and \nunderneath we have the middle lobe.\n14\n"},{"page":19,"text":"In the 19th century, thoracic surgeons removed lungs in problems like tuberculosis \n(TB), so the patient had to live with one lung, which is difficult. In 1920s/1930s, they \nstarted to remove only the lobe which was infected. After 80s, we now remove just \na segment of the lung. The most challenging part in the thoracic surgery on the \nlungs is to seal up bronchus. Blood vessels can be ligated but bronchi cannot due to \nstrong cartilage, so we use special staplers (metal clips) to seal the bronchus after \nwe cut it off. You can see the main bronchus and lower bronchi with your naked eye, \nbut not bronchus of the third generation (2-3 millimetres).\nPneumothorax: If there is any tiny leak from the bronchus, air will get into the \npleural cavity, tension forces go up, and lung will collapse. \n15\nBronchopulmonary segments\nBronchopulmonary segments\n◼ It is the largest subdivision of a lung \nlobe: pyramidal in shape (apex \ndirected toward lung root and base \nat the pleural surface)\n◼ Separated from adjacent \nsegments by surrounded by \nconnective tissue septa\n◼ Supplied independently by a \nsegmental (tertiary) bronchus \nand a tertiary branch of the \npulmonary artery\n◼ Drained by intersegmental parts of \nthe pulmonary veins that lie in the \nconnective tissue between and \ndrain adjacent segments\n◼ Diseased segment can be resected \nsurgically\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":20,"text":"We have 10 segments in the right lung. You will never be asked to identify the \nlocation of the segments on the pictures. You will be asked to recall what kind of \nthe segments we have in each specific lung. This picture doesn't represent the real \nlocation of the segments but gives you the chance to memorise them. \n(the doctor reads the classification of segments) \n16\nRight lung:\n10 bronchopulmonary segmentsRight lung:\n10 bronchopulmonary segments\nSuperior lobe:\n1. Apical\n2. Anterior\n3. Posterior\nMiddle lobe:\n4. Lateral\n5. Medial\nInferior lobe:\n6. Superior\n7. Anterior basal\n8. Posterior basal\n9. Lateral basal\n10.Medial basal\n1\n3\n2\n8\n9\n6 4\n5\n10\n7\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":21,"text":"ANSWER: A\n17\n"},{"page":22,"text":"We have 9 bronchopulmonary segments. In some textbooks, they are even ten.\nInferior lung in the left lung has absolutely the same segments like in the right lung.\nWe have two lingual segments (superior and inferior). Most people have \napicoposterior as a single segment and an anterior segment, but a significant \nvariation of normal patients have two segments (apical and posterior). So it could be \n9 or 10 depending on which person we are talking about. You will never be given \noptions to choose between these two. \n18\nLeft lung -\n9 bronchopulmonary segmentsLeft lung -\n9 bronchopulmonary segments\nSuperior lobe:\n1. Apicoposterior\n2. Anterior\n3. Superior lingular\n4. Inferior lingular\nInferior lobe:\n5. Superior\n6. Anterior basal\n7. Posterior basal\n8. Lateral basal\n9. Medial basal\n1\n2\n3 5\n4 7\n9 8\n6\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":23,"text":"Bronchogenic carcinoma: Tumour in the lungs. We call it bronchogenic because \ngenesis means origin. No tumours come from lung tissue, all tumours in the lungs \ncome from bronchus.\nClinical signs:\n1. Cough: constant, unprovoked\n2. Hemoptysis: Hemo = blood, Ptysis = spitting (of blood)\n3. Early metastasis: coming to thoracic bronchomediastinal (specifically) lymphatic \nnerves\n4. Pancoast tumor: shown on x-ray (better to have an MRI), not a kind of tumor \nbut location, specifically the apex of the right or left lung \n19\nBronchogenic carcinoma\nBronchogenic carcinoma\n◼ Arises in the mucosa of the \nlarge bronchi\n◼ Produces a persistent, \nproductive cough or \nhemoptysis\n◼ Early metastasis to thoracic \n(bronchomediatinal) lymph \nnodes\n◼ Hematogenous spread to the \nbrain, bones, lungs, and \nsuprarenal glands\n◼ A tumor at the apex of the lung \n(Pancoast tumor) may result \nin Thoracic Outlet Syndrome \nand Horner Syndrome\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":24,"text":"99% of the time hemoptysis is a clinical sign of tumour (no 1% because deep diagnosis is \nnot part of clinical anatomy), but it could also be present in different disorders so do not \njump to conclusions. \n19"},{"page":25,"text":"Pancoast tumours are unique because of the symptoms they could produce. The \nyellow stuff is nerves (brachial plexus) running to the upper limb.\nThoracic Outlet Syndrome (TOS):\n- When inferior trunk is affected by the tumour\n- Anatomically, we call this inlet because it is the entrance to the thoracic cavity. \nClinicians talk from the heart so from their point of view it is outlet. \n- Atrophy (weakness) of the intrinsic muscles of the hand is the most prominent \nclinical sign (patient cannot move fingers)\n20\nBronchogenic carcinoma (Pancoast tumor) may lead to:\nBronchogenic carcinoma \n(Pancoast tumor) may lead to:\n1\n1. Thoracic outlet syndrome (TOS)\n◼ It can cause pressure on the lower \ntrunk of the brachial plexus C8-T1 and \nsubclavian artery by a cervical rib or \npancoast tumor. It results in pain \ndown the medial side of the forearm \nand hand and atrophy of the intrinsic \nhand muscles)\n2. Horner syndrome:\n2 1. ptosis - drooping of the eyelid due to \nparalysis of the superior tarsal muscle\n2. miosis - constriction of the pupil due \nto paralysis of the dilator pupillae \nmuscle\n3. hemianhydrosis - loss of sweating on \none side of the face\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":26,"text":"Horner Syndrome: \n- Pancoast tumour will interrupt sympathetic pathways running through neck towards the \nhead.\n- Ptosis: The patient’s upper eyelid droops down. The eye is still visible, but only the \nlower half (partial). We have complete ptosis because of the oculomotor nerve, but \nthat’s a neurosign.\n- Miosis: Constriction of pupil in the affected side of face (looks smaller than normal)\n- Hemianhydrosis: Lack of sweating in the affected side of face. Sweat glands are \ninnervated by sympathetic, and we need them to decrease the local temperature of the \nbody by fluid evaporating. The half of the face that has no sweat becomes red and dry \nbecause of local temperature.\n- Horner’s Syndrome could happen on either side of the face \n20"},{"page":27,"text":"Superior Vena Cava Syndrome:\n- Only in tumour of the right lung\n- Left side of SVC: aortic arch (red), which is the strongest elastic artery and cannot \nbe compressed (like a brick). \n- SVC will be compressed (thin walls), compromising blood outflow, leading to \ndilation of superficial veins of head, neck, and upper limbs since SVC drains blood \nfrom them (become swollen)\n- Cyanosis: blue skin\n- Dysphagia: difficulty swallowing due to esophagus obstruction. \n- Diaphram paralysis: paradoxical diaphragm movement due to phrenic nerve\n21\nBronchogenic carcinoma may lead to:\nBronchogenic carcinoma \nmay lead to:\n3. Superior vena cava \nsyndrome, which causes \ndilation of the head and \nneck veins, facial \nswelling, and cyanosis\n4. Dysphagia as a result of \nesophageal obstruction\n5. Hoarseness as a result \nof recurrent laryngeal \nnerve involvement\n6. Paralysis of the \ndiaphragm as a result\n3 of phrenic nerve \ninvolvement\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":28,"text":"The pulmonary arteries and veins do not provide nutrition and supplements to the \nlungs because they are for gas exchange.\nBronchial arteries: provide nutrients and oxygen to lung cells \nBronchial veins: drain deoxygenated blood from lungs \nBronchomediastinal lymph nodes: on mediastinal surface around bronchi\n22\nBlood supply and lymphatic drainage of the lungs\nBlood supply and lymphatic \ndrainage of the lungs\nArteries:\n◼ bronchial arteries – from \nthe thoracic aorta supply \nthe structures comprising \nthe root of the  lung, \nsupporting tissues of the \nlung, and the visceral pleura\nVeins:\n◼ bronchial veins drainage \nto azygos system of veins\nLymphatic Drainage:\n◼ bronchomediastinal\nlymph nodes and trunks\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":29,"text":"Pneumonia looks bright on a plain X-ray (no contrast used). Pneumonia is typically \ncaused by infection:\n1. Fungal pneumonia: Most dangerous and has a 90% mortality rate because we \ncannot treat it (we don't have antifungal antibiotics). It will take at least 3 to 6 \nmonths to treat but the patient will not survive a pneumonia for the 3 months \neven with all kinds of modern treatments.\n2. Viruses: Mild, except deadly viruses like Ebola\n3. Bacterial: Difficult but treatable with antibiotics.\n23\nPneumonia\nPneumonia\n◼ Pneumonia is an inflammation of \nthe lung, caused by an infection or \nchemical injury to the lungs.\n◼ Three common causes are \nbacteria, viruses, and fungi.\n◼ Symptoms: cough, chest pain, \nfever, and difficulty in breathing.\n◼ Chest x-rays: areas of opacity \n(seen as white) of the lung \nparenchyma and enlargement of \nbronchomediastinal lymph nodes \n(mediastinal widening).\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":30,"text":"Posteroanterior projection:X-ray images of lungs, where x-ray beams are coming from the \nback. We don't do it from the front because the heart shadow will cover most of the lungs.\nAnteroposterior projection: X-ray images for the rest of the body (head, neck, upper and \nlower limbs) but not for the thorax.\n23"},{"page":31,"text":"24\nPlease take the practice quiz:\nPlease take the practice quiz:\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"},{"page":32,"text":"25\nReferences\nReferences\n◼ Gray's anatomy for students by Richard L. Drake, Wayne Vogl, \nAdam W. M. Mitchell; Churchill Livingstone; 2 edition; ISBN-13: \n978-0443069529, ISBN-10: 0443069522\n◼ Clinically Oriented Anatomy by Keith L. Moore, Arthur F. Dalley,\nA. M. R. Agur; Lippincott Williams & Wilkins; 7th edition; ISBN-\n13: 978-1451119459; ISBN-10: 1451119453\n◼ Langman's Medical Embryology by Thomas W Sadler; Lippincott \nWilliams & Wilkins; 12th edition; ISBN-13: 978-1451113426; \nISBN-10: 1451113420\n◼ Netter's Clinical Anatomy by John T. Hansen, David R. \nLambert, Frank H. Netter; ICON Learning Systems; \nISBN13: 9781929007714\n@ Dr. Olena, 2026"}],"url":"/cvp-pulmonary-reader.html?source=cvp-pulm-t-a16"},{"id":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","displayName":"A17_Histology of the Lower Respiratory Tract_Transcription (1)-1.pdf","name":"A17_Histology of the Lower Respiratory Tract_Transcription (1)-1.pdf","kind":"Supplied lecture transcript","unit":"page","lectureIds":["CVP-A17"],"pages":[{"page":1,"text":" \n \n \nTranscription by: Adam \nReviewed by: Binish \nTranscription: \n- Last week we covered the nasal cavity, pharynx, larynx and trachea and stopped there. This \nweek we continue the conducting portion and cover the respiratory portion of the \nrespiratory tract. \n \n1 \n"},{"page":2,"text":" \n \nThe lecture covers the structure of each part of the bronchial tree, the difference between \nbronchi and bronchioles, the structure and function of the alveoli, and the structure of the \npleura. \nTranscription: \n- From the bronchial tree inside the lung, there are two structures you have to know: the \nbronchi and the bronchioles, plus the alveoli. This is the structure of the lung. \n \n2 \n"},{"page":3,"text":" \n \nThe respiratory tract is divided functionally into a conducting portion, which cleans and \nhumidifies the inspired air and provides conduits for air movement (inside and outside the \nlung), and a respiratory portion, where gas exchange takes place (only inside the lung). \nTranscription: \n- Recap from last lecture: the respiratory system is divided into two parts, the conducting \nportion and the respiratory portion. \n- We consider a part to be respiratory because gas exchange can happen in that area. \n- The last part of the conducting portion is the terminal bronchiole. \n- The first part of the respiratory portion is the one that has alveoli: the respiratory \nbronchiole. \n- This is really very important; you have to know it. \n \n3 \n"},{"page":4,"text":" \n \nThe lungs are a pair of spongy, air-filled organs on both sides of the chest, covered by a thin \nlayer called the pleura. Each lung is divided into lobes (2 left, 3 right), then bronchopulmonary \nsegments (8 left, 10 right), then pulmonary lobules. \nTranscription: \n- We have two lungs, and each is a spongy structure. We have to know the lobes, segments \nand lobules. \n- Left lung: two lobes and eight segments (different books give different numbers; in our \nbooks it is eight, so we consider eight). \n- Right lung: three lobes and 9 to 10 segments, according to different books. \n- If we divide the segments further, the next level is the lobule. So we have lobes, then \nsegments in the middle, then lobules. \n- We will never ask about the number of segments in histology. Stick to the anatomy \nnumbers. \n \n4 \n"},{"page":5,"text":" \n \nThe trachea divides into the right and left main (primary) bronchi, which enter the hilum of \neach lung. Each primary bronchus divides into lobar (secondary) bronchi: 3 on the right and 2 \non the left, each supplying one lobe. The lobar bronchi give rise to segmental (tertiary) bronchi: \n10 on the right and 8 on the left, each supplying one bronchopulmonary segment. Each \nsegment is subdivided into pulmonary lobules, and each lobule is supplied by a bronchiole. \nTranscription: \n- The trachea, with its C-shaped hyaline cartilage, divides into two before it reaches the lung. \nThis is the first division, and before it enters the lung we name it the main or primary \nbronchus. \n- Left side: there are two lobes, so the second division gives two branches. We name it the \nlobar bronchus because it supplies the lobe. This is the secondary bronchus. \n- There are eight segments on the left, so the third (tertiary) division gives eight segmental \nbronchi. \n- Right side: one primary bronchus, then three lobar (secondary) bronchi for the three lobes, \nthen 9 to 10 segmental (tertiary) bronchi. \n- The segmental bronchi then divide to supply the lobules. These are the bronchioles: the \nbronchiole supplies the lobule. \n- Summary of the divisions: primary (main) → secondary (lobar) → tertiary (segmental) → \nbronchiole. \n- You need to know this by heart, not only in histology. Even in anatomy you need to know \nexactly how we divide the bronchial tree. \n- Histologically, the primary, secondary and tertiary bronchi all have the same histological \nstructure. Once we reach the bronchiole, it is a different one. \n5 \n"},{"page":6,"text":" \n \nInitially, the bronchi have the same general structure as the trachea. Where they enter the lungs \nto become intrapulmonary bronchi, the wall changes in two ways: the cartilage rings are \nreplaced by irregular cartilage plates around the entire circumference of the wall, and smooth \nmuscle is added to form a complete circumferential layer. \nTranscription: \n- Recap of the trachea (four layers, from inside out; this is just memorization): \no Mucosa: epithelium plus lamina propria. The epithelium is respiratory epithelium. \no Submucosa: with mucous glands with serous demilunes (mucous cells capped by \nsome serous cells). \no Cartilage: C-shaped hyaline cartilage. \no Adventitia. \n- When a question comes and the answer is respiratory epithelium, I expect the long name, \nnot the word \"respiratory\": pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, with goblet \ncells if it has goblet cells. If it does not, you do not write them. \n- The trachea has C-shaped cartilage so the esophagus behind it has no problem while we \nswallow. The cartilage is C-shaped and the back is just soft. \n- Inside the lung there is no esophagus to press on, so we do not need the C-shape here. The \ncartilage is a full circle, but not one full plate: it is small pieces (plates) that encircle the \nwhole wall. \n \n7 \n"},{"page":7,"text":" \n \nThe wall of the intrapulmonary bronchi has five layers. The mucosa is folded because of \ncontraction of the smooth muscle in the wall. Its epithelium is ciliated pseudostratified \nepithelium with the same cellular composition as the trachea, and its lamina propria is loose \nconnective tissue rich in elastic and collagen fibres with diffuse and nodular lymphatic tissue \n(BALT). \nTranscription: \n- Layers starting from the inside: the mucosa (epithelium plus lamina propria), and the \nepithelium is the same as in the trachea. \n- Why is the mucosa folded? Because there is a smooth muscle layer that was not there \nbefore. We name this extra layer the muscularis. \n- Then the submucosa (yes, we still have it), the cartilage as small plates making a full circle, \nand the adventitia. \n- So the trachea has four layers and the bronchi have five. \n- Compared with the trachea, the bronchi add three changes: a folded mucosa, a smooth \nmuscle layer, and small pieces of cartilage making a full circle. The rest is exactly the same \nas the trachea. \n- Once you see this folding, it means there is muscle. \n \n8 \n"},{"page":8,"text":" \n \nThe muscularis is a continuous layer of smooth muscle in large bronchi, more attenuated and \nloosely organized in smaller bronchi, and it regulates the diameter of the airway. The \nsubmucosa is loose connective tissue with glands in the larger bronchi. The cartilage layer is \ndiscontinuous hyaline cartilage plates that become smaller as the diameter decreases. The \nadventitia is moderately dense connective tissue continuous with adjacent structures. \nTranscription: \n- Whatever I mentioned here is written in the slides; I did not add anything. \n- Histologically: epithelium (respiratory), lamina propria, smooth muscle, then the small \npieces of hyaline cartilage, then the adventitia. \n- There is submucosa too, under the muscle: some connective tissue with some very small \nglands. It is not always that clear. \n- There are two things you have to know here: the cartilage and the muscle. The layers are \notherwise exactly the same. \n- How do I know this section is inside the lung (intrapulmonary)? Because I can see the \nalveoli. Once you see the alveoli, you are inside the lung. \n \n9 \n"},{"page":9,"text":" \n \nBronchioles are air-conducting ducts that measure 1 mm or less in diameter. Large bronchioles \nbranch repeatedly to give the smaller terminal bronchioles, which branch to give the \nrespiratory bronchioles. \nTranscription: \n- The bronchiole is less than 1 mm in diameter, so it is very narrow. Some are large and some \nare small. \n- The last part of the conducting portion is the terminal bronchiole. You see how many \ntimes they ask this: terminal bronchiole, bronchiole, not bronchi. \n- The first part of the respiratory portion is the respiratory bronchiole. \n \n10 \n"},{"page":10,"text":" \n \nThe wall of bronchioles is made of a mucosa (epithelium and a lamina propria of loose \nconnective tissue with many elastic fibres and no glands), a muscularis (a relatively thick layer \nof smooth muscle in all bronchioles) and an adventitia attached to the surrounding alveoli, with \noccasional lymph nodules or diffuse lymphocytes. Cartilage plates and glands are not present \nin bronchioles. \nTranscription: \n- Bronchioles cannot have many goblet cells. Can we tolerate glands here? No; if we had \nglands it would be a disaster. So there is no submucosa. \n- At this size, do we need cartilage for support? No. So there is no cartilage. \n- So the bronchiole has only three layers: epithelium with lamina propria (mucosa), muscle, \nand adventitia. The muscle layer is thick compared with the bronchi. \n- Two layers are missing compared with the bronchi: no glands (no submucosa) and no \ncartilage. \n- All bronchioles, until the terminal bronchiole, have this structure. \n- Again, we know it is inside the lung because we can see the alveoli. \n \n11 \n"},{"page":11,"text":" \n \nLarge bronchioles are initially lined by ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium with few \ngoblet cells, and smaller bronchioles by simple ciliated columnar or cuboidal epithelium. Goblet \ncells are still present in large bronchioles but not in terminal bronchioles. Club (Clara) cells are \npresent in small bronchioles, and their number increases greatly along the length of the \nbronchioles. Occasional brush cells and small granule cells are also present. \nTranscription: \n- The epithelium depends on the size: \no Large bronchiole: respiratory epithelium, but a little shorter. \no Smaller: simple columnar. \no Very narrow: simple cuboidal. We can see this very clearly under the microscope. \n- Goblet cells: as we go inside the lung, we cannot have a lot of mucus, otherwise we would \nbe suffocating in our secretions. So there are some goblet cells at the beginning, and they \nbecome fewer as it gets narrower. \n- Then we have a new cell called the club cell (Clara cell). \n- In the exam, if you are given a bronchiole picture and asked for the lining epithelium, you \ncannot write all of them. Look at the picture, see which type of epithelium it is, and write \nthat one. \n \n12 \n"},{"page":12,"text":" \n \nClub (Clara) cells are non-ciliated cells with a rounded or dome-shaped apical surface. They are \nprotein-secreting cells. Their functions are to secrete a surface-active agent, a lipoprotein that \nprevents luminal adhesion and airway collapse, particularly during expiration; to produce club \ncell secretory protein (CC16), which has immunomodulatory and anti-inflammatory activities; \nand to act as progenitor cells that can differentiate into other epithelial cell types. \nTranscription: \n- Why do we need this cell? It secretes a surfactant-like substance. Surfactant itself is made in \nthe alveoli, but we are not yet in the alveoli. At this point the lumen is very narrow, we need \nsomething to decrease the surface tension, and there are no type II pneumocytes here. This \nis why we have the club cell. \n- Shape: it has no cilia on its surface. This is why we sometimes describe the bronchiolar \nepithelium as having ciliated columnar cells and some non-ciliated cells. \n- It is a protein-forming cell, so the organelles I expect are: rough endoplasmic reticulum \n(protein factory), Golgi, mitochondria for energy, and vesicles (granules) to store what it \nmakes. \n- These cells are not easy to find under the light microscope, which is why you will not find \nthem in the lab material. \n- It also produces CC16 (club cell protein 16), which has an immunological function: \nanti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory for the surrounding environment. \n- Last lecture, respiratory epithelium had five cells: columnar cells, goblet cells, brush cells, \nsmall granule cells and stem (basal) cells. In simple columnar or cuboidal epithelium there \nare no basal stem cells, so the club cell is the progenitor cell of the epithelial cells in the \nbronchiole. \n13 \n"},{"page":13,"text":"- These three functions are all important; you have to know them: surfactant-like \nsubstance, CC16 protein, and progenitor cell. \n- Do not compare it with type I alveolar cells; do not go that way. \n- The name: the cells were described by a scientist named Clara using humans as \nexperimental subjects during the Second World War. Very recently the textbooks removed \nhis name and now use the word club cell. \n \n14 "},{"page":14,"text":" \n \nThe presence of cilia beyond the most distal mucus-secreting cells prevents bronchiolar mucus \naccumulation and airflow obstruction. The ciliated cells lining the bronchioles are the beginning \nof the mucociliary escalator. \nTranscription: \n- Can we tolerate goblet cells without cilia in the very small passages of the lung? No. The \nsecretions would accumulate in our lung and there would be no way to push them up. \n- This is why the goblet cells have to stop before the cilia stop. This is very important \nstructurally. \n- Order of disappearance: the glands stop first, then the goblet cells, then the cilia. So if \nsome secretion goes a little further, there are still cilia there to push it up. \n- The function of this area: it is part of the mucociliary escalator. You should know this \nexpression. \n \n15 \n"},{"page":15,"text":" \n \nBack to the functional organization: the conducting portion ends at the terminal bronchiole, and \nthe respiratory portion, only inside the lung, begins at the respiratory bronchiole. \nTranscription: \n- This is the terminal bronchiole, which has the same three-layer structure. Then all of a \nsudden some alveoli start to appear in the wall of the bronchiole. \n- Once we have alveoli, we stop calling it terminal and call it the respiratory bronchiole. \n \n16 \n"},{"page":16,"text":" \n \nRespiratory bronchioles are a transitional zone involved in both air conduction and gas \nexchange. Their wall has two components: areas lined by simple cuboidal epithelium with \nciliated cells and club cells (club cells predominate distally) on a lamina propria of elastic fibres \nand smooth muscle, and scattered thin-walled outpocketings, the alveoli, capable of gas \nexchange. \nTranscription: \n- The difference between the terminal and the respiratory bronchiole is the alveoli: they are \noutpocketings from the wall of the bronchiole. \n- The respiratory bronchiole is a transition part: one part is exactly like the terminal \nbronchiole, and the other part has the structure of the alveoli. \n- It is the first part of the respiratory portion because it has alveoli, and in the alveoli gas \nexchange happens. By respiration here I mean gas exchange: taking oxygen and getting rid \nof carbon dioxide. \n- Identifying the respiratory bronchiole is one of the very common questions in the OSPE. \nThe terminal bronchiole has no outpocketings; I know this one is respiratory because I can \nsee the alveoli in its wall. \n- Layers of the bronchiolar part: the lining is simple cuboidal, with only a single layer of round \nnuclei. No options here, because there is no space for simple columnar. \n- There are a lot of club cells and no goblet cells (we cannot have them). Then the smooth \nmuscle (muscularis) layer, then the adventitia. \n \n17 \n"},{"page":17,"text":" \n \nAlveolar ducts are elongated airways with almost no walls, only alveoli, as their peripheral \nboundary; rings of smooth muscle are present in the knob-like interalveolar septa. Alveolar sacs \nare spaces surrounded by clusters of alveoli that open into them. They usually occur at the end \nof an alveolar duct but may occur anywhere along its length. \nTranscription: \n- There are two structures you have to know here: a long one and a rounded one. \n- The long one is a pathway with no wall, made only by the openings of the alveoli: the \nalveolar duct. \n- The rounded one is like a bunch of grapes, a distended space made by alveoli: the alveolar \nsac. \n- Very simple: if it is long, it is a duct; if it is distended, it is a sac. \n- Do ducts or sacs have club cells? No. The last part with club cells is the respiratory \nbronchiole. Once we have only alveoli, there are no club cells and no respiratory epithelium; \nthere are different cells, covered next. \n \n18 \n"},{"page":18,"text":" \n \nAlveoli are the terminal air spaces of the respiratory system and the actual site of gas exchange \nbetween air and blood. Each adult lung has about 150 to 250 million alveoli. Each is a \nthin-walled polyhedral chamber about 0.2 mm in diameter, surrounded by a network of \ncapillaries that brings blood close to the inhaled air. \nTranscription: \n- There are millions of alveoli, about 150 to 250 million, a very big number. \n- Each is a very small structure (about 0.2 mm in diameter, as on the slide). \n- The cells of the alveolus: \no Two cells make the wall: type I and type II pneumocytes (alveolar cells). \no There are also macrophages, but they wander inside the lumen. They are not \nattached and do not make the wall. \n \n19 \n"},{"page":19,"text":" \n \nType I alveolar cells (type I pneumocytes) are extremely thin squamous cells. They make up \nonly 40% of the alveolar-lining cells but line 95% of the alveolar surface. They are joined to one \nanother and to the other alveolar cells by occluding junctions and are not capable of cell \ndivision. Function: gas exchange, forming part of the air-blood barrier. \nTranscription: \n- Type I cells are very flat, long cells. This is why they are only 40% of the cells lining the \nalveoli but cover 95% of the surface area. \n- Function: gas exchange, and they form the blood-air barrier. These are the two important \nfunctions you have to know. \n- Can they regenerate? No. \n- Can we allow fluid to pass between the cells? No. This is why they are attached to each \nother by tight (occluding) junctions. \n- Remember: 40% of the cells, covering 95%, occluding junctions, gas exchange and part of \nthe blood-air barrier, and they cannot regenerate. It is that simple; do not complicate it. \n \n20 \n"},{"page":20,"text":" \n \nType II alveolar cells (type II pneumocytes or septal cells) are secretory, cuboidal cells \ninterspersed among type I cells that tend to congregate at septal junctions. They make up 60% \nof the alveolar-lining cells but cover only about 5% of the surface, and they bulge into the \nalveolar space. Their apical cytoplasm is filled with lamellar bodies. Functions: progenitor cells \nfor type I and type II cells, and secretion of surfactant. \nTranscription: \n- If type I is 40%, type II is 60%. If type I covers 95%, and there are only these two cells, type II \ncovers only 5%. \n- The other cell cannot regenerate, so type II is the stem cell for both type I and type II. \n- Compare: the stem cell in the bronchiole is the club cell; the stem cell of the alveolus is the \ntype II cell. \n- Type II is the one that projects into the lumen. Type I is squamous; type II is cuboidal. \n- Other names: secretory cell, because it secretes surfactant, and septal cell, because it is \nusually found close to the septum. \n- Function: production of surfactant, which decreases the surface tension so the alveoli do \nnot collapse. \n \n21 \n"},{"page":21,"text":" \n \nSurfactant is a surface-active agent synthesized by type II pneumocytes and stored in lamellar \nbodies. It is an oily mixture of phospholipids (the most critical is DPPC), neutral lipids and \nsurfactant proteins. DPPC accounts for almost all of its surface-tension-reducing properties. \nFunctions: it decreases alveolar surface tension and actively participates in the clearance of \nforeign materials. \nTranscription: \n- Something very unique to the type II cell: the vesicle that stores surfactant. The surfactant is \narranged as layers inside the vesicle, which is why we call it the lamellar body. \n- It is a very unique structure that you cannot find in any other place. Once you see a lamellar \nbody, this is a type II cell. \n- Surfactant has three parts: phospholipids, neutral lipids and proteins. The structure is not \nthat important in histology. \n- The most important component is the phospholipid (DPPC), which is responsible for \ndecreasing the surface tension. I want this information. \n- Functions: decreasing the surface tension, plus some immunological function, like cleaning \nthe surface. \n \n22 \n"},{"page":22,"text":" \n \nSurfactant is secreted by exocytosis to form a thin layer lining the alveoli. Without enough \nsurfactant, the alveoli would collapse with each exhalation. This happens in preterm infants \nwhose lungs cannot yet produce enough surfactant, leading to neonatal respiratory distress \nsyndrome (RDS). \nTranscription: \n- Surfactant comes out by exocytosis, a highly active transport. Once it is out, it covers the \nwhole surface of the alveoli to decrease the surface tension. \n- A baby delivered in the seventh month: the lung is not developed, so we keep him in the \nincubator until the cells that produce surfactant become well developed. \n- The disease is neonatal respiratory distress syndrome, in babies with immature lungs, \nmostly preterm babies. \n- From the questions: once they start breathing normally, they no longer have RDS. They are \ngiven medication to enhance this process, so they do not stay in the incubator for long. \n \n23 \n"},{"page":23,"text":" \n \nAlveolar macrophages are derived from blood monocytes and belong to the mononuclear \nphagocyte system. Alveolar macrophages function in the air space of the alveolus and remove \ninhaled particles such as dust and pollen (dust cells) and red blood cells that may enter the \nalveoli in heart failure (heart failure cells). Engorged macrophages pass up the bronchial tree in \nthe mucus and are disposed of by swallowing or expectoration. \nTranscription: \n- The mother cell of the macrophage is the monocyte, the same family as the microglia you \nremember. \n- One type lives inside the alveolus: the alveolar macrophage. It works like a vacuum, \nscavenging anything we do not want from the surface. If dust reaches this part, the cell eats \nthe dust, which is why we call it the dust cell. \n- In heart failure, patients get pulmonary hypertension. The pressure pushes some red blood \ncells into the alveoli, where they should not be. The macrophages eat them and become \ndarker. These are called heart failure cells. \n- After some time the cell is filled with particles and we have to get rid of it. How does it reach \nthe parts with cilia? These cells have no cilia, but they have pseudopodia, so they can move \nuntil they reach the closest part that has cilia. The cilia then push them up to the pharynx. \n- There they are either swallowed (mostly), and the HCl helps, or, if the cough is strong \nenough, they are coughed out. \n \n24 \n"},{"page":24,"text":" \n \nSeptal macrophages reside in the connective tissue of the interalveolar septa. They are filled \nwith accumulated phagocytosed material and may remain in the septa for much of a person's \nlife. Both alveolar and septal macrophages increase in smokers. \nTranscription: \n- The other option: if the cell cannot be taken out, it squeezes between the cells and goes to \nlive in the septum. This is why we call it the septal macrophage. \n- It is a cell that has already done its function inside. We do not want it anymore, so we just \nkeep it here, for life. \n- This is a problem in smokers, because the number of these cells increases a lot, and over \ntime I think it affects the function of the lung. \n- So there are two macrophages, or the same cell in a different situation: the alveolar \nmacrophage (scavenger, also called dust cell or heart failure cell) and the septal \nmacrophage (stays for life, increased in smokers). In the picture you can see the difference \nin color. \n- From the questions: the alveolar macrophage can pass between the cells to reach the \nseptum. \n \n25 \n"},{"page":25,"text":" \n \nThe interalveolar septum is thin connective tissue between adjacent alveolar air spaces. Its wall \nis a network of elastic and reticular fibres; the elastic fibres are responsible for the stretching \nand recoil of the alveoli during respiration. It has a rich capillary network from the pulmonary \nartery and is the site of the air-blood barrier. Alveolar pores (of Kohn) penetrate the septa and \nconnect neighbouring alveoli that open into different bronchioles, allowing air to circulate from \none alveolus to another. \nTranscription: \n- If I want to say one structure inside the interalveolar septum, it is the elastic fibres. The \nmain function of the alveoli is to stretch and recoil, so we cannot ignore them. \n- Imagine a bronchus, a bronchiole and its alveoli. If I put a block in one bronchiole, nothing \nreaches its alveoli, and they would collapse. \n- But between the alveoli there are openings called the pores of Kohn. If only one bronchiole \nis blocked, its alveoli are still ventilated through these pores from the neighbouring alveoli. \nThe air concentration will be higher in one than the other, but it is still functioning. \n- Without these pores, whatever block happened, the whole part would collapse. \n- This is normal, not pathological. \n- The most important structure that you cannot forget in the septum is the elastic fibres. \nAnd the pores connecting the alveoli are the pores of Kohn. \n \n26 \n"},{"page":26,"text":" \n \nThe air-blood barrier (respiratory membrane) is the cells and cell products across which gases \ndiffuse between the alveolar and capillary compartments. Its thin portion is made of a thin \nlayer of surfactant, a type I epithelial cell and its basal lamina, and a capillary endothelial cell \nand its basal lamina. These two basal laminae are often fused. Most gas exchange occurs in the \nthin portion. \nTranscription: \n- Layers of the blood-air barrier, in order, from the air to the blood: \no Surfactant (the liquid lining). \no Type I pneumocyte. \no Fused basement membranes (of the type I cell and the endothelium). \no Endothelium of the capillary. \n- It has to be in order. This part is also called the respiratory membrane. \n- There is a thin portion and a thick portion. The thin portion is very important, and its \nfunction is gas exchange. \n \n27 \n"},{"page":27,"text":" \n \nThe thick portion contains connective tissue cells (such as macrophages and fibroblasts) and \nfibres between the two basal laminae. It is thought to be where tissue fluid accumulates before \nbeing drained into the lymphatic vessels. \nTranscription: \n- In the thick portion there is a space between the two alveoli. Why do we need it? \n- We cannot tolerate any fluid inside the alveoli, so any extra fluid has to go somewhere. It \ngoes here. \n- This space has a few connective tissue cells, like macrophages and fibroblasts. \n- Function: it accumulates any extra fluid in the lung for a very short time, until it reaches the \nlymphatic vessels. \n \n28 \n"},{"page":28,"text":" \n \nThe pleura is a serous membrane that covers the outer surface of the lung and the internal wall \nof the thoracic cavity. The parietal pleura lines the internal thoracic walls, and the visceral \npleura tightly covers the outer surface of each lung. The two layers are continuous at the hilum \nof each lung. \nTranscription: \n- You already had the pericardium. The pleura is exactly the same; nothing changes. \n- The layer touching the lung is the visceral pleura, the one lining the thoracic wall from \ninside is the parietal pleura, and between them is the pleural cavity. \n \n29 \n"},{"page":29,"text":" \n \nBoth pleurae are made of simple squamous mesothelial cells on a thin connective tissue layer \nwith collagen and elastic fibres, which allows flexibility during breathing. The pleural cavity is a \nnarrow space between the parietal and visceral layers, lined by mesothelium. Mesothelial cells \nproduce a thin film of serous fluid that acts as a lubricant, so the surfaces slide smoothly during \nrespiratory movement. \nTranscription: \n- The lining is mesothelium (simple squamous epithelium). \n- Its function is producing serous fluid, exactly the same as in the pericardium. \n- Is there a lamina propria under it? Yes. If there were no connective tissue under the \nepithelium, how would these cells survive? We have to have connective tissue under the \nepithelium. \n \n30 \n"},{"page":30,"text":" \n \nBronchial asthma is a chronic inflammatory disease of the airways (genetic and environmental \nfactors), with wheezing, coughing, shortness of breath and chest tightness. The bronchiolar \nepithelium has more goblet cells and a thick basement membrane, with a thick hyperplastic \nsmooth muscle layer. \nTranscription: \n- There are a few disease names you have to know. You will mostly see them in the TBL and \nin the final exam, in the cases. I do not want the pathology at all, but when you see \nbronchial asthma, understand that I am talking mostly about the bronchioles, and with \nemphysema I am talking about the alveoli. \n- These patients, mostly kids seen in the pediatric clinic, come with wheezing and cannot take \ntheir breath. \n- The cause is sometimes genetic and sometimes environmental, most commonly dust in the \nair. \n- Why is the drawing a bronchiole and not a bronchus? There is no cartilage. It has three \nlayers: epithelium, muscle and adventitia. \n- What happens: a lot of thick secretion, so many goblet cells, and a thick muscle layer. It is a \nproblem in the bronchioles. \n \n31 \n"},{"page":31,"text":" \n \nEmphysema is a chronic lung disease most commonly caused by cigarette smoking. It involves \ndilatation and permanent enlargement of the air spaces with destruction of the alveolar wall. \nThe main symptom is shortness of breath, which usually begins gradually. \nTranscription: \n- Emphysema is common in those who smoke. \n- The interalveolar septa are destroyed. Imagine several alveoli; at the end there is only one \nspace because many septa have disappeared. \n- So there is less surface area for gas exchange, and they complain of shortness of breath. \n \n32 \n"},{"page":32,"text":" \n \nCystic fibrosis is a genetic disorder with production of thick, viscous mucus from almost all \nexocrine glands. The mucociliary escalator malfunctions, so the thick mucus accumulates, and \npatients have frequent respiratory infections. \nTranscription: \n- It is genetic and affects multiple organs, but it affects the lung too. \n- In the lung there is very thick mucus. This is very important: the cilia are functioning, but \nthey cannot push the thick mucus. So the patients drown in their secretions. \n- You should at least know these three, and also the clinical items from last time, because \nthey are mostly in the TBL and the final. \n33 \n"}],"url":"/cvp-pulmonary-reader.html?source=cvp-pulm-t-a17"},{"id":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","displayName":"P16 - Introduction to Pulmonary Physiology Transcription (Hatem).pdf","name":"P16 - Introduction to Pulmonary Physiology Transcription (Hatem).pdf","kind":"Supplied lecture transcript","unit":"page","lectureIds":["CVP-P16"],"pages":[{"page":1,"text":"1\n Transcribed by Hatem\nWhat I'm teaching now in these 13 hours is pulmonary physiology.\n"},{"page":2,"text":"2\nAnd now I'm coming back to respiratory physiology. In respiratory physiology, we use a lot of symbols.\nAnd these are the primary symbols, for example, capital C stands for compliance that you have learned\nalso in cardio. And the C also stands for concentration of a gas in blood. For example, F for fraction\nand so on. That is listed for you. And if you read there and see these symbols and you don't know what\nis it, then look at this list and you see what does it mean. Okay?\n"},{"page":3,"text":"3\nWe use other symbols, for instance, here capital A, B, and so on for alveolar, for barometric, for dead\nspace, and so on.\n"},{"page":4,"text":"4\nAnd then we have also a secondary symbols for blood phase. In respiratory physiology, sometimes we\nare in the gas. As we are in the alveolus, alveolus full with the gas, we merge with the gas phase and\nsuddenly we dive into the blood. And it becomes fluid. And we must call the things in the fluid. And\nthese are symbols which are used for blood phase. Small A for arterial, C for capillaries, C dash here at\nthe top, it means end capillary. At the end of the capillary.\nAnd V dash on the top like this means mixed venous. What it mean mixed venous? You know that the\nvenous blood which comes from different organs with different veins and have different concentration\nof oxygen and CO2. After they mix all of these venous blood mix in the heart together in the right heart\nand the right ventricle. And then the composition of that blood is called mixed venous blood. It means\nafter the blood has been from all organs have mixed together. That is mixed venous blood.\nAnd sometimes we combine these symbols together. For example, this one CaO2. C for concentration,\nA arterial, O2 is oxygen. It means arterial concentration of oxygen. Oxygen concentration in arterial\nblood. And here for instance, E expiratory fraction, N2 nitrogen, it means expiratory fraction of nitrogen.\nAnd so on, you can combine these symbols together.\n"},{"page":5,"text":"5\nAnd now to the topic of the lecture. today's lecture is introduction morphology of the lung. Then we\nhave lung volume, lung mechanics, the lecture in airflow resistance. These are the first six lectures that\nyou receive my quiz on the afterwards. And then these are the learning objectives for this lecture, for\ntoday's lecture. And my objectives are numbered, P1, P2, P3.\n"},{"page":6,"text":"6\nOkay. Now I will start with the respiration physiology or pulmonary physiology. What is the job of the\npulmonary physiologies? The respiration has a lot of functions. There is no respiration, I cannot talk to\nyou. How can I talk without the bringing the air in and out? How can I sing without respiration? You see\nthere are a lot of respiration also immune function has the respiratory tract and so on. There is a lot of\nfunctions, but the main function of respiratory system is to transport oxygen from the air into individual\ncells in the body.\nAnd CO2 is produced in the cells and then transport that CO2 from the cells back to the atmosphere to\nthe outside. That is the main main job of the respiratory system. Transport oxygen from here outside to\nall cells of the body here. And you see as this oxygen enters the lung, enters the blood, transported\nwith blood, and goes to the cells, and then it is used in mitochondria of the cells to produce ATP. ATP is\nthe energy coin of the body that you need for every process.\nAnd by metabolism, CO2 is produced, and you know that CO2 is transported in the opposite direction\nback to the atmosphere. So, and now if you look at the oxygen partial pressure here, you see that\noxygen partial pressure is here in the air about 150 mm of mercury. That is about 200 cm of water. And\nyou see as oxygen is transported through these chains, and it reaches in the cell, it is almost zero. The\noxygen pressure drops during this transport from the air to the cells.\nBefore it is utilized by mitochondria, the pressure is not zero. It is a little bit above zero. But the good\nthing is that the mitochondria can use this oxygen even with a low pressure and produce ATP. And\nCO2 is now produced in the cells. Therefore, the highest pressure of CO2 you find in the cell. And then\nduring the transport to the outside, you see that this CO2, the highest value in the cell, and then drops\nand drops, and at the air as we exhale it to the air, it is almost zero.\nSo, the partial pressure of the gases drops this transport. Why? You will see it during the lectures. And\nthe question is what is oxygen partial pressure? What is this? Oxygen partial pressure? We have a lot\nof pressure. Oxygen partial pressure, arterial blood pressure, venous pressure, colloidal osmotic\n"},{"page":7,"text":"7\npressure, and I don't know this pressure, that pressure. They are all the same. You must differentiate.\nThat is what you can understand if you know what is the pressure. Pressure is actually the weight of\nsomething on the surface.\nFor example, let me take this one for me. It has a weight. It has a weight, yeah? If I put it on the table,\nthe weight of this on this surface area where it is on it, that is the pressure. It means this produce a\npressure on the surface. And now what is air pressure? If you look, we have air from here is starting. If\nyou go up and up and up and at sometimes you are in a space. In a space, there is no air.\nYeah, empty air. At some point there, the air started. Probably by 30 km and so on. we start to have air.\nAnd now from there, let's say the air starts here and there is a space. And now from here until the\nsurface of the earth, we have air. And this column of the air has a weight because of the gravity of the\nair. And the weight of this air column on the surface of the air is the pressure of the air.\nAnd that is what we call barometric pressure. Okay, barometric pressure. And then you see barometric\npressure if I have here the sea level, the column of the air is from here until there. It's a larger column,\nhas a higher weight, a higher pressure. If I take the mountain until here where my second hand is,\nwhere this Mount Everest, 8,848 m high, then you see the column of the air on the top of the Mount\nEverest is shorter. Therefore, the weight of the column of the air is less.\nTherefore, the barometric pressure is less at the top of Mount Everest. It is the column of the air weight\nproduces the pressure. So, it is the barometric pressure. Now, we know the air has a pressure,\nbarometric pressure. Now, what is oxygen pressure? What is nitrogen pressure? You know that the air\nis 21% of the molecules of the air are oxygen, 79% almost nitrogen, only 1% or other gases. So, if I\nhave a certain pressure, let's say of 760 mm of mercury, then the oxygen pressure in this air is\nproportional to its concentration.\nConcentration was 21%. Then the pressure is 21% of the total pressure. Total is 760. 21% is almost\n150. Therefore, you see that I start here with 150 mm of mercury. Yeah? And I then transported in the\nbody, it becomes less and less and less. And this 20% or 21% either you use the term percent or you\nuse the term fraction. Fraction is really related to one. Percent is related to 100. It means 21% is the\nfraction 0.21. Yeah, 0.21 is 21%.\n0.3 is how many percent? 30%. And so, either you use the fraction or percentage to make it\nunderstandable for you. So, now we know that oxygen transported there to the cells, its partial pressure\ndecrease. As we call it partial pressure because that is a part of the total pressure, partial pressure of\noxygen. And now, how does oxygen, for example, is transported here? From outside here into the lung,\nit is transported by ventilation. Ventilation is the process which transport the gases from outside into the\nlung or CO2 from the lung to the outside.\nActually, that is the breathing. We call it ventilation. Ventilation is a fast transport system, a transport\nsystem which is very fast. And then, after the oxygen is in this alveoli, then the oxygen must enter the\nblood capillaries. And you know that the blood capillary is separated from alveolar gas by a very thin\nmembrane, which we call it alveolo-capillary membrane. Alveolo from alveolus, capillary from capillar.\nalveolo-capillary membrane. This movement through this membrane is not convection. That is\ndiffusion. But diffusion is very slow.\nNot fast like convection, like ventilation. It is very slow. It's diffusion is like walking like this, ventilation is\nlike driving a car. Very fast. Diffusion is very slow process. So, through diffusion oxygen or CO2 are\n"},{"page":8,"text":"8\ntransported through this alveolo-capillary membrane. And now the gas is in the blood. And now the\noxygen will be transported with the blood to the cells. And this is circulation. You have learned\ncirculation. And again, circulation is a convective transport. Convective it means it is a very fast\ntransport.\nAnd now with circulation it reaches the systemic capillaries. And then from the blood capillaries it must\nmove through the cell. And again through these membranes oxygen is transported by diffusion. Again\ndiffusion. And also it diffuses through the cytoplasm of the cell, diffuses through the mitochondrial\nmembrane. And then where it is used. You see here we have ventilation, diffusion, circulation,\ndiffusion. These are the transport which are in series. But they are no parallel transport. It is in series.\nWhat does it mean?\nIt means if I interrupt the transport of oxygen through alveolar capillary membrane, downstream there I\nwould have no oxygen. There is not a parallel pathway to transport oxygen. One is after the other. If I\ninterrupt the transport in one place, then I cannot transport downwards. Okay? Therefore it is a in\nseries transport are connected in series. Okay. And how these gases are transported through these\nmechanisms that is the topic of the six lectures that I've the my last six lectures. Not the beginning.\nThe last six lectures. And before I go and start with the transport, I spend a lot of time over the\nmechanical properties of the respiratory system. Why I do that? Because most of the pulmonary\ndiseases was caused by the mechanical properties of the pulmonary system. If we don't know the\nmechanical properties of the system, we cannot understand the diseases. And you want to be\nphysicians, therefore that is very important. Although my first six or seven lectures are about the\nmechanical properties of the respiratory system. Okay? And I start now with those mechanical\nproperties.\n"},{"page":9,"text":"9\nFirst, I start with the alveolocapillary membrane. Okay? That oxygen must diffuse through this\nmembrane. And now we look at this membrane. Here is the alveoli that is filled with the gas. And here\nthese are which are looking like sausage, they are the capillaries behind that membrane. And you see\nhere are the erythrocytes within that capillary. The alveolocapillary membrane is this very thin\nmembrane that you see here. That is the membrane. And through this membrane, the gases must\ndiffuse. And now we want to analyze what kind of membrane to satisfy the demand.\nTherefore, first we have to know what is the demand here. The demand is to transport oxygen and CO2\nthrough this membrane. How much oxygen and CO2 will be transported per day there? We use 300 ml\noxygen per minute and we probably use almost 300 ml carbon dioxide per minute. Also, both together\n600 ml gas in a minute at least should go through this membrane. If you calculate it times 60, it makes\n36 L per hour. 36 L per hour. If you multiply it times 24, then make 865 L per day.\nJust roughly say 1,000 L gas per day. Normally, if you are sitting there, must be able to move through\nthis membrane 1,000 L gas. And that is in resting condition. If you work, the transport rate must\nincrease tenfold. 10 time increase. 10 times 1,000 makes 10,000 L gas. 10,000 L gas must diffuse\nthrough this membrane. 10,000 L per day. Imagine how big how much this is. So, and now what should\nbe the property of this membrane to be able to do it?\nFirst thing is that this membrane should be very thin. Because if this is thick, then the diffusion is very\nslow. Then it will take much more time. It will not be possible. It will be extremely thin. On the other\nhand, if you make it very thin, extremely thin, then will break. If it breaks, then the blood and gas will\nmix together. And we will die. So, the thickness of this membrane is compromised by two things. The\ndiffusion tells this membrane to be thin as possible.\nAnd the mechanical stability tells this membrane, \"Please be a little bit thick so that you don't break.\"\nAnd the compromise of these two different tendencies is that this membrane in average has a\nthickness of 1 micrometer. 1 micrometer is thick enough for mechanical stability and thin enough for\n"},{"page":10,"text":"10\ndiffusion. Okay? On the other hand, this membrane, because we have 10,000 L that must go through\nthis membrane in a day, this membrane must have a very huge surface. The surface must be very\nlarge.\nSo, because there is too much gas to go through. And this surface is around 90 square meter. Can you\nthink how large is 90 square meter? That is a tennis court. A tennis court, the size of a tennis court is\nthe alveolar membrane, alveolar capillary membrane. And now the problem happens. Where in the\nbody I can bring a tennis court inside my body? Tennis court is very large. Outside it cannot be\nbecause it is a thin membrane and if it is touched, it breaks.\nIt must be inside the body. Where can I bring a 90 square meter surface in the body? That is in thoracic\ncavity. Then you ask yourself how it fits in thoracic cavity, such a large membrane in such a small\nthoracic cavity. That is the law of physics. The law of physics says that if you divide a surface into very\nsmall vesicles, the volume decreases dramatically. Therefore, this membrane is divided into alveoli. Do\nyou know how many alveoli we have? 300 millions of alveoli.\n300 millions. They are very small vesicles. And now because it is divided into 300 million alveoli, the\nvolume is so much reduced that you can put the surface into the thoracic cavity. So, this problem\nsolved. But then we have another problem. Every one of this alveoli, 300 millions, must be connected\nto the air because the air must go through these alveoli. Therefore, all of them must be connected to\nthe air. How can I do? I put the alveoli here and make a hole to every alveoli, then my body will have\n300 million holes over the chest wall.\nReally, by mammals, by us, it is not the case. By worms, it is the case. Some worms have holes which\nconnect the air with their air things, but in our body is not. And how is in our body? Each alveolus all\nthese 300 million are connected to the outside. That is another solution that you know from the tree.\nTree has so many leaves. And every leaf is connected to the root to the stem. How the tree that did it?\nWe have one stem and then it divides into two.\nWe call it division one. And each one of those divided into two others, that is the second division. And\neach one of those divided into two others, that is the third division. And so in our body, this airways\nfrom trachea. Trachea is the division zero. From trachea, trachea divided to two main bronchi,\neveryone of them to two. And they divide into 23 generation. 23 times. One is divided into two. Also 23\ndivision. At the end of this division sits an alveolus.\nAnd that alveolus is like a leaf. Then has connection all of these divisions are connected to each other,\nopen connection. And finally all of those alveoli are connected to the outside through our mouth. So\nthat is exactly therefore we call it bronchial tree. Yeah? 23 division of branching. Okay.\n"},{"page":11,"text":"11\nWhat you see here is the alveolus. The ductus alveolaris that you look in. This is an alveolus, that is an\nalveolus, here is an alveolus, and so on.\n"},{"page":12,"text":"12\nAnd here you see the trachea divide into these two, and this one divides into two others. You see they\ndivide and divide and divide in 23 times, and at the end of those divisions are these little alveoli sitting\nthere. And it is not only the bronchial tree which divides. Also the blood vessels go with the airways\ntogether. The blood vessels divide and divide and divide and divide, and at the end you have capillary\nwhich surround the alveolus. Okay, you see here on this side only the airways, on this side you see the\nblood vessels which are dividing.\n"},{"page":13,"text":"13\nAnd you see here that is the airway, that is the blood vessel, venous and arterial. And you see they\nhave a very tight neighborhood. They are just following each other. So, and here at the end you have\nthese alveoli. This is one, this is one, and so on.\n"},{"page":14,"text":"14\nAnd now in a picture I can show you here is the trachea. You see that it is in the division zero. And then\nthis two is the division one. And then you have lobar bronchus, that is the division the second division.\nAnd segmental bronchus is the third division, and so on. And you see 23 divisions. And now Alveoli\nappear up division 16. Only in this area. From 17 to 23. In this area, you have alveoli. And where you\nhave alveoli, you have gas exchange.\nThe gas exchange take place in alveoli between blood and gas. Therefore, we call it this area, we call it\nrespiratory zone. Or alveolar region. Both are the same. Respiratory zone or alveolar region. That is\nthe division from division 17 to 23. These divisions from zero to 16, they do not contribute to gas\nexchange. And we call them anatomical dead air space. Why dead air space? We call it dead air space\nbecause it is dead for the gas exchange. There is no gas exchange there.\nTherefore, it is dead for the gas exchange. And we call it anatomical dead air space because anatomy\ndescribes everything related to structure. Until this division, here to respiratory bronchiole, there is no\ngas exchange for them. And therefore, they belong to dead air space. We call it also conducting zone.\nWhat does it mean conducting? It means that that is a floor for air to walk through until it reaches\nalveoli. If you reach this classroom, you must come from the floor. Walk through the floor.\nThat is the floor for the alveoli, practically. Okay. That is the definition of anatomists the dead air space.\nAnd how do we physiologists define this? Physiology defines everything related to function. Not only\nthe structure, but the function. If you want to describe this functionally, what we do? Let's assume I\nhave these two alveoli and the inlet to these alveoli, these two is blocked. And the gas cannot move\ninto these two alveoli. If the gas cannot move into, then it cannot participate in gas exchange.\nThen functionally, it is like dead space. It is alveoli but with no gas exchange, therefore functionally\ndead space. Therefore, I add these two alveoli to the anatomical dead space and call it physiological\ndead space. Okay? Physiological dead space is anatomical dead space plus alveoli which are not\nventilated, which has no gas exchange. And now tell me, can physiological dead space be smaller than\n"},{"page":15,"text":"15\nanatomical dead space? No, impossible. Physiological dead space is either bigger or equal to\nanatomical dead space. But it is never smaller than anatomical dead space.\nGot it? Okay. And now let's say how much gas we have in these two areas. The volume of the gas that\nwe have through these 16 branches, from here to here, that volume is about 150 ml. 150 ml altogether.\nAnd the volume of the gas or the air which is in these last seven generation or last seven division,\nnormally by us as we are sitting like this is about 3 L 3,000 mL. Look, all the air from here to here is 150\nmL.\nAnd the air here is 3,000 mL. It means the most of the air is the alveolar region. And this trachea\nbronchus and so on, they are as a whole they have a small diameter, they cannot contain so much air.\nSo, if I breathe, let's say I want to increase the lung volume, I do like this. Inhale, you see that\neverything increases. Where this air is going? Then, I will tell you that the volume of the dead space\ndoesn't change. It remains 150.\nYou cannot increase it. If I inhale 1 L gas, the volume of which region increases by 1 L? Alveolar\nvolume will increase by 1 L. Because at the end the whole gas is moved into the alveola. And if I exhale\ngas from the lung, the volume of dead space doesn't change. The volume change I see in the alveolar\nregion that is reduces. It means during breathing in and out, the alveolar volume decreases and\nincreases, not the dead space volume. Dead space volume remains constant, but this gas which\nincreases the alveolar volume or alveolar region lung volume must move through the dead space, but\ndoesn't increase the dead space volume.\nDead space volume is constant. Here, the lung volume or the volume of alveolar region is variable\ndepending on breathing. Okay? It can be 3,000 mL normal. If I maximal exhale, you can do it by\nyourself, this alveolar region doesn't become empty. The volume reduces only to 1 1/2 L. Yeah? The\nrest 1 1/2 we cannot exhale, and that is called residual volume. If I inhale too much air, maximally, then\nthis volume can increase to 6 L. From 3 to 6 L.\nIt means the volume here changes between 1 1/2 L and 6 L depending how much do I inhale or exhale.\nDid you get that? The variable volume is the volume in alveolar region, and can change between 1 1/2\nL and 6 L depending on breathing. So, and now we want to see something else. I have here dead\nspace volume 150 ml. Alveolar gas here 3,000 ml in this region. Yeah, 3,000 ml or 3 L. The volume\nwent from here to here, 150 ml.\nAnd I can say that this situation at the end of a normal exhalation. At the end of a normal exhalation,\nthe gas which you have in the mouth is actually the gas which was in the alveolar region. Now, you\nexhaled it and now it is in your mouth. And the gas in alveolar region, we call it used gas. Because this\ngas which is coming from alveolar region has less oxygen, more CO2. Oxygen concentration of that\ngas is 14% and CO2 is 7%.\nIt has less oxygen, more CO2 compared to the fresh air. Fresh air has 21% oxygen and 0% CO2. And\nnow you see if I take a breath, let's say normally a breath the size of a breath is 500 ml. That is what we\ncall tidal volume. Let's say here I take a breath. I don't put here 500 but 450 because I can show you\nwith this better. 450 ml. If I inhale it, it means I push it into the trachea here.\nThis gas which is in the dead air space, that will be first pushed into the alveolar region. And then at the\nend of inhalation, all this 450 is pushed here through. And then what you see here, first this volume\nmoved into the alveoli, that is this one. And then 300 ml of fresh gas moved into the alveoli, and 150 ml\n"},{"page":16,"text":"16\nof fresh gas remains in trachea in the dead air space. Yeah? And you see what happened. The dead\nair space volume doesn't change at all.\nThe lung volume increased by 450 ml, which was this volume that we inhaled. But from 450 ml there,\n150 ml is used gas, which was already in the dead air space, and 300 ml fresh gas entering the\nalveolar region. And this fresh gas, which enters the alveolar region, we call it alveolar volume. That is\nthis 300 ml defined as alveolar volume, and that is from tidal volume, you have 450 ml, 2/3 reaches the\nalveolar region, 1/3 remain in the dead air space.\nIt means that the dead air space at the end of exhalation has used air, at the end of inhalation has fresh\nair. Okay. This 2/3 in the alveolar region, 1/3 in the dead space is only by a normal VT of 500 ml. If you\ninhale more than that, let's 1 L, then 150 remains in dead space, 850 ml moves into the alveolar region.\nSo, that 850 ml is not 2/3 of 1,000. It is much more than 2/3. This 2/3 and 1/3, 1/3 dead space, 2/3\nalveolar region is given only by normal tidal volume. Okay.\n"},{"page":17,"text":"17\nAnd now, these are the gas exchange region. That is what I told you already. I don't need to read it.\nYou read it yourself. And understand it.\n"},{"page":18,"text":"18\nAnd the question is now, what is the function of this dead space? It has at least three functions.\nCleaning of the air, warming of the air to the body temperature, and clean the air from germs, from\nmicrobes, and bacteria, and so on. And how does it work here? You see here we have the cells which\nare producing mucus, or we have the glands which produce mucus. The mucus comes on the surface\nand make the surface a sticky. After we inhale the air, air had dust and so on stick with mucus, and\nmucus is transported by this mucociliary movement toward our throat, and then we swallow it down into\nthe stomach.\nSo, that this material doesn't arrive in the alveolar region. Even it is a very good system, they have also\nimmunoglobulins or the antibodies which fight against bacteria and other things. But by the time even if\na tiny fraction of dust and everything reaches the alveolar region and remains over there, then the\nmembrane becomes thicker and it is not good for diffusion. Therefore, the alveolar region must be\ncleaned out every time.\n"},{"page":19,"text":"19\nAnd who cleans the alveolar region? This guy. That is a macrophage. Macrophage, I call it the walking\ncleaner of alveolar region. That is like a walking cleaner, goes over the alveolar membrane, sucks\neverything which is there, and will be transported out of the lungs. So, that is a cleaner of the alveolar\nregion.\n"},{"page":20,"text":"20\nThese are the function of the dead space that I told you. You can read it.\n"},{"page":21,"text":"21\nAnd they are the keynotes.\n"}],"url":"/cvp-pulmonary-reader.html?source=cvp-pulm-t-p16"},{"id":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","displayName":"P17. Lung Volumes Transcript by Binish.pdf","name":"P17. Lung Volumes Transcript by Binish.pdf","kind":"Supplied lecture transcript","unit":"page","lectureIds":["CVP-P17"],"pages":[{"page":1,"text":"P17. Lung Volumes \n    red = the doctor said it is important  |  green = meh, not needed or just for understanding \n \nSLIDE 18: LUNG VOLUMES \n \n• After a maximal exhalation we still cannot get the lung empty. 1.5 L remains in the lung and that \nvolume is called the residual volume \n• Lung volume = how much gas we have in the lung. It depends on the phase of breathing: the \nvolume after maximal exhalation is different from after normal exhalation, after a tidal breath and \nafter maximal inspiration \n• The lung volume changes between 1.5 L and 6 L \n• We define these volumes to bring some structure into it, and to be able to measure them when they \nchange, for example in disease \n• We learned yesterday that when lung volume increases or decreases, it is the volume of the \nrespiratory zone (alveolar region) that changes. The dead space volume is very small (about 150 \nmL) and stays constant \n• Residual volume (RV) = the volume at the end of a maximal exhalation. It cannot be expired any \nmore \n• After a normal exhalation the volume is larger than that. The difference between normal and \nmaximal exhalation is the expiratory reserve volume (ERV). Reserve because if we need to, we can \nexhale it \n• At the end of a normal exhalation we inhale normally. Yesterday's example was 450 mL but \nnormally it is half a litre. This is the tidal volume (VT) \n• We can still inhale much more with maximal inspiratory force. The difference between a maximal \nand a normal inspiration is 2.5 L, the inspiratory reserve volume (IRV). Reserve because normally \nwe breathe in to here, but if we need more air we can inhale more \n• So there are 4 lung volumes: RV, ERV, VT, IRV \n"},{"page":2,"text":"• ERV, VT and IRV can be mobilised by breathing (I can exhale and inhale them). RV cannot, \nbecause we cannot exhale it \n• Can we measure them? The 3 we can mobilise are simple to measure: exhale into a bag and \nmeasure how much. RV cannot be done that way, it needs a separate method \n• We measure the 3 first. A simple classic method is the spirometer "},{"page":3,"text":"SLIDE 19: SPIROMETER \n \n• A spirometer is just a chamber containing air (yellow, top) with water at the bottom. The water has \nno function except letting the top of the device move in and out without producing pressure (things \nfloat in water) \n• We fill it with air and connect the person, with the nose closed so he cannot breathe to the outside. \nHis lung is directly connected to the spirometer gas, so the lung and the spirometer make a closed \nsystem. Nothing comes in or goes out \n• When he inhales, lung volume increases and spirometer volume decreases. The increase in lung \nvolume equals the decrease in spirometer volume, so we can measure the lung volume change \nindirectly from the spirometer \n• The spirometer is connected by a rope to a pen writing on paper. Inhale: the top of the spirometer \nmoves down, the rope is pulled up, the pen goes up, so inspiration is written upwards. Exhale: air \nmoves into the spirometer, the top goes up, the rope comes down, the pen writes downwards \n• Normal breathing: in and out between the end of normal inspiration and the end of normal expiration \n= tidal volume \n• Ask him to exhale maximally: the difference between maximal and normal exhalation = ERV. Ask \nhim to inhale maximally: the difference between maximal and normal inspiration = IRV \n• We have measured VT, ERV and IRV. We could not measure RV, because it cannot be exhaled, so \nthat volume is missing \n"},{"page":4,"text":"SLIDE 20: MEASURING RESIDUAL VOLUME (HELIUM DILUTION) \n \n• Now we want to measure the missing volume, the residual volume \n• Analogy: 2 glasses of water, one drop of blue ink in each. The bigger glass is paler. From the \nintensity of the colour you can work out the volume: if one glass has double the volume, its \nconcentration is half. That is the dilution method \n• In the lung we use a gas that is not used and not produced in the body. If the volume is large the \ngas is diluted more, if small, less. This is the helium dilution method \n• Same spirometer but with a valve. We close it so the person cannot breathe in and out of the \nspirometer. The nose is closed in the figure but while setting up the nose must be open, otherwise \nhe cannot breathe and will die. Imagine the picture without the nose clip \n• Fill the spirometer with a certain volume of air, e.g. 5 L (Vs), and inject a little helium. It spreads \nthrough the spirometer volume. The valve is closed so it cannot enter the lung \n• Take a sample and measure the helium concentration. For gas mixtures we use the fraction (O2 \nwas 0.21). F0 = 0.1, so 10% helium and 90% air \n• We want the amount of helium. Concentration = amount / volume, so amount = concentration × \nvolume. Remember this, we need it all the time in physiology \n• The volume that contains helium is only the spirometer volume (no helium in the lung yet). Amount \nbefore mixing = F0 × Vs \n• Now close the nose and open the valve. He breathes from the spirometer and the helium spreads \nover the lung volume too, so the concentration falls. F1 = 0.08: from 10% to 8% \n• Amount after mixing = F1 × (Vs + VL). The volume is now spirometer plus lung \n• Did the amount of helium change? No. The concentration and the volume changed but the amount \nstays the same, because the helium is not used or produced in the body. 1 g before, 1 g after \n• So put an equal sign: F0 × Vs = F1 × (Vs + VL). F0, Vs and F1 are known, only VL is unknown, so \nwe can solve for it \n"},{"page":5,"text":"WHICH LUNG VOLUME IS VL? \n• It depends on when you open the valve. VL is whatever is in the lung at that moment \n• At the end of a maximal exhalation = RV \n• At the end of a normal exhalation = RV + ERV = FRC \n• At the end of a normal inhalation = RV + ERV + VT \n• At the end of a maximal inhalation = TLC (all of them together) \n• To measure RV: open the nose, tell him to exhale maximally, then close the nose and open the \nvalve \nTHE MISTAKE \n• If he does not exhale maximally, the lung volume is larger than RV and you think it is RV. It is an \neasy mistake to make \n• His example: he did not exhale fully, you measure 3 L instead of 1.5 L, think \"huge RV, probably \nemphysema\" and give medication. A month later you repeat it correctly, get 1.5 L and say \"one \nmonth of therapy and you went from emphysema to healthy\". But emphysema is not reversible, so \nthe first measurement was just wrong \n• Point: it is a simple procedure but mistakes have real consequences for the patient. As a physician \nyou have a lot of accountability, so be serious about these measurements \n• Student question: how do you know the lung volume if you start with 5 L? Answer: the 5 L is only \nthe spirometer. When the valve opens, the only thing added is the lung volume, nothing else "},{"page":6,"text":"SLIDE 21: LUNG CAPACITIES \n \n \n• We have 4 volumes and also 4 capacities. Capacity = sum of 2 or more lung volumes. If you hear \ncapacity, think at least 2 volumes \n• RV + ERV = functional residual capacity (FRC), about 3 L. The volume in the lung at the end of a \nnormal exhalation \n• VT + IRV = inspiratory capacity (IC): 0.5 + 2.5 = 3 L \n• ERV + VT + IRV = vital capacity (VC). The maximal volume you can mobilise \n• All 4 volumes together = total lung capacity (TLC) \n• VC is the maximum possible tidal volume: breathe out maximally and then in maximally. ERV and \nIRV are reserves for the tidal volume, so VT can grow at their cost until both become zero and VT = \nVC. That is why they are called reserve volumes \n \nIS THE VOLUME WE CANNOT EXHALE A GOOD THING? \n• Yes, it is positive. Reasons: \n– Surface tension: if the lung collapsed it would be very hard to reinflate, like blowing up a \nballoon that is completely empty (hard at the start, easier after a bit of air). It would need much \nmore muscle force, which is a waste of energy. It is more economical not to empty the lung \n– Speech and thinking would be interrupted every time the lung was empty \n– Gas exchange stays continuous. If the lung were empty there would be no exchange of O2 and \nCO2 between gas and blood at that moment. The gas that stays guarantees continuous \nexchange \n– Alveolar gas stays stable. The FRC (3 L) is \"used\" air with 14% O2 and 7% CO2, while fresh \ngas has 21% O2 and 0% CO2. If we emptied the lung each time, the concentrations would \nswing dramatically and these changes would also show in the blood. The receptors that \nmeasure PO2 and PCO2 and adjust breathing would \"go crazy\" \n"},{"page":7,"text":"– Instead we add about 300 mL of fresh gas (2/3 of a 450 mL breath) to 3 L, so O2 only goes \nfrom about 14% to 14.2 to 14.5%. PO2 and PCO2 of the alveolar gas stay virtually constant, \nwith only small changes during breathing "},{"page":8,"text":"SLIDE 22: EFFECTS OF AGEING ON LUNG VOLUMES \n \n• These volumes depend on many things: gender, size, physical training and so on. So compare the \nmeasured volumes with the average of a group of people \n• Ageing: after birth the lung volume (TLC) is very small. It increases sharply up to age 20, then only \na little. RV also increases after 20, slowly, but RV increases sharply after 60 (he is 75, so for him it \ngoes sharply up) \n• RV is the volume I cannot mobilise. The volume I can mobilise is the difference between TLC and \nRV, which is the vital capacity \n• As we age, the volume we can use for breathing (VC) gets smaller and the volume that does not \nhelp (RV) gets bigger \n• This is a typical sign of emphysema: RV increases, TLC stays about the same and VC decreases. \nSo elderly people are functionally like a person with a little emphysema \n"},{"page":9,"text":"SLIDE 23: IDEAL GAS LAW \n \n• The volume of a gas depends on temperature and pressure: high pressure and low temperature \nmake it shrink, low pressure and high temperature make it expand \n• We measure lung volumes indirectly from the gas that comes out. We are really interested in how \nbig the \"room\" in the lung is, not the air, but we cannot go into the lung and measure it, so we use \nthe gas volume \n• The spirometer is at room temperature (about 20 °C) and the body is 37 °C, so the gas volume in \nthe spirometer is smaller than the real lung volume. We must correct volumes to the body \nconditions \n• By the ideal gas law there are 3 conditions: BTPS (body: temperature, pressure, saturated with \nwater vapour), ATPS (ambient, the spirometer) and STPD (standard). We measure under ATPS \nand convert to BTPS. Converting ATPS to BTPS increases the volume by about 10% \n"},{"page":10,"text":"SLIDE 24: CONVERSION OF VOLUMES \n \n• Conversion factors come from the ideal gas law. I could do it all for you but it is not necessary \n• He said you can forget these, do not memorise them or learn them from the slides (not tested) \n• Why: devices today either measure at the correct body temperature or have software that makes \nthe correction, so they give you the BTPS volume directly. It matters only if you are sent to a rural \narea with no modern device and a normal spirometer. Then you must remember that the measured \nvolume is not the volume in the lung and convert it to BTPS. When he became a physician every \nvolume had to be converted with these equations, now the device does it \n"},{"page":11,"text":"SLIDE 25: CAUSES OF REDUCED VITAL CAPACITY \n \n• There are a lot of diseases that reduce the vital capacity (the volume you can mobilise): skeletal \nabnormality, weak respiratory muscles, lung diseases and airway obstruction \n• Point: measuring lung volumes is important because they are affected by diseases of the airways, \nlung, muscle and skeleton \n• Do not memorise these diseases and you will not be asked about them. You learn them in \npathology later. They are just examples \n"},{"page":12,"text":"SLIDE 26: CHANGES IN LUNG VOLUMES IN RESPIRATORY DISEASES \n \n• This is what you have to know and never forget: from a functional point of view respiratory diseases \nare divided into 2 categories, obstructive and restrictive \n• Obstructive (e.g. emphysema): as said before, RV increases very much. Because RV is part of \nTLC, TLC increases a little. So the ratio RV / TLC is high. You could see the same on the ageing \ncurve above 60 \n• Restrictive: all lung volumes decrease, but proportionately. If one volume decreases by 20%, the \nother decreases by 20% too, so RV / TLC stays normal \n• From this ratio alone you can differentiate an obstructive from a restrictive disease \n"}],"url":"/cvp-pulmonary-reader.html?source=cvp-pulm-t-p17"},{"id":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","displayName":"P20. Airway Resistance by Tanisha Syed.pdf","name":"P20. Airway Resistance by Tanisha Syed.pdf","kind":"Supplied lecture transcript","unit":"page","lectureIds":["CVP-P20","CVP-P21"],"pages":[{"page":1,"text":"V. Airflow and ResistanceLearning objectives:P15.Understand the factors that determine airflow through airways P16.Describe how lung volume, autonomic innervations,respiratory gas partial pressures and humoral agentsaffect airway resistance.P17.Describe the distribution of the resistance related to branching division of airways H. Shams 1TRANSCRIBED BY : TANISHA SYED REVIEWED BY: HAJRA ZEESHANRespiratory zone to alveolar region. Now we are dealing with airflow and airway resistance. Now we are in, let's say, dead space because the resistance is produced through the airways. The airways are the first, the upper divisions until division 16. The resistance is mostly localized there and therefore we are dealing with that part of the respiratory system.Okay but before I start with this, I want to remind you of something. These are the learning objectives: \n1"},{"page":2,"text":"H. Shams 1\nSLIDE NOT PRESENT IN THE PDF FROM DOCTOR, BUT HE EXPLAINED IN CLASS. That you know from cardio physiology, you have learned, to remind you of something. These are the learning objectives for something that you know from cardio physiology. You have learned from Dr. Moni that if you have a vessel then the blood is going into the vessel only if you have a driving force for flow. The driving force is the pressure difference: pressure at the beginning of the vessel minus the pressure at the end of the vessel. That is the driving force, the pressure difference. It causes the blood to flow through this tube or vessel and so on. Another parameter that determines the flow as well is the resistance of the vessel, R. The flow through the vessel was equal to ΔP/R. That is what we have learned. If R was equal to these factors, 8, this one L, this one, and so on. 8 is, if you look at these parameters which determine the resistance, a constant. I cannot do something with the constant. It is always 8. This one is the viscosity of the liquid (n). By blood flow viscosity can be changed if the electrolyte is too much or too low and so on. L is the length of the vessel and this one, P , is again a number, 3.14 something. I cannot do much with it. Then we get R as the radius and it goes to the power of 4. Have you ever seen that the length of a vessel changes? No, it is almost constant. All these factors are virtually constant except 2"},{"page":3,"text":"viscosity can change a little bit. The only parameter which is important in the determination of resistance is the radius. That is because of two things: The first thing is the radius. The radius effect on resistance is huge. It goes to the power of 4. It means if you reduce the radius of a vessel by half, its resistance will increase 16-fold. It has a very strong effect on resistance: the changes in radius.It is so important because it is the only one that you can change in the body. You cannot change viscosity you cannot change the length and you cannot change the 8. The only parameter that you can change is the radius.Therefore it becomes important in the regulation of the resistance and in the regulation of blood flow and so on. Now that was a blood vessel. The same information you can apply to the airway. The airway is also a tube through which the air is flowing and that is the same thing. \n2"},{"page":4,"text":"H. ShamsAs you learned earlier, a parallel system of branching airways (including all bronchi andbronchioles) that connects the atmosphere with the alveolar space is called conductingairways. During inspiration, the air flows through the airways from the atmosphere into thealveoli and moves in the opposite direction during expiration. Air volume passing through theairways in a unit of time is called air flow(V). Driving force producing air flow is the pressuredifference between both ends of the airways. For expiration, this pressure difference wouldbe: Alveolar pressure(PA)minus pressure at airway opening(Pao). Airway resistance(Raw)can be then obtained due to Hagen-Poiseuille law as shown above.Therefore, PA, Pao and airflow should be measured to use for the calculation of airwayresistance. Pao is zero (atmospheric pressure), and airway flow can easily be measuredwith a pneumo-tachometer. A direct PA measurement is nearly impossible, because alveolarspace is not easily accessible for applying noninvasive methods. However, techniques havebeen developed for indirect measurement of PA such as interrupter method or the whole-body plethysmography.Changes in airway resistance induced by natural stimuli occur by changes in airway radius.The principal changes are achieved either actively by alterations in the contraction of theairway smooth muscle cells, or passively by changes in transmural pressure of airways.Recall from geometry that cross sectional area of a tube is equal to πr2, so changes inradius have magnified effects.2Airway resistance•VAirwaysAPaoAirway resistance:PA-Pao8 ꞏ ηꞏ LRaw ==Vπꞏ r4Alveolar spaceAir Flow(Pressure at air way opening)(= Mouth pressure)PI. Airway ResistanceThe driving force I showed you now. Here we have the lung: that is the alveolar region and here is the outside. You have learned that in the alveolar region, outside, you have the airways, the 16 upper divisions of the airways. Do you remember from the first lecture? They were also in blue.Now at one end of these airways, we have the alveolar region. On the other hand,we have the outside. It means, for example, if I take the exhalation, during exhalation the pressure difference which drives the air out will be the alveolar pressure (because that is the beginning of the airway during exhalation) minus outside pressure, which is outside. We saw yesterday also that during exhalation alveolar pressure becomes +2 and outside was 0. Therefore, the air was coming.My inhalation is the opposite: the alveolar region is the second or the end of the airways and outside is the beginning of the airways. Exactly by results here you can also say that resistance depends exactly on these factors that you know from circulation. There is no change. Also, the most influential parameter is the radius of the arteries because we can change the radius of the arteries and modify the resistance. Also, it goes to the power of 4. It means it is very powerful in the determination of the resistance.3"},{"page":5,"text":"Okay? Now you will tell me that I did lie to you some time particularly in the first lecture. Do you remember I told you that the dead space volume is always constant? If it is always constant, how can I change the radius? If I increase the radius then the volume of the dead space must increase. If I reduce the volume,then the dead space must decrease. So that doesn't work that way.The point is you see that radius affects resistance to the power of 4. It means if I make a very very small change in diameter, I affect resistance, but I don't affect the volume so much because it is a very very small change. This very small change probably changes my maximal dead space volume from 150 to 130, probably by a 20 mm difference. If I increase it by 20 mm more, that is in terms of volume. These volumes are 4 L, 5 L in the lungs. It is a small change but in terms of resistance it is a huge change.Why? Because by this change, by a little reduction in airways, we get difficulty in breathing because the resistance goes up. That goes up because it is to the power of 4. The change in radius is very strongly influential on the resistance. That is what we have learned now. The resistance here will be determined by the radius.Now how can I change the radius? That is the question because during regulation I must do that. How can I change the radius? There are two different forces which can change the radius. Think about the radius: the area is the tube. If you want to increase the radius of the tube, one force that you can apply is the force that you learned already from me. Do you remember what I told you? If I want to open the door, I push it outward or if I want to inflate the balloon, what was required there to inflate the balloon or open the door? Transpleural pressure difference, a pressure difference over the wall.Exactly, here is the same thing. If I want to increase this diameter or this radius, then I must increase the inside pressure minus outside pressure, this pressure difference. There are two ways: either I increase the inside pressure, or I decrease the outside pressure. It is the same principle. That is what we call a passive force. It means I can't change the radius by changing the transpleuralpressure difference. That is a passive force: either changing inside pressure or changing outside pressure.Here there are other forces which we call active force. What do you think if you hear the name active? Muscle action. Here in the air ways, you have smooth muscle. If they contract, then this radius becomes narrower. It increases the resistance and that is an active force. It means by contraction of the bronchioles' 3"},{"page":6,"text":"smooth muscle I can also change the radius, by contraction or relaxation in both directions. With that I can change the resistance to the airflow.There are two types of forces: passive force changes with the transpleural pressure difference and active force is smooth muscle contraction of the airways. There are two ways to modify the airway resistance, okay? \n3"},{"page":7,"text":"H. ShamsThis slide shows that the volume of anatomic dead spsce (upper airways from airway opening until terminal bronchioles; upper 16 bronchinggenerations) is small about 150 ml, whereas most of the volume (2.8 L) resides in peripheral airways beyond bronching generation 16. Therefore, total diameter of airways is much larger and their resistance much lower in peripheral airways beyond generation 16. 3Branching of airways and the lung volume321002322Respiratory zone2120191817161514131221 0Dead space48 12 16 (mm)10 20 (cm)Distance from the end of air ways Lung volume ( l )Dead spaceNow we want to see the airway from the airway from the mouth until the alveolar region. I want to know at which location the airway is normally the highest and at which location across the airway the airway resistance is low. Also, I want to know where the resistance is high and where it is low across the airway.You already know if you look at this picture. We had it at the beginning: the trachea was division 0 and it divided into two main bronchi (division 1). It divided 23 times. Do you remember the first lecture? What did I tell you? Upper divisions 16 to 23, the last 7 divisions, had the alveolar region. How many liters did you have in the alveolar region? You had FRC, 3,000 mL, and in the upper 16 generations you had only 150 mL.If I look at this picture I would say from here to here is generation 16. You see here division 16. You have only 150 mL in the dead space. From here on in the last 7 generations, you have 2.8 L, also 3,000 mL. Most of the air is only in this red area.What do you think: is the total diameter in the red area big or small? It must be very big to occupy so much here. Therefore, if the diameter here is so big, then 4"},{"page":8,"text":"the resistance is extremely low. In this green area, the upper airways, you see it is only 250 mL of gas. It means the total diameter of these upper airways is not so big. Therefore, you suggest just from the distribution of volume, that the airway resistance must be mostly located in this upper area, not in the alveolar region, not close to the alveolar region, along from the volumes that you see here. \n4"},{"page":9,"text":"H. ShamsA disadvantage of this resistance distribution is called “silent lung disease”; i.e., even largeperipheral lung tumors do not constrict the large airway diameter of this region to a level thatcould produce obstructive symptoms or to be detected by measurement of Raw. On theother hand, small obstruction in upper airways produces a significant change in Raw.Airways branch like a tree in 23 generations, so the total number of airways goes from one to 223 or 8,388,608.The radius of individual bronchi becomes smaller from generation 1 to 23, but the totaldiameter of all bronchi within one generation decreases only in the first 4 generations. Thisleads to the increase of total resistance in the early four dividing generations.Thereafter total airway diameter increases rapidly from generation to generation towardperipheral airways. As a result, total airway resistance exhibits a sharp decrease towardperipheral airways. Only 20% of the total airway resistance is then localized in the peripheralairways beyond dividing generation 7, although very narrow airways are residingin this region of the lung (diameter below 2 mm).415 20 5 10Generation of airways branching, NResistance per generation N0 5106010020408000 15 20Diameter of Bronchibelow 2 mmASegmental bronchiTerminal bronchioli Resistance from mouth to GenerationNSegmental bronchiTerminal bronchioli Distribution of resestance along airwaysB(% of total)Conclusion:Themajorsite of resistance is inupper airways (80%). Thus, measurement of resistance is a poor test for detecting small airways obstructionA. Distribution of airway resistanceConsequently, the major site of airwayresistance is in upper airways, prior to division 7 (80%).If you look at this, here on this line, I plot the resistance of the arteries, divided by the branching division. It means if it is 5 here, it is the 5th division. Division 0 is the trachea.1 is the main bronchus. (by the way you have learned it in anatomy).2 is the lobar bronchi.3 is the segmental bronchi.That is, 5 means resistance in the 5th generation. 1 means resistance in the 1st generation. You see, from the trachea if you go to the main bronchi, the resistance increases. If you go to the lobar bronchi, the resistance increases a little bit. If you go to the 4th generation, the resistance increases a little bit. You see here, at the segmental bronchi, then if you go further, the resistance starts to decrease. After here, in this area, 10 or 15, you see that the resistance is extremely low. Resistance of the airways in that generation. It's very low. Here is the lowest resistance in these later generations. Can you understand it? Now from anatomy, you know the trachea is that big and if you take a bronchioles, that is very small. Why would the resistance in the bronchial area be low and in the trachea high? We imagine if it is very tiny, the radius is small, then there is resistance will be high.5"},{"page":10,"text":"Bronchioles have a much smaller radius, therefore the resistance must be higher. 9. The reason is that it is total resistance, not the resistance of one individual airway. You have one trachea. It is that big and the resistance is given by that. But you have 5 million or 10 million bronchi and bronchioles. If you add the radius of all 10 million together, then it is much, much bigger than the diameter of the trachea and therefore the resistance is lower. It is not the resistance of individual areas. It is the resistance of all areas in that division. Okay that would be 1 million, 2 million, 3 million. You have 300 million alveoli. That is why as we go to the peripheral part of the lung, the resistance of the area decreases. Even though every individual area becomes smaller, the number is growing. If you take the diameter of all of them together, then the diameter is much larger than the entire trachea. In this graph you see that the resistance is plotted against... You get the resistance from that generation. It means if it is here at 5, it means the resistance in division 5, only division 5. Here 5 means the resistance from division 0 to division 5. This means there is, essentially, division 0, division 1, division 2, division 3, division 4, division 5, starting from 0 until division 5. How much of the total resistance is located there? You see that until division 7 here, from 0, from the trachea until division 7, we have 80% of the total resistance. 80% of the total resistance is starting from the trachea to division 7. Below that, at 7, we have only 20% of the resistance. You see here that is division 7. The rest of the division, from here to there, to 100%, is 20%. A very small portion of the resistance is localized in the peripheral part. Most of the resistance is localized in the upper area, in the trachea, in the bronchus, and so on. Now it has consequences for us. The consequence is this: if the resistance is very low here in the upper part of the lung, mostly the diseases of the lung start there. For instance if you have a tumor, mostly the tumor is not sitting in the trachea. It is sitting in the alveolar region somewhere or in the bronchial respiratory area somewhere. Now this tumor, if it is in these areas, in the peripheral part of the lung, then it is growing and growing and so on. You don't feel anything because it doesn't disturb your brething. It is not increasing the resistance because here the total diameter is huge. If you bring a little ball here, this ball and this total diameter don't affect anything and you don't feel it. The tumor is growing and you feel healthy. Even if you go to a physician and the physician measures air way resistance, it doesn't find that the area resistance is increased because the diameter there is huge. The tumor doesn't reflect constriction of the areas. If you have a very small tumor in the trachea, suddenly you feel that you cannot breathe too easily. Also if you measure the airway resistance, suddenly you find that the airway resistance is increased. Do anything which plays in the upper airways, you feel it much faster than a process which plays in the peripheral part of the lung because there the resistance is extremely low. Did you get that? That is a problem that you have as a physician. 5"},{"page":11,"text":"You go to a physician very late, after the tumor is huge, for example. \n5"},{"page":12,"text":"H. ShamsThe lower the density and viscosityof the respiratory gas, the lower will be Raw. Thus, a helium-oxygen gas mixturehaving a low density would help divers at extreme depths and patients with extremeobstructive lung disease to breathe more comfortably.b) Reduction in PCO2:A reduction in airway PCO2 causes bronchoconstriction andthereby increases Raw. This effect plays a role in regional distribution of ventilation. Asa lung region becomes relatively hyperventilated and its PCO2 decreases, airwayresistance of that region increases to divert the ventilation to other hypoventilatedareas.c) Sympathetic innervations.Sympathetic innervations and epinephrine produce bronchodilation through stimulation of smooth muscle β- receptors.d) Agents:such as histamine, thromboxane A2 and prostaglandin F2 are broncho-constrictors.5Factors affecting airway resistancea) Density and viscosity of the inspired gas: b) Reduction in PCO2c) Sympathetic and parasympathetic innervation andtheir transmiters d)Agents: histamine, acetylcholine, thromboxane A2, prostaglandin F2, and leukotrienes LTB4, C4and D4e) Lung volumee) Lung volumeB. Five Factors influencing airway resistancea) Density and viscosity of the repiratory air:Factors affecting air resistanceNow we know about air resistance. What is this? It is mostly localized in the upper airways and not close to the alveolar region. Now we want to see which factors affect our airway resistance.One is the density and viscosity of the inspired gas. Actually, not too much needs to be known about that because the viscosity of the inspired gas doesn't change too much. We are breathing mostly air. It only changes if you go to high altitude for example.At high altitude as I told you, the barometric pressure decreases. The concentration of oxygen and other gases remains the same, but every molecule is apart from another one. The density and viscosity of air decrease. Therefore,air breathing itself is easier there, up the mountain. No, it is difficult because it has less oxygen pressure.In the past we used it in the clinic. In the clinic in the past, if people had emphysema and had difficulty breathing, then as a physician you wanted to 6"},{"page":13,"text":"make his breathing easier. You let him breathe not air. Air is, let's say, 79% nitrogen and 21% oxygen. You let him breathe 21% oxygen and 79% helium. You replace nitrogen with helium. It changes the viscosity. Oxygen remains the same because 21% oxygen must be kept. The replacement of nitrogen with helium makes the density and viscosity of the gas go down and it is easier for the person to ventilate. Today nobody does it. In the past some people did it in the hospital, these things.The other factor which affects breathing is sympathetic and parasympathetic innervation. Sympathetic innervation relaxes the airway smooth muscle and therefore you get a little bit of an increase in the radius. That decreases the resistance of the airways and breathing becomes easier. Whereas in parasympathetic activity, it does the opposite. It can contract the smooth muscle and therefore increases the resistance. In these other agents, histamine, acetylcholine, prostaglandin F2, all of them are making bronchoconstriction. Constriction means the radius becomes smaller, the resistance goes up, and breathing becomes more difficult. Okay all of them do the same thing.Another factor in determining air resistance is the lung volume. Now we want to study how lung volume affects air resistance. \n6"},{"page":14,"text":"H. Shams 6Recoil forceExpanding of airways by elastic recoil of the lung tissueLung tissueBlood vesselSmooth muscleBronchusWe have learned that we have the lung tissue, the alveolar region, and that region is always recoiling inwards. Do you remember the lung is always recoiling inwards?In those airways (it means bronchioles, bronchi, and all other airways are within the lung tissue), as the lung tissue recoil inwards, these airways are pulling outwards by the recoil force of the lung. They become a little bit larger and therefore the resistance decreases.Let's say these vessels in these airways remain open because of the recoil force of the lung, because the lung is recoiling in these directions. Now we want to see how increased lung volume will affect this. That is a picture that I drew in the morning. Explain it. 7"},{"page":15,"text":"H. Shams 7\nSLIDE NOT PRESENT IN THE PDF FROM DOCTOR, BUT HE EXPLAINED IN CLASS. See how increased lung volume will affect this. That is a picture I drew, by the way, to explain it in the morning. I don't know if it is done well.You see, here is an airway. This air way is, let's say, in the middle of these two alveoli. These alveoli recoil inward. What happens with this airway? It will be expanded outward. Therefore, it remains open. That is a normal case.Now I increase the lung volume. If I increase the lung volume, what do I get? I get this and now this lung becomes bigger because I increase the volume. Then you would say if I increase the volume this one in the middle should be compressed. Yeah, because the lung occupies that space and it will be compressed, the resistance should go up.That is the case if you increase the volume without increasing the total volume here, without increasing the volume of the thoracic cavity, and that is normally not the case. When do you increase the volume of the lung? When you inhale. How do you inhale? The diaphragm goes down and the chest wall goes up. It 8"},{"page":16,"text":"means that the increase in lung volume comes at the cost of increasing the total space. Therefore, this is not happening by increasing lung volume.This is what is happening: the total cavity was this size and now becomes much bigger because the diaphragm goes down and the chest wall goes up. Then this box becomes larger. In other words the lung becomes larger because of more volume.Now if you increase the lung volume, what happens to its recoil force? It increases. If you increase the lung volume, compliance decreases. Recoil force and compliance are opposites of each other.Now we increase this alveolar region. Its recoil force compared to this one is bigger. If this is bigger, with greater recoil force, then the expanding force for this area in the middle increases. The airway in the middle will be more extended outwards and then the diameter of the airway increases and the resistance decreases. The conclusion is that an increase in lung volume causes a decrease in airway resistance.That is what we see in this graph. No, from these two possibilities this is not happening (upper box) because we must increase the box. This is happening (below box) and therefore an increase in the volume of alveoli here causes more recoil force. The greater recoil force causes expansion of the airway, which is in the middle of the alveoli. The higher the lung volume the lower the resistance of the airway. \n8"},{"page":17,"text":"H. Shamsthe higher the lung volume, the lower will be Raw. Bronchi andbronchioles are residing within the lung tissue surrounded by alveoli. Their wall ispermanently pulled by the neighboring inward recoiling alveoli. Therefore, lung recoilis a distending pressure for middle and small airways keeping them open. Rememberthat lung recoil increases with lung volume, so that airway distending pressure, andthereby airway diameter becomes larger at higher lung volume. Consequently, Rawdrops as volume increases.7Airway resistance,Raw-1(cm HO ꞏ lꞏ s)2Residual volumeFRCTotal lung capacity4Lung volume 2.01.0002(l)6Raw = 1,3 cm H2O ꞏlꞏ s8Effect of lung volume on resistance-1Factors influencing airway resistancee) Lung volume:Now we have it in a graph. For example what are the lung volumes? That is residual volume: by the 0.5 L you cannot exhale. Then you have the FRC (functional residual capacity). Here it should it be total lung capacity.I plot the airway resistance against this lung volume and at the FRC I have this airway resistance. If I reduce lung volume, go in that direction, to the left, then the airway resistance increases. If I increase the lung volume the airway resistance decreases. It means that at a higher lung volume it is easier to breathe because the resistance is lower.Do you know what the patient does who has difficulty in breathing, for instance, an emphysematic patient? Those patients have high airway resistance and therefore they have problems with breathing.Now we want to see this patient and what he does. Normally what you do is this: where is the functional residual capacity? Here is the functional residual capacity. Normally from the functional residual capacity you inhale 0.5 L, exhale 0.5 L, inhale, exhale, inhale, exhale. Do you remember?9"},{"page":18,"text":"Now these patients don't do this. This patient inhales first a large volume and then it starts here: inhale, exhale, inhale, exhale, inhale, exhale, inhale, exhale. It means first the patient inhales a large volume and then starts to inhale and exhale. Why does he do it? Because after a large volume the lung volume is increased, air resistance is decreased, and then is he starts here to breathe it is easier for him to do it here.They don't know physiology but they do it because of their comfort. He does take a deep breath, a deep inhalation, a deep tidal volume. He breathes from that deep airway.Here is an example: a emphysema patient must have high air resistance by a disease to do that and most of them are emphysematic patients. \n9"},{"page":19,"text":"KEY CONCEPTS:1.In laminar flow as exists in small airways, the resistance is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the radius. 2.Both passive forces (transmuralpressure difference) and active forces (smooth muscle contraction) determine the diameter and therefore the resistance of airways 3.Lung airway resistance is reduced by increasing lung volume. If airway smooth muscle is contracted, as in asthma, the resistance is reduced by β2-adrenergic agonists. 4.The flow resistance is much larger in upper airways than in the lower airways.H. Shams 8That is the airway resistance and these are the key concepts for this lecture. \n10"},{"page":20,"text":"VI. Assessing Pulmonary FunctionLearning objectives:P18.Draw a spirogramresulting from a maximal expiratory effort. Label the forcedvital capacity (FVC), forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV1), and the maximal expiratory flow rate between 25-75% of FVC (FEF25-75%). P19.Differentiate between the two broad categories of restrictive and obstructive lung disease, including the spirometricabnormalities associated with each category.P20.Draw a normal maximal effort flow-volume curve, labeling the effort-dependent and independent regions. P21.Use the concept of dynamic compression of airways to explain why each point in the effort-independent region of the curve represents a maximal flow rate that is uniquely dependent on lung volume. Describe how and why the shape of the flow-volume curve is shifted in chronic obstructive lung disease (COPD) and in restrictive disease H. Shams 9Now we have assessing pulmonary function, which I start now with. These are the learning objectives for assessing pulmonary function.\n11"},{"page":21,"text":"H. Shams 10Forced exspiration64201 sTotalLung capacity (TLC)Residual volumenormal: 80 %of FVCOne second capacity (FEV1)ForcedVital capacity (FVC)TimeLung volume (l)Assessing pulmonary functionMost of respiratory diseases are associated with changes in Raw or CRS. Measuring Raw and CRWisnot so easy and cannot be performed in every physicians office. What matters for a physician is to beable to evaluate and observe changes in above parameters, but knowing their absolute values is notessential. Therefore, several functional tests (easy to apply) have been developed to serve thisgoal.These tests are introduced below:I. ForcedExpiratoryVitalCapacityTo measure forced vital capacity the patient is asked to take a maximum breath in, hold it for asecond, then exhale as hard and fast as he/she can. The most important data derived from thismaneuverare:1. ForcedExpiratoryVolume in1second(FEV1):It is the volume of air that is forcibly exhaled fromthelungsinthefirstsecondofaforcedexpiratorymaneuver.2. ForcedVitalCapacity(FVC):After a maximal inspiration, the volume of air that is forcibly andmaximally exhaled untilnomorecanbeexpired.3.RatioofFEV1/FVC which isnormally0.8(80%).4. PeakExpiratoryFlowRate(PEFR):The maximum flow rate achieved during the forced vital capacity maneuver beginning after full inspiration and starting and ending with maximal expiration.These spirometry variables will be discussed later to help understand normal lung physiology and to diagnosespecificlungpathology.We are certainly interested in a patient who has obstructive disease. We are interested in measuring airway resistance.Airway resistance, do you know, was ΔP pressure difference divided by R, and by R I mean ΔP/Q, by flow. Then you say it's really difficult for me to measure all these parameters and calculate the airway resistance. You say as a physician you want to do things very simply and get the same information. You say I'm not interested to know the absolute value of the airway resistance. I want to know if the airway resistance has been changed, if it is increased or decreased. I am not interested to know the exact value to calculate it from those parameters.I need a test to apply to the patient and see if this patient's airway resistance is higher than normal or lower than normal, but not the absolute value. For that purpose they developed tests and one of these tests is the forced expiration test.What do you do? You connect the patient with the spirometer. You let him normally breathe in. Now the spirometer is breathing in and out. You ask him, at some point, to inhale maximally. He inhales maximally at this point (I put on capacity) and then you tell him to hold it for a while. He holds it and then you say, 12"},{"page":22,"text":"\"Now exhale as much as you can and as fast as you can. \"These two factors are very important: that he maximally exhales and that he exhales as fast as he can. If he exhales slowly you are lost. He must exhale very fast. If he does, then volumes come out until here. Here you have residual volume. Residual volume cannot be exhaled.From the beginning of exhalation you count the time and after 1 second you go and see how much volume he exhaled. In 1 second he exhaled this volume from here to here. This volume that he exhaled by forced exhalation in the first second, we call it forced expiratory volume in the first second. F is for force, E is for expiratory, V is for volume, 1 is the first second.This total volume that he exhales is vital capacity. We call it only forced vital capacity because it was by forced exhalation. Vital capacity by forced exhalation is a little bit smaller than if you exhale slowly. Why, you don't know now. Later on you will understand it.Now if FEV1/forced vital capacity is 0.8, it means this person, normally, exhales 80% of the vital capacity in the first second. By normal 80%: if it is normal then it is 80% and if it is not normal then it is not 80%. \n12"},{"page":23,"text":"Spirogramtraces by obstructive and restrictive Diseases FEV1 FVCII. Normal and abnormal spirogramsIn the figure above, volume exhaled is plotted as a function of time during a singleforced expiration (the same maneuver as before). You can clearly see how thepattern of flow changes from normal to that in obstructive lung diseases andrestrictive lung diseases.In obstructive lung disease:Both FEV1 and FVC decrease in acute (asthmaattack) and chronic obstructive lung diseases (lung emphysema). The ratio of thetwo, FEV1/FVC, decrease as well, which is an important parameter for differentiationbetween obstructive and restrictive lung disease. FVC decreases slightly, becausesome airways are closing during forced expiration (dynamic airway obstruction) andthe air from those regions is entrapped and can not be exhaled. FEV1 decreases,because of high airway resistance and reduced FVC.In restrictive lung disease:Both FEV1 and FVC are reduced, because of the smallTLC, but more importantly the ratio FEV1/ FVC is either normal or slightly enhanced.It is because the recoil of the lung is increased in restrictive lung diseases such aslung fibrosis.H. Shams 11We go there and see that he is a normal person. He started exhalation here from total lung capacity. That is FEV1 and that is forced vital capacity. If you build the ratio of these two, for instance, here it’s exhaled for 4 L, total vital capacity was 5 L, and this ratio of this one divided by this one is 80%.In obstructive lung disease both values are decreasing. FVC, forced vital capacity, is reduced and also FEV1 is reduced but the ratio of these two is reduced. It is not 80%, it is 42%. In a restrictive disease all lung volumes were reduced, you remember? Therefore vital capacity is reduced and also FEV volume is reduced. Now you see, in these two parameters you cannot differentiate between restrictive and obstructive disease because both volumes are reduced. The only thing that you can use to differentiate them is this: in restrictive disease if you build the ratio of FEV to this one, then you see that it is even bigger than normal. It is 90%. In obstructive disease the ratio is less than 80%, let's say 70% or 40%. In restrictive disease the ratio is either normal or even higher. It is not reduced. Therefore by this ratio of FEV1 to this one, you can differentiate whether it is obstructive disease or restrictive disease.13"},{"page":24,"text":"H. ShamsWhen the different slopes of the previous expired volume–time curves are calculated in unitsof liters/second and plotted against corresponding lung volumes, or when expiratory flow isdirectly measured during forced expiration and plotted against lung volume, you obtain a “flowvolume loop”. This is an important diagnostic tool for obstructive and restrictive lung diseases.The following parameters are measured and analyzed by this test:Peak Expiratory Flow Rate (PEFR):It is the highest obtained flow that is observedimmediately after the beginning of expiration. This parameter is dependent on the patient’seffort. When patients do this with less effort they get a smaller peak flow but the lower part ofthe flow volume curve coincides with curves obtained with greater effort (effort independentregion).FEF 75:It is the forced expiratory flow at 75% of vital capacity. It means 25% of vital capacityis exhaled with remaining 75% in the lung.FEF 50:It is the forced expiratory flow at 50% of vital capacity. It means 50% of vital capacityis expired with remaining 50% in the lung.FEF 25:It is the forced expiratory flow at 25% of vital capacity. It means 75% of vital capacityis exhaled with remaining 25% in the lung.Absolute values of all above parameters are reduced by both obstructive lung disease(because of high resistance) and restrictive lung disease (because of low lung volume). Again,these values relative to lung volume TLC are normal by restrictive lung diseases, whereasthey are reduced in obstructive lung disease.12FEF 75FEF 504Lung volume (l)Lung volume in %  of TLCFEF 25105061007550225 00expiratory flow V(l/s)Etotal lung capacityEffort-independent regionResidual volume3PEFR1MaximaleffortModerate effort2Minimal effortExpiratory flow-volume curveV = PA / RIII. Normaland abnormalflow volumeloopsNow we do the same experiment, for example, but now we don't measure the volume and plot it against time like we did before. Now we measure the flow and plot it against lung volume. It means we do the same experiment. We take the patient connected with the spirometer and say, \"Maximal inspiration, maximal inhale, and then exhale as fast and as much as you can. \" He does it and you measure the flow. Then you see that the flow suddenly, here, if you look at the lung volume,my total lung capacity stoped after maximal inhalation, therefore 6L lung volume.Now as lung volume is high, you see that by exhalation the flow reaches a peak. That is the peak flow. Afterwards this flow is falling back to zero. Here you see that before it is zero volume. This volume we cannot exhale. The flow becomes zero.The problem of this test is that you say, \"Exhale maximally and as fast as you can, \" but if it doesn't do it you're lost. You have no power to do anything else. For example he does this. This card: these values have a name. That is the peak expiratory flow rate. That is the forced expiratory flow at 75% of vital capacity because if I take this value and come down, you see it's 5 L. 5 from 6 L is 75%. 14"},{"page":25,"text":"That is the forced expiratory flow at 50% of vital capacity. That is 25% of vital capacity.Anyway if you say to the patient, \"Do it, \" and he does it like this, then you get a peak flow here and that is not right because if the peak flow is reduced you have an obstruction. You see the peak flow is 12 L/s. If it comes to 8 L/s, 9 L/s, then you see he has an obstruction of the airway. There is some resistance there. Resistance is high. Therefore if I say pressure difference, we get some other flow.It is dependent on the patient. He can also excel like this: slow, fast, slow, fast. Other indication for U.S.: you must repeat it multiple times. It is not like other tests that you repeat it multiple times and take the average. If you do it multiple times and you got a high peak that is correct. All others were not correct. \n14"},{"page":26,"text":"8642084(L)206Lung volumeExpiratory Flow (L/sec)ObstructiveNormalChanges in Flow-Volume curve in obstructive and restrictive lung diseasesRestrictiveH. Shams 13\n15"},{"page":27,"text":"8642004(L)62Expired volumeExpiratory Flow (L/sec)Changes in Flow-Volume curve in obstructive and restrictive lung diseasesRestrictiveNormalObstructiveH. Shams 14\n16"},{"page":28,"text":"Pulmonary functionmeasurementFVC (L)FEV1 (L)FEV1/FVC (%)PEFR (L/sec)FEF25(L/sec)FEF50(L/sec)FEF75(L/sec)Slope of FV curveObstructive pulmonary diseaseRestrictivepulmonary disease, decreased.Chronic bronchitis, Emphysema, AsthmaPulmonary fibrosis))))Nto (PEFR/TLC(PEFR/TLC(PEFR/TLC(PEFR/TLC(PEFR/TLC N)(FEF25/TLCN)(FEF50/TLCN)(FEF75/TLCN)NtoN,Normal; Obstructive pulmonary disease:,increased; Restrictive pulmonary disease:C. Patterns of abnormalities in pulmonary function testsH. Shams 15\n17"},{"page":29,"text":"0_Alveolusintrathoracic Airways0_Alveolusintrathoracic Airways++PPPPPAPelPPelEPP (Paw= Ppl)(>0)AplawplawA. No breathingB. Forced expirationPressureAirway obstruction during forced expirationA. Dynamic compression of airways:During forced expiration patients are using the accessory muscles of expiration,abdominal and internal intercostal muscles. The contraction of these muscles createsa positive pressure in the thorax (like the Valsalva maneuver). At the point in anairway where this positive intrathoracic pressure is higher than the pressure insidethe airway, the airway becomes compressed. As voluntary effort increases, thecompression of the airway becomes stronger, so that the flow remains independentof the effort.H. Shams 16\n18"}],"url":"/cvp-pulmonary-reader.html?source=cvp-pulm-t-p20"}],"lectures":{"CVP-A14":{"sections":{"cvppa-a14-carotids":{"bodyAppend":[],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[],"questions":[],"bodyReplace":["Most of the first and second arch arteries regress. The third arch contributes the common carotid and proximal internal carotid.","The lecturer simplifies the retained derivative recall target to the carotid artery. Keep the embryonic arch arteries distinct from the adult arch of the aorta when answering this question."],"keyPointsReplace":["Third arch → common carotid and proximal internal carotid."],"referencesReplace":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","page":15},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","page":18}],"title":"Third arch and the carotid system"},"cvppa-a14-rings":{"bodyAppend":[],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[],"questions":[],"bodyReplace":["A double aortic arch retains right and left arterial routes around the trachea and esophagus, forming a vascular ring. Picture the arches passing from anterior toward posterior around these tubes: the esophagus lies posteriorly and the trachea anteriorly, so both can be compressed. Difficulty swallowing plus airway symptoms follows from the ring geometry.","The lecturer describes persistence of the distal right fourth-arch route as the explanation in this course. The full developmental model involves persistence of the right dorsal-aortic connection as well as the arch pathway; do not mistake the clinical result for a simple focal narrowing of the aortic lumen."],"referencesReplace":[{"source":"cvp-pulmonary-a14","page":18},{"source":"cvp-pulmonary-a14","page":19},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","page":21},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","page":22}],"keyPointsReplace":["Double arch can compress both trachea and esophagus.","The important spatial relationship is a ring around airway and food passage."],"title":"Double aortic arch and vascular rings","furtherReadingReplace":[]},"cvppa-a14-framework":{"bodyAppend":["Use the neural-crest statement as a developmental contribution: neural crest participates in formation/remodeling of the arch-arterial region. It does not mean that every structure in the adult vessel is exclusively derived from neural crest. The lecturer explicitly emphasizes this contribution before focusing on the fourth and sixth arches."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","page":5}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a14-crest-contribution","lectureIds":["CVP-A14"],"concept":"cvppa-a14-framework","question":"Which developmental contribution does the lecturer emphasize before discussing arch-artery remodeling?","options":["Conversion of mature intercostal nerves into arterial walls","Migration of alveolar macrophages into the aortic sac","Neural crest migration into the developing arterial region","Transformation of pleural mesothelium into all arch arteries"],"answer":2,"reasons":["Intercostal nerves are mature somatic nerves, not the migratory embryonic population emphasized here.","Alveolar macrophages are pulmonary defense cells, not the neural-crest population involved in arch development.","The transcript explicitly associates migration of neural crest with functional development of the aortic-arch region.","Pleural mesothelium is a serosal lining; this is not the contribution described for the embryonic arch arteries."],"explanation":"The transcript explicitly associates migration of neural crest with functional development of the aortic-arch region.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","page":5}],"skill":"recall","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]}},"newSections":[],"sourceNotes":["Transcript p23 says coarctation was already taught and is not discussed again; p24 similarly defers PDA to earlier teaching. These are cross-lecture deferrals, not claims that coarctation or PDA is excluded from the course exam. Existing sourced lessons remain.","Transcript PDF pages differ from printed slide/page labels; every transcript reference here uses the original PDF page index. p15 explicitly sets aside the second displayed carotid bullet; p18 sets aside first/second named remnants; p20 sets aside abnormal subclavian.","The chocolate-passing explanation on pp7–8 is a classroom analogy for sequential remodeling, not evidence that insufficient embryonic cardiac pumping mechanically causes arch regression. Do not teach that proposed causal mechanism."],"fillBlanks":[],"matching":[],"summaryReplace":["Aortic arches = embryonic paired arteries; arch of aorta = adult vessel.","Do not attribute the entire adult aortic arch to the fourth arch.","Sixth = pulmonary; distal persistence differs on the two sides.","Locate the narrowing relative to ductal insertion first.","Maternal rubella is associated with patent ductus arteriosus."]},"CVP-A15":{"sections":{"cvppa-a15-space":{"bodyAppend":["Separate two reasons that an intercostal block may be incomplete. A collateral branch runs along the upper border of the lower rib, whereas the main VAN bundle lies near the lower border of the upper rib. These are branches within one intercostal space. Dermatomal overlap is a different problem: adjacent segmental nerves can still supply the same skin, so the lecturer illustrates targeting the intended level together with the levels above and below. This is an anatomical explanation, not a stand-alone procedural protocol.","Identify the bundle by its plane and superior-to-inferior order in a specimen. Diagram colors are conventions; they are not reliable evidence that a preserved structure is artery, vein or nerve."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":3},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":4},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":5},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":6},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":22}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a15-block-two-problems","lectureIds":["CVP-A15"],"concept":"cvppa-a15-space","question":"A learner says that treating the collateral branch must eliminate sensation from adjacent dermatomes. Which correction distinguishes the two anatomical issues?","options":["Collateral branches and adjacent segmental nerves are two different sources of residual innervation","Collateral branches are veins, so neither issue involves nerves","The main intercostal nerve lies above the vein, so overlap is impossible","Adjacent dermatomes never share an area of skin"],"answer":0,"reasons":["A collateral branch belongs to the local branching arrangement; overlapping skin input can still arrive through neighboring segmental nerves.","The collateral branch discussed with nerve block includes neural supply; calling it only a vein misses the sensory issue.","The nerve is the lowest component of VAN, and its position does not eliminate overlap of neighboring sensory territories.","The lecture specifically explains that contiguous dermatomes overlap, which is why one-level blockade may be incomplete."],"explanation":"A collateral branch belongs to the local branching arrangement; overlapping skin input can still arrive through neighboring segmental nerves.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":4},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":5},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":6}],"skill":"mechanism","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]},"cvppa-a15-attachments":{"bodyAppend":["Follow both sides of the moving partition. During inspiration, diaphragm contraction lowers the central tendon: thoracic volume rises and intrathoracic pressure falls, while the displaced abdominal viscera raise intra-abdominal pressure. The same muscle movement therefore has opposite pressure effects in the two cavities; it does not lower pressure everywhere."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":10},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":14},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":15}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a15-paired-pressure","lectureIds":["CVP-A15"],"concept":"cvppa-a15-attachments","question":"During normal diaphragmatic inspiration, which paired pressure change is expected in the lecture model?","options":["Intrathoracic pressure falls while intra-abdominal pressure rises","Both intrathoracic and intra-abdominal pressures fall","Intrathoracic pressure rises while intra-abdominal pressure falls","Neither pressure changes because the central tendon cannot move"],"answer":0,"reasons":["Diaphragm descent expands the thoracic cavity and displaces/compresses abdominal contents, producing these opposite pressure changes.","Thoracic expansion lowers thoracic pressure, but descent compresses abdominal contents rather than expanding the abdominal compartment.","This reverses the described inspiratory mechanics: normal descent lowers thoracic pressure.","The central tendon moves downward with contraction; the resulting volume changes are what alter the pressures."],"explanation":"Diaphragm descent expands the thoracic cavity and displaces/compresses abdominal contents, producing these opposite pressure changes.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":10},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":14},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":15}],"skill":"mechanism","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]},"cvppa-a15-openings":{"bodyAppend":["The caval opening is in the central tendon. Relate its site to the thin, easily collapsible venous wall; the lecturer uses the tendon location to explain protection of the IVC during diaphragmatic activity. Do not transfer that relationship to the T10 esophageal opening merely because both structures cross the partition."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":12},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":13}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a15-caval-tendon","lectureIds":["CVP-A15"],"concept":"cvppa-a15-openings","question":"Which structure is described as crossing the central tendon of the diaphragm rather than the T10 esophageal opening?","options":["Inferior vena cava","Esophagus","Left vagus with esophagus","Esophageal branches of left gastric vessels"],"answer":0,"reasons":["The IVC uses the caval opening at T8 in the central tendon, as the transcript explicitly explains.","The esophagus uses the T10 esophageal opening, not the caval opening in the central tendon.","Vagal fibers accompany the esophagus at T10 rather than the IVC at T8.","The left gastric esophageal branches accompany the esophagus through its opening, not the central-tendon caval opening."],"explanation":"The IVC uses the caval opening at T8 in the central tendon, as the transcript explicitly explains.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":12},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":13}],"skill":"application","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]},"cvppa-a15-phrenic":{"bodyAppend":["C3–C5 identifies spinal cord segments/anterior spinal nerve rami supplying the phrenic nerve, whereas T8/T10/T12 identifies vertebral levels for the openings. These are different uses of spinal labels. A severe cord lesion above the phrenic outflow can interrupt descending drive to both hemidiaphragms even if the peripheral phrenic nerves themselves have not been severed."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":14}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a15-cord-above-outflow","lectureIds":["CVP-A15"],"concept":"cvppa-a15-phrenic","question":"Why can a severe spinal cord lesion above C3 prevent diaphragmatic activation even when both peripheral phrenic nerves are intact?","options":["Descending respiratory drive cannot reach the C3–C5 motor outflow","The phrenic nerves normally arise from T8 at the caval opening","The vagus is the sole motor supply of both hemidiaphragms","The lesion turns the central tendon into contractile muscle"],"answer":0,"reasons":["A lesion above the phrenic motor outflow can disconnect it from descending activation, as illustrated by the lecturer.","T8 describes the caval vertebral level, not the origin of phrenic motor fibers; their origin is C3–C5.","Motor supply of the diaphragm is phrenic; the vagus runs posterior to the lung root and is not its sole motor supply.","Loss of neural drive does not transform tendon into muscle; it prevents activation of existing diaphragmatic muscle."],"explanation":"A lesion above the phrenic motor outflow can disconnect it from descending activation, as illustrated by the lecturer.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":14}],"skill":"clinical localization","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]}},"newSections":[],"sourceNotes":["The transcript uses C10 for the vagus in places; interpret the intended label as cranial nerve X, not a tenth cervical spinal nerve. C3–C5 phrenic labels refer to spinal outflow.","Do not reproduce fixed local-anesthetic durations/generations (p4), a fixed 29-dermatome count (p5), or bedside fracture/bedding instructions (pp8,23) as general clinical facts. They are incidental remarks outside the supplied anatomy objectives, and are not needed for the retained teaching.","The p22 quiz wording asks which structure contracts in quiet expiration, but its intended answer is elastic tissue. The p23 explanation makes clear that this means passive elastic recoil after diaphragm relaxation; the authored practice preserves the physiological meaning rather than calling elastic tissue a contracting muscle."],"fillBlanks":[],"matching":[{"id":"cvpt-a15-match-pressure","title":"Match the event to the pressure or motion consequence","pairs":[{"term":"Contracting normal diaphragm","definition":"Central tendon descends and thoracic volume increases"},{"term":"Inspiratory abdominal displacement","definition":"Intra-abdominal pressure rises"},{"term":"Paralyzed dome during inspiration","definition":"Affected side moves superiorly despite normal side descending"},{"term":"Free flail segment during inspiration","definition":"Unsupported chest wall moves inward"}],"explanation":"The same inspiratory effort can move a normally activated diaphragm downward, a denervated dome upward, and an unsupported chest-wall fragment inward. The affected structures and mechanisms differ.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":8},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":10},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a15","page":17}]}]},"CVP-A16":{"sections":{"cvppa-a16-airway":{"bodyAppend":["The open posterior side of each C-shaped tracheal cartilage ring faces the esophagus. Its soft posterior wall accommodates the esophageal expansion associated with swallowing while the cartilaginous part maintains airway patency. The endoscope sees the internal carina and airway lumen; a surface view of the tracheal bifurcation is not the same view."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":4},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":5},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":6}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a16-posterior-soft-wall","lectureIds":["CVP-A16"],"concept":"cvppa-a16-airway","question":"Which relationship explains why tracheal cartilage is C-shaped with its gap posteriorly?","options":["The gap is the normal opening into the pleural cavity","The anterior thyroid cartilage must expand into the posterior tracheal wall","The esophagus behind the trachea needs room to expand during swallowing","The pulmonary artery enters through the gap in every cartilage ring"],"answer":2,"reasons":["The airway is normally sealed from pleural space; such a communication would be abnormal.","The cited relationship is to the posterior esophagus, not an anterior thyroid-cartilage expansion into the trachea.","The transcript links the soft posterior wall to expansion of the esophagus immediately behind it.","Pulmonary arteries do not enter through successive tracheal cartilage gaps; they are part of the lung-root vascular arrangement."],"explanation":"The transcript links the soft posterior wall to expansion of the esophagus immediately behind it.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":4},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":5}],"skill":"mechanism","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]},"cvppa-a16-lobes":{"bodyAppend":["Use lobe geometry at the bedside. The lecturer describes superior-lobe listening anteriorly, the right middle lobe over the anterior chest around ribs 4–6, and the inferior lobes predominantly posteriorly. Thus a learner who listens only over the front has not sampled the same lobe distribution as one who also examines the back. These are orientation guides rather than a claim that a lobe has only one audible point."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":14},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":18}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a16-posterior-auscultation","lectureIds":["CVP-A16"],"concept":"cvppa-a16-lobes","question":"According to the lecturer’s anatomical orientation, which lobe territory is especially important to sample over the back rather than relying only on the anterior chest?","options":["The superior lobes exclusively","Inferior lobes","Right middle lobe only","Lingula as a separate third left lobe"],"answer":1,"reasons":["Superior lobes are emphasized from the front in the lecturer’s comparison, whereas the question asks for the posterior emphasis.","The transcript emphasizes the posterior location of inferior-lobe territory when explaining auscultation.","The right middle lobe is emphasized anteriorly around ribs 4–6, rather than as the principal posterior territory.","The lingula belongs to the left superior lobe; it is not a separate third lobe."],"explanation":"The transcript emphasizes the posterior location of inferior-lobe territory when explaining auscultation.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":14}],"skill":"clinical anatomy","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]},"cvppa-a16-segments":{"bodyAppend":["Segmentectomy also requires control of the airway opening. The lecturer contrasts sealing the cut bronchus with ligating a blood vessel: after bronchial division, an unsealed route can allow air to reach the pleural space and undermine lung expansion. The useful principle is airway integrity after resection, not memorizing historical dates of lung surgery or assuming every pleural air leak is tension pneumothorax.","Retain segment names and their lobe membership for recall. The lecturer explicitly says that the stylized segment drawing is a memorization aid and will not be used to ask exact picture-location identification. The left apicoposterior grouping may vary; choosing between nine and ten left segments is explicitly not an exam task."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":19},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":20},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":22}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a16-airway-seal","lectureIds":["CVP-A16"],"concept":"cvppa-a16-segments","question":"After removal of a diseased lung segment, why is secure closure of the divided bronchus anatomically important?","options":["It prevents an abnormal airway-to-pleural air leak that can impair lung expansion","It converts pulmonary veins into arteries","It prevents the normal pores of Kohn from joining neighboring alveoli","It recreates the horizontal fissure in the left lung"],"answer":0,"reasons":["An open bronchial route can allow air into the pleural space; the transcript uses this to explain careful bronchial closure.","Bronchial closure preserves airway integrity and does not change a vessel from vein to artery.","Pores of Kohn are normal microscopic interalveolar connections, not the surgically divided bronchial opening.","The left lung normally has no horizontal fissure; closure of a bronchus does not create a new fissure."],"explanation":"An open bronchial route can allow air into the pleural space; the transcript uses this to explain careful bronchial closure.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":19}],"skill":"mechanism","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}],"bodyReplace":["A bronchopulmonary segment is a pyramidal subdivision of a lobe, apex toward root and base at pleura, separated by connective-tissue boundaries. Each has its own tertiary bronchus and pulmonary arterial branch. Pulmonary veins run between segments and drain adjacent territories. This arrangement allows a diseased segment to be resected while preserving neighboring segments.","The named territories remain the recall target. On the right: upper lobe—apical, anterior, posterior; middle lobe—lateral, medial; lower lobe—superior, anterior basal, posterior basal, lateral basal, medial basal. On the left: upper lobe—apicoposterior, anterior, superior lingular, inferior lingular; lower lobe—superior and the basal territories. Use lung/lobe membership rather than locating a numbered territory on a stylized picture."],"keyPointsReplace":["Bronchus + artery within segment; pulmonary veins intersegmental.","Know named segment territories and their lung/lobe membership.","Do not confuse a segment with a lobe or microscopic lobule."]},"cvppa-a16-cancer":{"bodyAppend":["In Horner syndrome, sympathetic interruption weakens the superior tarsal muscle and produces mild or partial ptosis. The lecturer distinguishes this from complete ptosis due to loss of oculomotor supply to the main eyelid elevator. Pair the mild ptosis with miosis and reduced ipsilateral facial sweating to localize the sympathetic pathway rather than treating every drooping eyelid as the same lesion."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":25},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":26}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a16-horner-eyelid-muscle","lectureIds":["CVP-A16"],"concept":"cvppa-a16-cancer","question":"An apical lung lesion causes miosis, ipsilateral anhidrosis and mild upper-lid droop. Which muscle explains the eyelid component?","options":["Intrinsic hand muscles after C8–T1 injury","Vocal-fold muscles after recurrent laryngeal injury","Superior tarsal muscle after loss of sympathetic input","Diaphragm after phrenic injury"],"answer":2,"reasons":["C8–T1 lower-trunk involvement can cause intrinsic-hand wasting and medial limb symptoms, not ptosis.","Recurrent laryngeal involvement causes voice dysfunction; it does not supply the superior tarsal muscle.","Loss of sympathetic input to superior tarsal muscle accounts for mild ptosis in the Horner pattern.","Phrenic injury produces ipsilateral diaphragmatic weakness, not the eyelid component of Horner syndrome."],"explanation":"Loss of sympathetic input to superior tarsal muscle accounts for mild ptosis in the Horner pattern.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":25},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":26}],"skill":"clinical localization","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]}},"newSections":[],"sourceNotes":["Contradiction audit: p12 says the RIGHT recurrent laryngeal nerve can be injured during PDA surgery, but p11 and A14 p12 place the LEFT nerve at the ductus/arch. Retain the left-sided anatomical relationship; a published PDA-ligation series documents left vocal-cord palsy (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2796367/).","Do not promote p23 “all tumours ... come from bronchus” into a universal fact. NCI identifies adenocarcinoma beginning in alveolar-lining cells (https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/patient/non-small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq). The active guide describes the course bronchogenic pattern rather than every lung neoplasm.","Do not reproduce the unsupported p24 “99%” hemoptysis attribution, p27 “only” right-lung SVC syndrome, p29 untreatable fungal pneumonia/90% mortality and blanket mild viral-pneumonia claims, or p30 denial of AP chest imaging. These are overgeneralizations, not reliable diagnostic rules. CDC explicitly describes effective antifungals for pulmonary histoplasmosis and severity-dependent management (https://www.cdc.gov/histoplasmosis/hcp/clinical-overview/).","p13 “NEVER superior” aspiration is overly absolute. Preserve the supplied upright posterior-basal emphasis as a position-dependent tendency, as the existing guide already does; do not imply that other sites are impossible."],"fillBlanks":[],"matching":[]},"CVP-A17":{"sections":{"cvppa-a17-bronchus":{"bodyAppend":["For a histology answer, replace the shorthand “respiratory epithelium” with the actual visible epithelial description: typically pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, with goblet cells when present. In bronchioles, the correct description varies with airway size, so do not list every possible lining as though all were present in the specimen."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":6},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":11}],"questions":[]},"cvppa-a17-bronchioles":{"bodyAppend":["An image with one layer of cuboidal cells should be called simple cuboidal epithelium. If its nuclei form one row and the cells are as high as they are wide, the fact that larger bronchioles may have pseudostratified or simple columnar epithelium does not change this observed lining. Match your answer to the displayed wall, then use cartilage/glands and alveolar openings to identify the airway level."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":11},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":16}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a17-observed-epithelium","lectureIds":["CVP-A17"],"concept":"cvppa-a17-bronchioles","question":"A bronchiolar micrograph shows a single row of round nuclei in cells approximately as tall as they are wide. What is the best lining description for that field?","options":["Simple cuboidal epithelium","All bronchiolar epithelial types simultaneously","Stratified squamous keratinized epithelium","Pseudostratified columnar epithelium solely because the airway is intrapulmonary"],"answer":0,"reasons":["One layer of roughly cube-shaped cells with one nuclear row supports simple cuboidal epithelium in this observed field.","Bronchiolar lining varies along the tree; an image question requires the visible type, not a list of every possible type.","The described single cuboidal layer is neither multilayered nor keratinized squamous epithelium.","Being inside the lung does not force pseudostratification; smaller bronchioles simplify to simple columnar or cuboidal lining."],"explanation":"One layer of roughly cube-shaped cells with one nuclear row supports simple cuboidal epithelium in this observed field.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":11},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":16}],"skill":"morphology interpretation","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]},"cvppa-a17-club-clearance":{"bodyAppend":["The distal loss sequence is glands first, then goblet cells, then cilia. This leaves a ciliated clearance margin beyond the last mucus-producing cells. Club cells have the protein-secretory machinery expected for CC16 production: rough endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi, mitochondria and secretory vesicles. Their secretions and progenitor role belong to bronchiolar epithelium; do not identify them as alveolar type I exchange cells."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":12},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":13},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":14}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a17-loss-sequence","lectureIds":["CVP-A17"],"concept":"cvppa-a17-club-clearance","question":"Which proximal-to-distal order of disappearance is explained in the transcript?","options":["Cilia → goblet cells → glands","Goblet cells → cilia → glands","Glands → all alveoli → pulmonary capillaries","Glands → goblet cells → cilia"],"answer":3,"reasons":["Losing cilia before mucus-producing structures would leave secretions without the clearance margin emphasized by the lecturer.","The lecture places loss of glands before loss of goblet cells, and loss of cilia after them.","Alveoli appear in the respiratory portion; their disappearance and loss of capillaries are not the normal sequence discussed.","Glands disappear first, goblet cells next, and cilia extend farther distally to clear secretions."],"explanation":"Glands disappear first, goblet cells next, and cilia extend farther distally to clear secretions.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":14}],"skill":"mechanism","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"},{"id":"cvpt-a17-protein-machinery","lectureIds":["CVP-A17"],"concept":"cvppa-a17-club-clearance","question":"Which organelle combination best supports the protein-secretory role of a bronchiolar club cell described in the transcript?","options":["Motile cilia as the sole protein-synthetic organelle","Rough endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi and secretory vesicles","Hyaline cartilage plates and perichondrium","A contractile sarcomere lattice replacing the nucleus"],"answer":1,"reasons":["Cilia move material and club cells are nonciliated; cilia are not the rough-ER/Golgi pathway for protein secretion.","The transcript links protein secretion to rough ER synthesis, Golgi processing and vesicular storage/release, supported by mitochondrial energy.","Cartilage is an extracellular supporting tissue of bronchi, not the protein-secretory organelle set of club cells.","Club cells are secretory epithelial cells rather than skeletal muscle fibers with sarcomeric machinery."],"explanation":"The transcript links protein secretion to rough ER synthesis, Golgi processing and vesicular storage/release, supported by mitochondrial energy.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":12}],"skill":"cell structure–function","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]},"cvppa-a17-respiratory-airways":{"bodyAppend":["Avoid the location trap: alveoli around the outside of an airway show that the section is inside the lung, but they do not by themselves make the airway a respiratory bronchiole. For that identification, alveoli must interrupt or open directly from its own wall. Club cells persist in respiratory-bronchiolar epithelial stretches; a duct/sac bounded by alveolar openings no longer has that bronchiolar club-cell lining."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":8},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":10},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":15},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":16},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":17}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a17-surrounding-versus-wall","lectureIds":["CVP-A17"],"concept":"cvppa-a17-respiratory-airways","question":"A small airway is surrounded by alveoli, but careful inspection shows its own epithelial wall is continuous and has no alveolar outpocketings. Which inference is justified?","options":["Surrounding alveoli prove that cartilage plates are present","Surrounding alveoli establish an intrapulmonary location but do not alone prove respiratory-bronchiole identity","Every airway surrounded by alveoli must be an alveolar duct","The airway must be outside the lung because its wall is continuous"],"answer":1,"reasons":["Cartilage is a separate wall feature; surrounding alveoli cannot establish its presence.","The transcript uses surrounding alveoli to establish location, but direct alveolar interruptions of the airway wall distinguish respiratory bronchioles.","An alveolar duct is bounded almost entirely by alveolar openings, unlike the continuous epithelial wall described.","An intrapulmonary bronchus or conducting bronchiole can have a continuous wall while alveoli lie around it."],"explanation":"The transcript uses surrounding alveoli to establish location, but direct alveolar interruptions of the airway wall distinguish respiratory bronchioles.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":8},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":10},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":16}],"skill":"morphology interpretation","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"},{"id":"cvpt-a17-club-cell-distal-limit","lectureIds":["CVP-A17"],"concept":"cvppa-a17-respiratory-airways","question":"In the airway progression described, which is the most distal part retaining bronchiolar epithelial stretches with club cells?","options":["Type I pneumocyte surface of one alveolus","Respiratory bronchiole","Alveolar duct lined only by alveolar openings","Alveolar sac common space"],"answer":1,"reasons":["Type I pneumocytes form thin alveolar exchange lining; they are not the club-cell population.","Respiratory bronchioles retain cuboidal epithelial stretches with club cells between alveolar outpocketings.","The lecturer states that ducts have only alveolar openings rather than the club-cell bronchiolar lining.","A sac is the common space into which alveoli open; it does not retain bronchiolar club-cell epithelium."],"explanation":"Respiratory bronchioles retain cuboidal epithelial stretches with club cells between alveolar outpocketings.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":16},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":17}],"skill":"comparison","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]},"cvppa-a17-pneumocytes":{"bodyAppend":["Type I occluding junctions help keep a continuous epithelial barrier rather than leaving open gaps for tissue fluid between adjacent cells. The thinness of the gas-exchange cell and the tightness of the epithelial seal are complementary: gases cross the thin exchange pathway, while the lining remains joined."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":19}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a17-occluding-junctions","lectureIds":["CVP-A17"],"concept":"cvppa-a17-pneumocytes","question":"Why does the transcript emphasize occluding junctions between alveolar lining cells?","options":["They turn type I cells into the renewing mitotic population","They store DPPC in lamellar layers","They create pores of Kohn inside capillary endothelial cells","They maintain an epithelial seal rather than leaving intercellular routes for fluid leakage"],"answer":3,"reasons":["The lecture assigns epithelial renewal to type II cells; junctions do not make type I cells the renewing population.","Surfactant storage in lamellar bodies belongs to type II cell cytoplasm, not the junctional seal.","Pores of Kohn are interalveolar air connections, not endothelial junctions within capillaries.","The lecturer connects tight junctions with preventing passage of fluid between adjacent alveolar epithelial cells."],"explanation":"The lecturer connects tight junctions with preventing passage of fluid between adjacent alveolar epithelial cells.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":19}],"skill":"mechanism","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]},"cvppa-a17-macrophages":{"bodyAppend":["An alveolar macrophage can move by pseudopodia toward a region with cilia; its own surface does not need cilia for this migration. Once its load reaches the mucociliary route, ciliary transport moves material toward the pharynx for swallowing or expectoration. Some loaded cells instead enter septal connective tissue, where retained particulate material can persist."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":23},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":24}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a17-clearance-two-motions","lectureIds":["CVP-A17"],"concept":"cvppa-a17-macrophages","question":"How does a particle-loaded alveolar macrophage reach the mucociliary clearance route despite having no cilia of its own?","options":["It migrates using pseudopodia, then ciliated airway epithelium transports the material proximally","It grows a hyaline cartilage ring to pull itself toward the trachea","It becomes a type I pneumocyte and is carried in pulmonary arterial blood","It depends on goblet cells inside every alveolus to beat it upward"],"answer":0,"reasons":["The transcript distinguishes macrophage movement by pseudopodia from subsequent ciliary transport toward the pharynx.","Cartilage supports bronchi and is not a locomotor structure produced by migrating macrophages.","Macrophages do not need to become pneumocytes or enter pulmonary arterial blood to reach the mucociliary route.","Alveoli do not have a goblet-cell mucociliary lining; this is why migration toward a ciliated region matters."],"explanation":"The transcript distinguishes macrophage movement by pseudopodia from subsequent ciliary transport toward the pharynx.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":23},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":24}],"skill":"mechanism","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]},"cvppa-a17-septum":{"bodyAppend":["Apply pores of Kohn to a blocked-bronchiole example: adjacent ventilated alveoli can provide a collateral air route across septal pores to some alveoli beyond the obstruction. This explains how a local bronchiolar blockage need not completely isolate every downstream alveolus. It is a normal air-space communication, not a new blood vessel or a guarantee of full normal ventilation."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":25}],"questions":[{"id":"cvpt-a17-collateral-blockage","lectureIds":["CVP-A17"],"concept":"cvppa-a17-septum","question":"One small bronchiole becomes obstructed while neighboring alveoli remain ventilated. Which normal structure can provide an alternative interalveolar air route?","options":["The central tendon of the diaphragm","A pulmonary vein becoming an air-filled channel","Pores of Kohn in the interalveolar septa","A closed epithelial tight junction"],"answer":2,"reasons":["The central tendon transmits diaphragmatic force and contains the caval opening; it is not an interalveolar passage.","Pulmonary veins carry blood between segments; converting them into air channels is not a normal ventilation mechanism.","Septal pores connect neighboring alveolar air spaces, permitting collateral air movement despite a local bronchiolar obstruction.","An occluding junction seals adjacent lining cells and is not the described open collateral air route."],"explanation":"Septal pores connect neighboring alveolar air spaces, permitting collateral air movement despite a local bronchiolar obstruction.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":25}],"skill":"application","provenance":"Authored from supplied lecture transcript"}]},"cvppa-a17-pathology":{"bodyAppend":["For cystic fibrosis, distinguish the ciliary machinery from the material it must move: the lecturer describes functioning cilia that cannot effectively transport very viscous mucus. In emphysema, several alveolar spaces merge as septa are destroyed, so a larger air space can have less remaining exchange surface. For these clinical labels, the requested depth is recognition of the affected structure and mechanism, not a complete pathology or treatment syllabus."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":30},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":31},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":32}],"questions":[]},"cvppa-a17-hierarchy":{"bodyReplace":["The conducting region comprises nasal cavities, pharynx, larynx, trachea, bronchi, bronchioles and terminal bronchioles. It conditions inspired air and provides conduits. The respiratory region begins at respiratory bronchioles and continues through alveolar ducts to alveoli. The appearance of alveoli in the airway wall marks access to gas exchange.","Lungs divide into lobes, bronchopulmonary segments and then pulmonary lobules supplied by bronchioles. These are different structural scales: a segment is not a microscopic lobule. Learn this hierarchy without a histology segment-count task."],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":4}],"questions":[]}},"newSections":[],"sourceNotes":["Histology transcript p4 explicitly defers numerical segment questions to anatomy. Anatomy transcript p22 explains the nine-versus-ten variation and says learners will not be asked to choose between them. Keep the hierarchy and names; do not convert the source disagreement into numerical exam priority.","Club-cell eponym history (p13) is incidental and is not turned into a medical assessment item. The club-cell protein-secretory functions, epithelial renewal and distal airway location are retained.","References are original PDF pages, not printed slide numbers: for example respiratory bronchiole OSPE emphasis is PDF p16 (printed p17), and thin barrier order is PDF p26 (printed p27)."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"cvpt-a17-fill-distal-sequence","prompt":"Distally, glands disappear first, then goblet cells, and finally ___.","answers":["cilia","ciliated cells"],"explanation":"Cilia persist beyond the most distal mucus-producing cells so secretions can still be moved proximally.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":14}]}],"matching":[{"id":"cvpt-a17-match-clinical-structure","title":"Match the case mechanism to its structural failure","pairs":[{"term":"Asthma","definition":"Bronchiolar mucus and thickened smooth-muscle wall narrow the passage"},{"term":"Emphysema","definition":"Destroyed alveolar septa merge spaces and reduce exchange surface"},{"term":"Cystic fibrosis","definition":"Very viscous mucus resists effective transport despite ciliary movement"},{"term":"Premature-infant surfactant deficiency","definition":"Alveolar surface tension promotes expiratory collapse"}],"explanation":"Localize the failure first: airway wall, alveolar septum, mucus transport or alveolar surface lining.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":22},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":30},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":31},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":32}]}]},"CVP-P16":{"sections":{"CVP-P16:pulm-transport":{"bodyAppend":["The mixed-venous bar means that venous blood returning from different systemic organs has mixed in the right heart; the organs can contribute different oxygen contents. End-capillary blood (c′) denotes blood at the end of a capillary and is a different sampling compartment. FEN₂ means expired nitrogen fraction: read the quantity, compartment and gas separately.","A gas partial pressure is its fraction of the mixture’s total pressure (Dalton’s law). Dry sea-level air gives PO₂ = 0.21 × 760 ≈ 160 mmHg. The approximately 150 mmHg value belongs to inspired air after humidification at body temperature. At altitude, oxygen fraction remains about 0.21 while barometric pressure and oxygen partial pressure fall; a smaller partial pressure does not mean a smaller oxygen percentage."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":3},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":4},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":6},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":7}],"questions":[{"id":"CVP-P16:transcript-altitude-fraction","lectureIds":["CVP-P16"],"concept":"CVP-P16:pulm-transport","question":"Dry air at a mountain station has barometric pressure 500 mmHg and oxygen fraction 0.21. Which description is correct?","options":["PO₂ remains 160 mmHg because the oxygen percentage is unchanged.","Oxygen fraction becomes 0.105 because barometric pressure fell.","PO₂ is 500 mmHg because all gases share the total pressure.","PO₂ is 105 mmHg although the oxygen percentage remains 21%."],"answer":3,"reasons":["An unchanged fraction cannot maintain the same partial pressure when total pressure falls.","0.105 confuses a pressure calculation with the fraction of oxygen molecules.","500 mmHg is total mixture pressure, including nitrogen and other gases.","0.21 × 500 = 105 mmHg: lower total pressure reduces the oxygen partial pressure."],"explanation":"For dry air use PO₂ = FO₂ × PB; the gas fraction and gas partial pressure answer different questions.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":7}],"skill":"Calculation","kind":"Calculation","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"},{"id":"CVP-P16:transcript-mixed-venous","lectureIds":["CVP-P16"],"concept":"CVP-P16:pulm-transport","question":"Why is blood in the pulmonary artery described as mixed venous?","options":["Systemic venous returns with different gas contents have combined in the right heart.","It has just completed equilibration with alveolar gas.","It is a mixture produced by the spirometer.","It is a mixture of alveolar gas and plasma."],"answer":0,"reasons":["The right heart combines systemic venous returns before sending them to the lungs.","Equilibration occurs across pulmonary capillaries; end-capillary blood has its own c′ notation.","The term concerns vascular sampling, not a breathing apparatus.","Mixing gas with blood is not what mixed venous denotes; that would describe a different physical problem."],"explanation":"Venous oxygen contents differ among organs, so the combined return has a mixed value.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":4}],"skill":"Notation","kind":"Notation","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"}]},"CVP-P16:pulm-zones":{"bodyAppend":["The transcript frames barrier design as a compromise: a shorter diffusion path favours a very thin wall, but blood and gas must still remain mechanically separated. Its roughly 300 million tiny alveoli provide a large surface within a small thoracic volume; branching connects that surface to a common airway opening. Blood-vessel branching brings a capillary network close to each exchange unit. The large daily gas-throughput figures are rough scale illustrations, rather than fixed metabolic reference values."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":9},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":10},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":11},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":12},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":13}],"questions":[{"id":"CVP-P16:transcript-barrier-tradeoff","lectureIds":["CVP-P16"],"concept":"CVP-P16:pulm-zones","question":"Why would making the air–blood barrier as thin as possible without any structural constraint be a poor design?","options":["Mechanical failure would mix blood and alveolar gas, so stability must be balanced against diffusion distance.","All exchange requires a thick cartilage layer.","A thin barrier removes the need for a large exchange area.","A thin barrier prevents passive diffusion."],"answer":0,"reasons":["The membrane must both permit diffusion and physically separate the two compartments.","Cartilage supports proximal conducting airways; it would obstruct a short diffusion path.","Thickness and area independently affect exchange; one does not remove the need for the other.","Thinning shortens the diffusion distance and generally favours diffusion."],"explanation":"The transcript explains the thin barrier as a compromise between efficient diffusion and mechanical integrity.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":9},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":10}],"skill":"Mechanism","kind":"Mechanism","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"}]},"CVP-P16:pulm-deadspace":{"bodyAppend":["Follow gas composition through a breath, not just the volume of its container. At the end of expiration, conducting dead space contains relatively oxygen-depleted, CO₂-enriched alveolar gas. The next inspiration pushes that gas back into the alveoli first; only the following fresh portion supplies new external gas to alveoli. At end inspiration the conducting space holds fresh inspired gas. Thus a 450-mL inspiration expands the lung by 450 mL, but only 300 mL is fresh gas reaching alveoli when VD is 150 mL.","Treat 150 mL as a useful fixed-volume approximation for these examples. P20 explicitly revisits the earlier claim of constancy: airway calibre changes can alter anatomical dead-space volume. For a fixed-length tube, volume scales as r² while laminar resistance scales as 1/r⁴, so a modest volume change can accompany a much greater resistance change. The transcript’s blocked, unventilated alveolus example is a source error already corrected above: preserved perfusion of an unventilated unit is shunt, whereas alveolar dead space requires wasted ventilation."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":14},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":15},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":16},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":5}],"questions":[{"id":"CVP-P16:transcript-end-expiratory-gas","lectureIds":["CVP-P16"],"concept":"CVP-P16:pulm-deadspace","question":"Immediately after quiet expiration, which gas is pushed into alveoli first by the next inspiration?","options":["Alveolar gas left in conducting dead space by the preceding expiration.","Pure oxygen stored in the trachea.","Mixed venous blood from the right heart.","Fresh atmospheric gas that has already crossed the full conducting airway."],"answer":0,"reasons":["Expiration leaves alveolar gas in the airway; inspiration first displaces that gas inward.","The trachea stores a gas mixture, not pure oxygen.","Blood does not normally enter an airway.","Fresh external gas follows the gas already occupying the conducting space."],"explanation":"Anatomical dead space changes gas composition across the cycle even when its volume is approximated as constant.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":15},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":16}],"skill":"Mechanism","kind":"Mechanism","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"},{"id":"CVP-P16:transcript-expansion-versus-fresh","lectureIds":["CVP-P16"],"concept":"CVP-P16:pulm-deadspace","question":"With VD = 150 mL, a 450-mL inspiration produces which pair in the transcript model?","options":["Lung expansion 300 mL; fresh gas reaching alveoli 450 mL.","Lung expansion 450 mL; fresh gas reaching alveoli 300 mL.","Lung expansion 150 mL; fresh gas reaching alveoli 150 mL.","Lung expansion 600 mL; fresh gas reaching alveoli 450 mL."],"answer":1,"reasons":["This reverses the distinction: re-inspired dead-space gas still contributes to expansion.","All 450 mL adds to lung gas volume, but 150 mL is re-inspired gas and only 300 mL is fresh alveolar supply.","150 mL is the dead-space component, not the total volume expansion or fresh alveolar component.","Adding dead space to VT double-counts gas already within the lung–airway system."],"explanation":"VT measures expansion; VT − VD measures fresh alveolar delivery. These are different quantities.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":15},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":16}],"skill":"Application","kind":"Application","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"}]},"CVP-P16:pulm-epithelium":{"bodyAppend":["The lecturer links clearance to exchange: mucus traps particles proximally and cilia move it toward the throat for removal/swallowing. Material escaping to alveoli is handled by macrophages. Keeping the distal surface clean protects a short diffusion path; a thick deposit over the exchange surface would undermine the benefit of the thin barrier."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":18},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":19},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":20}],"questions":[]}},"newSections":[],"sourceNotes":["Transcript expansion is authored teaching with page-specific evidence; transcript wording is preserved in the source reader. Lecturer emphasis is distinct from explicit exam priority. Source medical slips are clarified in the relevant topic."],"fillBlanks":[],"matching":[{"id":"CVP-P16:transcript-compartment-match","title":"Read the gas or blood compartment","pairs":[{"term":"A","definition":"Alveolar gas"},{"term":"a","definition":"Arterial blood"},{"term":"c′","definition":"End-capillary blood"},{"term":"v̄","definition":"Mixed venous blood after systemic returns mix"}],"explanation":"Similar letters refer to distinct compartments; case and marks alter the meaning.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":3},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p16","page":4}]}]},"CVP-P17":{"sections":{"CVP-P17:pulm-capacities":{"bodyAppend":["The reserve volumes can be recruited into a larger breath. From maximal expiration to maximal inspiration, the greatest mobilisable breath equals VC; RV still remains outside that excursion. Saying that a maximal breath uses the reserves does not mean the anatomical definitions of IRV and ERV disappear.","The gas reservoir at end quiet expiration (FRC) allows ongoing exchange between breaths and buffers alveolar gas composition: a few hundred millilitres of fresh gas mixes into several litres already present. This keeps alveolar PO₂ and PCO₂ changes much smaller than they would be if the lung emptied completely every expiration. Retaining inflation also avoids repeatedly reopening a fully collapsed lung against surface forces. FRC, rather than RV alone, is the reservoir present during ordinary quiet breathing."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":6},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":7}],"questions":[{"id":"CVP-P17:transcript-reservoir-buffer","lectureIds":["CVP-P17"],"concept":"CVP-P17:pulm-capacities","question":"Why does alveolar gas composition change only modestly during an ordinary breath?","options":["Gas exchange stops until the next inspiration.","Every breath completely replaces all alveolar gas.","A small fresh alveolar portion mixes with the larger gas reservoir present at FRC.","Anatomical dead space itself supplies all oxygen consumed between breaths."],"answer":2,"reasons":["Blood–gas exchange continues across ventilated alveolar surfaces between inspirations.","Complete replacement would create much larger composition changes.","The reservoir dilutes each incoming portion and continues supporting exchange between breaths.","Conducting dead space does not exchange gas with pulmonary blood."],"explanation":"The transcript compares several litres at FRC with only a few hundred millilitres of fresh gas delivered per breath.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":6},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":7}],"skill":"Mechanism","kind":"Mechanism","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"},{"id":"CVP-P17:transcript-maximal-breath","lectureIds":["CVP-P17"],"concept":"CVP-P17:pulm-capacities","question":"A person expires maximally and then inspires maximally. Which quantity describes that entire inspired excursion?","options":["TLC, because no gas remained before inspiration.","VC, because the excursion spans RV to TLC.","FRC, because every inspiration starts at quiet expiration.","RV, because that volume was present at the beginning."],"answer":1,"reasons":["RV remained before inspiration; TLC is the final absolute volume, not the change.","VC = TLC − RV is the maximum volume that can be mobilised.","The starting point was maximal expiration, not quiet expiration at FRC.","RV is the initial non-exhaled volume, not the subsequent inspired excursion."],"explanation":"Distinguish a starting absolute volume, an ending absolute volume and the difference between them.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":6}],"skill":"Application","kind":"Application","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"}]},"CVP-P17:pulm-helium":{"bodyAppend":["The lecturer’s clinical trap concerns the connection landmark. If a test intended to measure RV connects before maximal expiration is complete, the added communicating lung volume includes gas above RV. Calling that result RV falsely implies air trapping. A later correctly performed test can appear to show dramatic recovery even though only technique changed. First check the manoeuvre and starting phase before explaining a change biologically.","The dilution analogy depends on conservation of tracer amount, not conservation of tracer fraction: the same helium amount occupies a larger accessible gas volume after mixing, so its fraction falls. A sealed circuit and sufficient equilibration are assumptions of that calculation; inspired and expired gas movements alone do not create or consume helium."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":4},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":5}],"questions":[{"id":"CVP-P17:transcript-false-rv","lectureIds":["CVP-P17"],"concept":"CVP-P17:pulm-helium","question":"A helium test intended to measure RV connects after an incomplete maximal expiration and gives 3 L. A later properly performed test gives 1.5 L. What is the first explanation to examine?","options":["A larger measured helium fraction always means a larger lung volume.","Rapid regeneration of emphysematous alveolar walls.","The first connection included extra gas above true RV.","Helium was produced by the patient during the first test."],"answer":2,"reasons":["With a fixed initial helium amount, a larger final fraction implies less dilution and a smaller added volume.","A technique-related change must be checked; the lecturer uses this as an example of false apparent treatment success.","Starting above true RV measures a larger communicating volume and mislabels it as RV.","The dilution method assumes helium is neither generated nor consumed appreciably."],"explanation":"Identify the starting respiratory landmark before assigning a name to VL or interpreting a difference as disease change.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":5}],"skill":"Clinical reasoning","kind":"Clinical reasoning","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"},{"id":"CVP-P17:transcript-tracer-dilution","lectureIds":["CVP-P17"],"concept":"CVP-P17:pulm-helium","question":"Two sealed helium-dilution tests start with identical spirometer volume and helium fraction. Test B equilibrates with a larger communicating lung volume. Which result follows?","options":["B cannot equilibrate because helium must be metabolised first.","B has a lower final helium fraction but the same total helium amount.","B has a higher final helium fraction because more gas is present.","B must contain less total helium because its fraction is lower."],"answer":1,"reasons":["Helium is used because it is not appreciably metabolised during the test.","More accessible volume dilutes the same conserved tracer amount.","A larger added volume dilutes the tracer further rather than concentrating it.","Fraction is amount divided by volume; it can fall without any loss of tracer."],"explanation":"Conservation applies to amount; fraction changes as tracer spreads into a larger volume.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":4}],"skill":"Mechanism","kind":"Mechanism","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"}]},"CVP-P17:pulm-pathology":{"bodyAppend":["RV/TLC in the lecture is a simple pattern comparison, not a complete stand-alone diagnosis: a disproportionately high RV fraction supports air trapping, while a low TLC establishes restriction. Ageing can also raise RV/TLC, and restrictive patterns need not reduce every compartment in exactly the same proportion. Preserve the distinction between the lecturer’s illustrative table and clinical reference-limit interpretation."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":8},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":11},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":12}],"questions":[],"bodyReplace":["VC = TLC − RV. It can fall because maximum inflation is limited, because more gas remains after expiration, or both. The transcript groups influences into skeleton/chest wall, respiratory muscle, lung tissue and airway obstruction; the named disease list on its slide 25 is explicitly illustrative and outside required memorisation. Learn the mechanism families and the functional patterns on the following slide.","In the lecture's aging curve, lung volumes rise through growth and early adulthood. In later life RV rises while TLC changes little, so VC falls. Reduced VC is therefore not by itself a diagnosis of fibrosis or a unique disease process.","The source's obstructive example is emphysema: RV and FRC rise because of air trapping, and TLC may also rise. RV/TLC and FRC/TLC increase when trapped/resting volumes rise disproportionately. In a simple restrictive pattern such as fibrosis, TLC falls and volumes shrink approximately in parallel, so RV/TLC can remain normal. This is a teaching model: not every restrictive disorder reduces each volume in exactly the same proportion.","Clinical interpretation needs measurements, not labels alone: a reduced FVC with a preserved FEV1/FVC suggests restriction but a reduced TLC confirms a restrictive ventilatory defect. A reduced FVC during obstruction may reflect air trapping rather than concurrent restriction."]},"CVP-P17:pulm-conditions":{"bodyAppend":["The transcript reconfirms the boundary: understand that gas volume depends on the reporting conditions and that modern devices report corrected volumes, but the conversion factors and calculations are not required for this examination."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":9},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":10}],"questions":[]}},"newSections":[],"sourceNotes":["Transcript expansion is authored teaching with page-specific evidence; transcript wording is preserved in the source reader. Lecturer emphasis is distinct from explicit exam priority. Source medical slips are clarified in the relevant topic.","P17 is a structured transcriber summary rather than a verbatim speech transcript. Its emphasis and exclusion evidence is quoted exactly as printed; the red/green legend alone is not treated as proof of exam scope."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"CVP-P17:transcript-fresh-reservoir","prompt":"In the transcript example, 300 mL of fresh gas mixes with a 3-L end-expiratory reservoir. Enter the fresh-gas volume as a percentage of that starting reservoir (number only).","answers":["10","10.0"],"explanation":"0.3 L / 3 L × 100 = 10%. A relatively small added portion helps explain buffering; this is not the percentage of fresh gas in the final expanded mixture.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":6},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":7}]}],"matching":[]},"CVP-P20":{"sections":{"CVP-P20:pulm-flow":{"bodyAppend":["P20 corrects the earlier statement that dead-space volume never changes. In a tube of fixed length, reducing radius to 90% makes its volume 0.9² = 0.81 of the original while laminar resistance becomes 1/0.9⁴ ≈ 1.52 times the original. The strong resistance response therefore coexists with a smaller change in conducting gas volume. The relevant geometry is a comparison within a tube; combined resistance across a branching generation still requires parallel conductances.","Do not infer that lower barometric pressure automatically lowers gas dynamic viscosity in proportion to density. At fixed temperature in the dilute-gas range, dynamic viscosity is approximately pressure independent. The density advantage of helium-containing mixtures is most relevant to nonlaminar flow; the transcript’s claim that helium helps because both density and viscosity must fall is an oversimplification."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":2},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":3},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":4},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":5},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":12},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":13}],"questions":[{"id":"CVP-P20:transcript-volume-resistance-scaling","lectureIds":["CVP-P20"],"concept":"CVP-P20:pulm-flow","question":"In the fixed-length laminar-tube model, radius falls to 90% of its initial value. Which comparison is correct?","options":["Resistance falls to 66% because the tube contains less gas.","Volume and resistance both become 81% of baseline.","Volume becomes 81% of baseline while resistance rises to about 152%.","Volume becomes 90% of baseline while resistance stays unchanged."],"answer":2,"reasons":["1/1.52 is the flow fraction at fixed pressure, not the resistance fraction.","Volume scales with r², but resistance is inversely related to r⁴.","0.9² = 0.81; 1/0.9⁴ ≈ 1.52, so volume and resistance change differently.","Cylinder volume scales with area and therefore radius squared; resistance also changes."],"explanation":"A modest change in calibre can markedly change resistance without requiring a similarly large absolute dead-space volume change.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":5}],"skill":"Calculation","kind":"Calculation","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"}]},"CVP-P20:pulm-volume":{"bodyAppend":["For an airway, the passive distending pressure is airway lumen pressure minus the pressure outside its wall (transmural pressure). Raising lumen pressure or lowering outside pressure can increase distension without smooth-muscle relaxation. The transcript sometimes calls this transpleural pressure; for the local airway wall, transmural is the precise term.","The hand-drawn classroom example answers the “crowded alveoli” trap. Inspiration expands the thorax as well as the parenchyma: the diaphragm descends and the chest expands. Enlarging alveoli inside a permanently fixed outer box would be a different mechanical model. In the breathing lung, greater tissue stretch pulls the embedded airway outward, rather than simply squeezing it between two expanding alveoli.","The lecturer illustrates an obstructed patient breathing at a higher operating lung volume, where radial traction makes airway resistance lower. This describes a mechanical advantage, not a recommendation to treat all breathlessness with a sustained deep breath. Hyperinflation also has costs and emphysema does not regain its lost elastic attachments merely by increasing volume."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":5},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":6},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":14},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":15},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":16},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":17},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":18}],"questions":[{"id":"CVP-P20:transcript-transmural-distension","lectureIds":["CVP-P20"],"concept":"CVP-P20:pulm-volume","question":"An intrapulmonary airway has lumen pressure +2 and surrounding pressure −3 cm H₂O. Surrounding pressure falls to −5 while lumen pressure and muscle tone remain unchanged. What passive effect is expected?","options":["β₂ receptors must have been activated to change the diameter.","Distending pressure rises from 5 to 7 cm H₂O, tending to widen the airway.","Distending pressure falls from 5 to 3 cm H₂O, tending to narrow it.","The airway must constrict because all negative pressures are collapsing pressures."],"answer":1,"reasons":["The stem changes a passive pressure and explicitly holds muscle tone constant.","Pinside − Poutside changes from 2 − (−3) = 5 to 2 − (−5) = 7.","Subtracting a more negative outside pressure increases the difference.","A negative outside pressure relative to the lumen favours distension."],"explanation":"Local transmural pressure can change calibre without an active autonomic signal.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":5},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":6}],"skill":"Application","kind":"Application","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"},{"id":"CVP-P20:transcript-expanding-thorax","lectureIds":["CVP-P20"],"concept":"CVP-P20:pulm-volume","question":"A student argues that inspiration must squeeze an airway shut because its neighbouring alveoli enlarge. What assumption causes the error?","options":["The student assumes the thoracic space remains a fixed box while lung volume increases.","The student assumes inspiration expands the chest and lowers the diaphragm.","The student assumes airways have smooth muscle.","The student assumes lung tissue is attached to airway walls."],"answer":0,"reasons":["Inspiration enlarges the containing thorax; stretching attached tissue increases radial traction.","Expansion of the chest and descent of the diaphragm are the relevant corrections.","Airway smooth muscle exists, but its presence does not establish the fixed-box compression argument.","Parenchymal attachments are what transmit the outward tethering force to airways."],"explanation":"The lecturer’s extra classroom diagram contrasts a fixed container with the enlarging thorax during inspiration.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":15},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":16}],"skill":"Mechanism","kind":"Mechanism","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"}]},"CVP-P20:pulm-distribution":{"bodyAppend":["Read the two resistance plots differently. Resistance per generation asks how much resistance is attributable to one generation; cumulative resistance from mouth to generation N sums the central generations encountered up to N. Thus an 80% cumulative value at generation 7 does not mean generation 7 alone contributes 80%. Adding individual radii is only an informal classroom image: the correct parallel relation adds conductances, not radii."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":9},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":10},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":11}],"questions":[{"id":"CVP-P20:transcript-cumulative-graph","lectureIds":["CVP-P20"],"concept":"CVP-P20:pulm-distribution","question":"A cumulative resistance graph reaches 80% at airway generation 7. What does that mean?","options":["Generation 7 alone generates 80% of resistance.","The path from the mouth through the central generations to generation 7 contributes 80% in the source model.","Every airway after generation 7 contributes 80% individually.","The resistance of the whole lung must be 80 cm H₂O·s/L."],"answer":1,"reasons":["That would confuse a cumulative plot with a per-generation plot.","Cumulative values combine the contributions encountered up to the indicated generation.","The remaining distal generations together account for the smaller fraction in the model.","Percent of total is dimensionless and cannot be substituted for an absolute resistance."],"explanation":"Check whether the vertical axis is per generation or accumulated from the mouth before reading the number.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":9},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":10}],"skill":"Graph interpretation","kind":"Graph interpretation","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"}]}},"newSections":[],"sourceNotes":["Transcript expansion is authored teaching with page-specific evidence; transcript wording is preserved in the source reader. Lecturer emphasis is distinct from explicit exam priority. Source medical slips are clarified in the relevant topic."],"fillBlanks":[],"matching":[]},"CVP-P21":{"sections":{"CVP-P21:pulm-forced":{"bodyAppend":["Technique changes interpretation: beginning below maximal inspiration reduces the starting volume; a slow start depresses early forced outputs; premature termination can underestimate FVC. A low peak on one manoeuvre therefore cannot establish obstruction by itself. The transcript emphasises repeated effort rather than averaging poor trials. For clinical reporting, ATS/ERS standards require quality review and use the largest acceptable FEV1 and largest acceptable FVC, which need not come from the same manoeuvre.","FVC may be lower than slow vital capacity when forced expiration dynamically closes susceptible airways and traps gas that a slower manoeuvre can release. That discrepancy concerns the amount expelled, whereas the early peak and FEV1 also depend on how rapidly expiration is initiated."],"keyPointsAppend":[],"referencesAppend":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":21},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":22},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":24},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":25}],"questions":[{"id":"CVP-P21:transcript-poor-peak","lectureIds":["CVP-P21"],"concept":"CVP-P21:pulm-forced","question":"A person produces one low peak flow with a hesitant start and a higher peak on a well-performed repeat. Which conclusion is best?","options":["Later effort-independent flow means the early peak cannot depend on effort.","Average both peaks before deciding whether effort was adequate.","Review manoeuvre quality; a low peak from a poor start alone does not prove obstruction.","A low first peak proves irreversible emphysema."],"answer":2,"reasons":["Effort independence refers to much of the later descending limb, not the early peak.","An average incorporating poor performance is not a substitute for acceptability review.","Early peak flow depends on effort and the quality of initiation.","One technically poor manoeuvre cannot establish a disease diagnosis or irreversibility."],"explanation":"The transcript warns that cooperation can lower peak flow; clinical standards also require quality assessment before reporting the best acceptable results.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":24},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":25}],"skill":"Clinical reasoning","kind":"Clinical reasoning","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"},{"id":"CVP-P21:transcript-slow-vital-capacity","lectureIds":["CVP-P21"],"concept":"CVP-P21:pulm-forced","question":"An obstructed patient expels less volume during a forced manoeuvre than during a slow vital-capacity manoeuvre. Which mechanism best fits?","options":["The patient synthesises additional air during the slow manoeuvre.","The forced manoeuvre must change the definition of TLC.","The forceful manoeuvre can dynamically close susceptible airways and trap gas.","Slow expiration removes all residual volume."],"answer":2,"reasons":["The difference reflects emptying and airway mechanics, not gas production.","TLC remains the maximum lung volume; the difference concerns the amount expelled.","Force increases surrounding intrathoracic pressure and may limit emptying by airway compression.","RV remains after maximal expiration; slow VC does not empty the lungs completely."],"explanation":"The transcript notes a slightly smaller forced VC and its later dynamic-compression explanation supplies the mechanism.","references":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":22},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":23},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","page":29}],"skill":"Mechanism","kind":"Mechanism","sourceType":"Authored from supplied transcript · reviewed teaching practice"}]}},"newSections":[],"sourceNotes":["Transcript expansion is authored teaching with page-specific evidence; transcript wording is preserved in the source reader. Lecturer emphasis is distinct from explicit exam priority. Source medical slips are clarified in the relevant topic."],"fillBlanks":[],"matching":[]}},"priorityClaims":[{"lectureId":"CVP-A14","sectionId":"cvppa-a14-framework","kind":"lecturer","quote":"all these aortic arch is forming the arch of the aorta and other arteries because of the migration of the neural crest cells. This is very important.","page":5,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","teaching":"Neural crest contributes to development of the aortic-arch arterial region."},{"lectureId":"CVP-A14","sectionId":"cvppa-a14-pulmonary","kind":"exam","quote":"So, if I ask in the exam, ductus arteriosus coming from where? Ductus arteriosus is coming from the left 6thaortic arch, the distal portion.","page":10,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","teaching":"Distal left sixth arch → ductus arteriosus; proximal sixth → pulmonary artery."},{"lectureId":"CVP-A14","sectionId":"cvppa-a14-rings","kind":"exam","quote":"Sometimes distal portion of the 4thright aortic arch doesn’t go away, we can ask in the exam, so what will happen? There will be double arch of aorta.","page":10,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","teaching":"Persistence of the right arch pathway can produce double arch and a vascular ring."},{"lectureId":"CVP-A14","sectionId":"cvppa-a14-pulmonary","kind":"exam","quote":"We ask in the exam, if you are looking for the recurrent laryngeal nerve on the right side, which structure should you look for? Subclavian artery on the right.","page":12,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","teaching":"Right recurrent laryngeal nerve loops below the right subclavian; the left is retained at the ductal/ligament region."},{"lectureId":"CVP-A14","sectionId":"cvppa-a14-systemic","kind":"lecturer","quote":"(Doctor reads first part of the sentence, until ‘intersegmental artery’) just memorize it.","page":19,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","teaching":"Entire left subclavian derives from the left seventh intersegmental artery; this is the sentence immediately preceding the emphasis."},{"lectureId":"CVP-A16","sectionId":"cvppa-a16-segments","kind":"exam","quote":"You will be asked to recall what kind of \nthe segments we have in each specific lung.","page":20,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","teaching":"Know segment names and lung/lobe membership; exact locations on the stylized figure are excluded."},{"lectureId":"CVP-A17","sectionId":"cvppa-a17-bronchioles","kind":"exam","quote":"In the exam, if you are given a bronchiole picture and asked for the lining epithelium, you \ncannot write all of them. Look at the picture, see which type of epithelium it is, and write \nthat one.","page":11,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","teaching":"Name the observed bronchiolar epithelium rather than listing all possible linings."},{"lectureId":"CVP-A17","sectionId":"cvppa-a17-respiratory-airways","kind":"exam","quote":"Identifying the respiratory bronchiole is one of the very common questions in the OSPE.","page":16,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","teaching":"Recognize alveoli interrupting the bronchiolar wall, not merely alveoli elsewhere in the lung field."},{"lectureId":"CVP-A17","sectionId":"cvppa-a17-club-clearance","kind":"lecturer","quote":"These three functions are all important; you have to know them: surfactant-like \nsubstance, CC16 protein, and progenitor cell.","page":13,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","teaching":"Club cells provide bronchiolar surface protection, CC16 and epithelial renewal."},{"lectureId":"CVP-A17","sectionId":"cvppa-a17-club-clearance","kind":"lecturer","quote":"This is why the goblet cells have to stop before the cilia stop. This is very important \nstructurally.","page":14,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","teaching":"Cilia extend beyond the distal mucus-producing cells, preserving clearance of secretions."},{"lectureId":"CVP-A17","sectionId":"cvppa-a17-surfactant","kind":"lecturer","quote":"The most important component is the phospholipid (DPPC), which is responsible for \ndecreasing the surface tension. I want this information.","page":21,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","teaching":"DPPC is the emphasized surface-tension-lowering surfactant phospholipid."},{"lectureId":"CVP-A17","sectionId":"cvppa-a17-pathology","kind":"exam","quote":"You will mostly see them in the TBL and \nin the final exam, in the cases. I do not want the pathology at all, but when you see \nbronchial asthma, understand that I am talking mostly about the bronchioles, and with \nemphysema I am talking about the alveoli.","page":30,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","teaching":"Clinical-case recognition: asthma → bronchiolar wall/mucus; emphysema → destroyed alveolar septa; limit depth to the stated structural mechanisms."},{"lectureId":"CVP-A17","sectionId":"cvppa-a17-pathology","kind":"exam","quote":"You should at least know these three, and also the clinical items from last time, because \nthey are mostly in the TBL and the final.","page":32,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","teaching":"Know the three named clinical examples (asthma, emphysema and cystic fibrosis) at the taught structure–function level; the quotation also refers to prior clinical items, without adding unspecified pathology detail."},{"lectureId":"CVP-P17","sectionId":"CVP-P17:pulm-pathology","kind":"lecturer","quote":"This is what you have to know and never forget: from a functional point of view respiratory diseases are divided into 2 categories, obstructive and restrictive","page":12,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","teaching":"Know the functional obstructive versus restrictive comparison, including the illustrative changes in RV, TLC and RV/TLC; do not use the simplified ratio as a stand-alone diagnosis."},{"lectureId":"CVP-P21","sectionId":"CVP-P21:pulm-forced","kind":"lecturer","quote":"These two factors are very important: that he maximally exhales and that he exhales as fast as he can.","page":22,"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p20","teaching":"A forced manoeuvre requires both rapid effort and a complete expiration; check performance before interpreting a low result."}],"exclusions":[{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","page":15,"quote":"(Doctor reads 2nd point) forget it.","scope":"Specific second displayed bullet: remaining internal carotid from cranial dorsal aorta. Remove active distal-ICA origin question; retain third-arch carotid association.","affectedSections":["cvppa-a14-carotids"],"affectedQuestionIds":["cvppa-a14-carotids-distal-ica"]},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","page":18,"quote":"maxillary artery, hyoid, this is derivative of the aortic arch, not important for you, just disappearing that’s all.","scope":"Explicitly deprioritized first/second-arch named remnants: remove their active teaching, questions and summary; retain source reader.","affectedSections":["cvppa-a14-carotids"],"affectedQuestionIds":["cvppa-a14-carotids-remnants","cvppa-a14-carotids-second"]},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","page":20,"quote":"Forget about abnormal subclavian.","scope":"Exclude aberrant/retroesophageal right subclavian from active mixed rings section, its summary and source diagram key cvp-pulmonary-a14:17. Retain double arch.","affectedSections":["cvppa-a14-rings"],"affectedQuestionIds":["cvppa-a14-rings-retroesophageal"]},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a14","page":25,"quote":"I'm not going to ask you any questions on that, because I'm not embarking on the physiological part.","scope":"Embryonic cardiac parameter table (heart volume, stroke volume, output values) is explicitly not an assessment target. No existing anatomy questions found.","affectedSections":["cvppa-a14-framework"],"affectedQuestionIds":[]},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":20,"quote":"You will never be asked to identify the \nlocation of the segments on the pictures.","scope":"Exclude segment-position identification from the stylized segment diagrams; retain named segments and lobe/lung membership as explicitly examinable.","affectedSections":["cvppa-a16-segments"],"affectedQuestionIds":[]},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a16","page":22,"quote":"You will never be given \noptions to choose between these two.","scope":"Do not test choosing nine versus ten left bronchopulmonary segments. The existing cross-source-count MCQ is best removed from active practice because it makes numerical disagreement the question focus.","affectedSections":["cvppa-a16-segments"],"affectedQuestionIds":["cvppa-a16-segments-counts"]},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":4,"quote":"We will never ask about the number of segments in histology. Stick to the anatomy \nnumbers.","scope":"Do not make segment counts a histology assessment target. Retain lobe→segment→lobule hierarchy and source disagreement as a note, not numerical recall.","affectedSections":["cvppa-a17-hierarchy"],"affectedQuestionIds":[]},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-a17","page":30,"quote":"I do not want the pathology at all","scope":"Limit disease material to the structural recognition explicitly requested in the same sentence, not full pathology/treatment expansion.","affectedSections":["cvppa-a17-pathology"],"affectedQuestionIds":[]},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":10,"quote":"He said you can forget these, do not memorise them or learn them from the slides (not tested)","scope":"The conversion factors/calculations on slide 24 (and their slide-23 setup); retain names/meaning of ATPS, BTPS and STPD. Confirms the earlier slide-source exclusion cvp-pulm-p16-17 page23. Do not exclude helium dilution or ordinary volume arithmetic.","affectedSections":["CVP-P17:pulm-conditions","CVP-P17:pulm-helium"],"affectedQuestionIds":[]},{"source":"cvp-pulm-t-p17","page":11,"quote":"Do not memorise these diseases and you will not be asked about them. You learn them in pathology later. They are just examples","scope":"Named disease list illustrating reduced vital capacity on slide25 only. Retain mechanism families and the immediately following obstructive/restrictive functional comparison, whose lecturer emphasis is explicit. Do not suppress independently taught asthma/emphysema mechanisms elsewhere.","affectedSections":["CVP-P17:pulm-pathology"],"affectedQuestionIds":["CVP-P17:pulm-pathology-q3"]}],"diagrams":{},"removeDiagramKeys":["cvp-pulmonary-a14:17"]};
